01b122f9-34a2-480e-b361-fd29f719cfdb	5f4100e9-4b32-470a-a5e8-b25012671850	\N	14	OrgName	\N	{"public_sector":["Federal Government (US)","State Government (US)","Local Government (US)","International Governments","Educational Institutions (Public)","Public Healthcare Systems","Public Utilitie	501-1,000	["United States Only"]	 pain point	business outcomes	Legal & Compliance	$100K - $250K	3-6 months	High	vendor name	solution name	vendor's product features	["Document Management"]	["Cloud (Azure)"]	Defined	Developing	No	Yes	["Data / Analytics"]	Internal	["HIPAA"]	Moderate	High (strategic or financial)	["Customers"]	Internal security review	Adequate	["SOC 2 Type II"]	No	Partial / phased rollback	Yes, formal plan	\N	No	No	No	No risks defined in the system yet.	No risk domains available.	Intent: Unintentional, Timing: Pre-deployment (contextual multipliers can be extended when rules are defined).	[RISK-1] Adaptation of Mitigations for Unintended Model Use (Risk Management) – Adapt mitigations in recognition of risks from unintended model use or use in unexpected contexts, such as a model that is modified to remove safeguards after open- sourcing or a model that is combined with another model for unanticipated purposes. For example, at a given risk threshold, information security control mitigations might be put in place before even internal use of a model within a frontier AI organisation. In general, the use of caution may be helpful, given current limitations in information security controls and the prediction of models’ emergent abilities.\n\n[RISK-1] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-10] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-10] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-10] Determine Deployment Readiness (Safety Decision Frameworks) – A determination ismade as to whetherthe AI system achievesits intended purposesand stated objectivesand whether itsdevelopment ordeployment shouldproceed.\n\n[RISK-10] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-10] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-100] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-100] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1000] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1001] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1001] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1002] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1003] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1004] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1004] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1005] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1005] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1005] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-1012] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1005] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1005] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-1006] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-1006] Digital Content Transparency (Content Safety Controls) – Use digital content transparency solutions to enable the documentation of each instance where content is generated, modified, or shared to provide a tamper-proof history of the content, promote transparency, and enable traceability. Robust version control systems can also be applied to track changes across the AI lifecycle over time.\n\n[RISK-1006] Training Data Harm Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess existence or levels of harmful bias, intellectual property infringement, data privacy violations, obscenity, extremism, violence, or CBRN information in system training data.\n\n[RISK-1006] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-1006] Application of Infrastructure Principles (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply good infrastructure principles to every part of this process from design to decommissioning. This is important as threats can occur at different stages of an AI project’s life cycle.\n\n[RISK-1008] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1008] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1008] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-1008] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1008] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-1009] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1009] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1009] Restricted usage terms for open-source models (Access Management) – Developers of open-weights and open-source AI models can vet and restrict the users of their AI systems by requiring them to sign a Terms of Service agreement before getting access to the model weights. Such agreements can include limitations to the usage, modification, and proliferation of the AI model [88].Such agreements have the advantage that users only need to be vetted once before getting model access, but are often limited in practice in preventing unauthorised use or distribution.\n\n[RISK-1009] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-1012] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-145] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-1009] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-101] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-101] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1010] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1010] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1010] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1010] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1010] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-1011] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1011] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1011] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1011] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1011] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-721] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-1012] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1012] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-1012] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1013] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-1013] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1013] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1013] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1013] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-1014] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1014] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1014] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1014] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1014] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-1015] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1015] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-1015] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1015] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1015] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1016] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1016] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1016] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-1016] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-1016] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-1017] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1017] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1018] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-1018] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1018] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-1019] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-1019] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1019] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-1019] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1019] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-102] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-102] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1020] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1020] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1020] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1020] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1020] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-1021] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1028] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1042] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1021] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1021] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-1021] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1021] Advanced information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implementing advanced cybersecurity measures and insider threat safeguards to protect proprietary and unreleased model weights.\n\n[RISK-1022] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1022] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1023] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1023] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-1024] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-1024] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1024] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1024] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-1024] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1025] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1025] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1027] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1028] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-1028] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1028] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-1028] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-103] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-103] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-103] Monitoring Inputs to AI Systems for Audit Development (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Monitor and log inputs to AI systems to enable audit, investigation and remediation in the case of compromise. Some attacks against AI systems rely on repeated querying. Proper logging will help you audit your system and identify any anomalous inputs.\n\n[RISK-103] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-103] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-1030] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1030] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1030] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-1030] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1033] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-1033] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-1030] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1031] AI Output Databases (Content Safety Controls) – Explore databases of content generated or manipulated by a model to identify AI- generated content. These databases could be queried by third parties, including auditors and regulators, facilitating identification of potentially AI-generated content. Such databases could include only a subset of generated content, which is flagged as potentially important. To ensure user privacy, privacy-preserving techniques could be explored in conjunction with such databases, such as hashing technologies. This allows for the identification of AI-generated content without the need to store the actual content, thereby respecting user privacy. Additionally, common standards between different databases from various frontier AI organisations could facilitate a unified search, allowing for the identification of AI-generated content across all frontier AI organisations simultaneously.\n\n[RISK-1031] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1031] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1031] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1031] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-1032] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1032] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1033] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1033] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1033] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-1034] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1034] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1034] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-1034] Tamper-resistant safeguards for open-weight models (Model Safety Engineering) – Training and implementing safeguards can improve the robustness of open- weight models against modifications from fine-tuning or other methods to change the learned weights of the models, especially those aimed at removing safety re- strictions. These safeguards can be resilient even after extensive fine-tuning, ensuring that the model retains its protective measures [199].\n\n[RISK-1034] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-1035] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1035] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1036] Provenance Privacy Documentation (Data Governance) – Document how content provenance data is tracked and how that data interacts with privacy and security. Consider: Anonymizing data to protect the privacy of human subjects; Leveraging privacy output filters; Removing any personally identifiable information (PII) to prevent potential harm or misuse.\n\n[RISK-1036] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1036] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1036] Upstream Dependency Documentation (System Documentation) – Identify and document how the system relies on upstream data sources, including for content provenance, and if it serves as an upstream dependency for other systems.\n\n[RISK-1036] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-1037] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1037] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1037] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-1041] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1037] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-1037] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1038] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1038] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1039] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1039] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-104] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-104] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-104] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-104] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-104] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-1040] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1040] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1041] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-1041] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1041] Regular Assessment of Supply Chain Security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Regularly assess the security of their supply chains and ensure suppliers adhere to the same standards their own organisation applies. Ensuring data, software components and hardware are obtained from trusted sources will help mitigate supply chain risk.\n\n[RISK-1041] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1042] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1042] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1042] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-1042] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1043] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1043] Pre-deployment risk disclosure (Risk Disclosure) – Does your organization share the results of its pre-deployment risk assessments with the appropriate government(s) before deploying a new model? Does this reporting include details on internal safety evaluations and any safety evaluations completed by independent third parties? Is the government provided with a justification for why the firm deems the system safe enough to deploy and is willing to accept the remaining risks? Please check all that apply.\n\n[RISK-1043] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1044] AI identification (Content Safety Controls) – AI identifiers can be used to indicate that an AI is involved in a process or an interaction [40].For AI systems that interact directly with users, a visible output may be used, e.g., a displayed text message saying “I am an AI language model”, accompanied with the appropriate warnings and caveats relevant to the user, [40]. Whereas, for AI systems that interact with other systems or applications, other forms of watermark or unique identifiers can be used. In either situation, agent cards can serve as an identifier, where further details about the underlying AI system, the specific instance of the AI agent, and other information relevant to the development of the agent, can be included.\n\n[RISK-1044] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1044] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1044] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-1044] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1078] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1045] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1045] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1045] Protection against espionage (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should take adequate measures to tackle the risk of state-sponsored or industrial espionage.*\n\n[RISK-1045] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-1045] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-1046] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1046] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1047] Risk Thresholds (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Quantitative or qualitative limits that distinguish acceptable from unacceptable risks and trigger specific risk management actions when exceeded.Risk thresholds for general- purpose AI are being determined by a combination of assessments of capabilities, impact, compute, reach, and other factors.\n\n[RISK-1047] Responsible Release and Deployment Strategies (Staged Deployment) – There is a spectrum of release and deployment strategies for AI including staged releases, cloud- based or API access, deployment safety controls, and acceptable use policies.There are some emerging industry practices that focus on release and deployment strategies for general- purpose AI.\n\n[RISK-1047] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1047] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1047] Feedback Exercise Metrics (Testing & Auditing) – Develop a suite of metrics to evaluate structured public feedback exercises informed by representative AI Actors.\n\n[RISK-1048] Publish assessment of the risk of catastrophic harms from the model (Risk Disclosure)\n\n[RISK-1048] Provenance Privacy Documentation (Data Governance) – Document how content provenance data is tracked and how that data interacts with privacy and security. Consider: Anonymizing data to protect the privacy of human subjects; Leveraging privacy output filters; Removing any personally identifiable information (PII) to prevent potential harm or misuse.\n\n[RISK-1049] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1049] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-1049] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-1049] Third-Party Risk (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Policies and proceduresare in place that addressAI risks associated withthird-party entities,including risks ofinfringement of athird-party’s intellectualproperty or otherrights.\n\n[RISK-1049] Monitor System in Production (Post-deployment Monitoring) – The functionalityand behavior of theAI system and itscomponents – asidentified in the Mapfunction – aremonitored when inproduction.\n\n[RISK-105] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-105] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1050] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1050] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1051] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1051] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1054] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1054] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1054] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-1054] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1054] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1055] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1055] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1055] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-1055] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-1055] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1056] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1056] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-1056] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-1056] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-1056] Application of Infrastructure Principles (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply good infrastructure principles to every part of this process from design to decommissioning. This is important as threats can occur at different stages of an AI project’s life cycle.\n\n[RISK-1057] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-1057] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1057] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-1057] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-1057] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-1058] Reversible and Small-Scale Deployment (Staged Deployment) – Deploy models in small-scale or reversible ways before deploying models in large-scale or irreversible ways. This makes it possible for frontier AI organisations to notice and mitigate harm before the harm becomes too large or unavoidable.\n\n[RISK-1058] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1058] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1058] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-1058] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1059] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1059] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-1059] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-1059] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1093] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-1059] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-106] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-106] Dangerous capability evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – This indicator reports on pre-deployment capability evaluations related to catastrophic risks. Model evaluations for other risks are not included here, as the empirical tests covered in the ‘Current Harms’ section provide a superior metric. Information includes evaluated risk domains, available information regarding model versions & task-specific fine-tuning, and relevant sources. We note that quality of evaluations may differ.\n\n[RISK-106] Detect & Share Incidents (Testing & Auditing) – Organizational practicesare in place to enableAI testing, identificationof incidents, andinformation sharing.\n\n[RISK-106] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-106] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-1060] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1060] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1060] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1060] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-1060] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1061] Sharing of Limited Information (Risk Disclosure) – Share more limited information with other AI organisations in order to facilitate learning and the development of best practices. This could include best practices and lessons learned in the development of risk assessment and mitigation measures and risk governance processes, some details about safety and security incidents (to enable increased awareness while protecting intellectual property), and highlights and lessons learned from risk assessments and capability evaluation. In general, it will be easier for organisations to share model-agnostic information with one another than model-specific information, which may be more commercially sensitive. It will also be important to consider that there is no privileged access to information that may give a competitive advantage to some firms.\n\n[RISK-1061] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1061] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1062] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1062] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1062] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-1062] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-1062] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1063] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1063] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-1063] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-1063] Robust Content Filters (Model Safety Engineering) – Invest in making content filters robust to “jailbreaking” attempts. Work to ensure filters are robust to jailbreaking attempts, for instance by including examples of jailbreaking attempts in the datasets used to develop filters.\n\n[RISK-1063] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-1064] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1064] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1065] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1065] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1066] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1066] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1066] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1081] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1066] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1066] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-1067] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1067] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-1067] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1067] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-1067] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-1068] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1068] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1068] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-1068] Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the robustness of models to different classes of adversarial attack (such as poisoning, model inversion and model stealing), based on priorities derived from threat modelling. This could involve a combination of benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-1068] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1069] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-1069] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1069] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-1069] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-1069] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-107] Bias and discrimination Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to address bias and discrimination in AI systems\n\n[RISK-107] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-107] Adversarial Testing Plans (Testing & Auditing) – Implement plans for GAI systems to undergo regular adversarial testing to identify vulnerabilities and potential manipulation or misuse.\n\n[RISK-107] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-107] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-1070] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1070] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1070] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1070] Public Risk Threshold for Deployment (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Has your organization publicly specified an evaluations-based risk or capabilities threshold that would cause the firm not to deploy a model and to pause further development until it can implement adequate risk mitigation? If yes, please provide a URL to the website specifying these commitments.\n\n[RISK-1070] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1071] Risk Threshold Engagement with Relevant External Stakeholders (Risk Management) – Engage with relevant external stakeholders when developing risk thresholds. Risk thresholds often concern externalities frontier AI organisations place on society, including both the potentially significant benefits of AI advancement and negative effects that might disproportionately affect specific stakeholder groups. As such, their risk thresholds may be made public to allow for external scrutiny, with thresholds set in consultation with relevant external stakeholders including relevant government authorities.\n\n[RISK-1071] Information Security (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Establish and regularly review specific criteria that warrants the deactivation ofGAI systems in accordance with set risk tolerances and appetites.\n\n[RISK-1071] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1071] Executive Risk Accountability (Board Structure & Oversight) – Executive leadershipof the organizationtakes responsibility fordecisions about risksassociated with AI sys-tem development anddeployment.\n\n[RISK-1071] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-1072] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1074] Differential privacy (Data Governance) – Differential privacy techniques [8] can be used to protect users’ privacy by ensuring that sensitive information is not leaked from a training dataset, even after thorough statistical analysis. With differential privacy, noise is added to the dataset or the model’s output in such a way that one cannot deduce the presence or absence of a particular data point within the dataset. This provides individuals with plausible deniability and prevents their information from being exposed.\n\n[RISK-1074] Mechanisms to Disclose Vulnerability Reports to Third Parties (Risk Disclosure) – Put in place mechanisms to disclose information about vulnerabilities to relevant government authorities, law enforcement, and other affected organisations. This may be particularly relevant for vulnerabilities where public disclosure might increase the risk of harm.\n\n[RISK-1074] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-1075] Digital Content Transparency (Content Safety Controls) – Use digital content transparency solutions to enable the documentation of each instance where content is generated, modified, or shared to provide a tamper-proof history of the content, promote transparency, and enable traceability. Robust version control systems can also be applied to track changes across the AI lifecycle over time.\n\n[RISK-1075] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1075] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1075] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-1075] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-1076] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-1076] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1076] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1077] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1077] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1077] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-1077] Public Risk Threshold for Deployment (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Has your organization publicly specified an evaluations-based risk or capabilities threshold that would cause the firm not to deploy a model and to pause further development until it can implement adequate risk mitigation? If yes, please provide a URL to the website specifying these commitments.\n\n[RISK-1077] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1078] Origin Tracing Methods (Content Safety Controls) – Employ methods to trace the origin and modifications of digital content.\n\n[RISK-1078] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1078] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-1078] Privacy Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to address the privacy risks associated with AI systems\n\n[RISK-1079] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1079] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1079] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-1079] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-1079] Privacy Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to address the privacy risks associated with AI systems\n\n[RISK-108] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-108] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-108] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-108] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-108] Privacy Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to address the privacy risks associated with AI systems\n\n[RISK-1080] Digital Content Transparency (Content Safety Controls) – Use digital content transparency solutions to enable the documentation of each instance where content is generated, modified, or shared to provide a tamper-proof history of the content, promote transparency, and enable traceability. Robust version control systems can also be applied to track changes across the AI lifecycle over time.\n\n[RISK-1080] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1080] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1080] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-1080] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1081] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1081] Post-deployment ongoing monitoring for unexpected use cases (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Ongoing monitoring of AI systems can uncover emergent or newly identified capabilities and limitations, in particular when new use cases are found, or in a large-scale deployment with a diverse population of users. These new capa- bilities or limitations can inform ongoing risk analysis. New use-cases can be discovered via monitoring publications, online forums, or APIs [131].For example, a LLM might have unexpectedly high competence at giving con- vincing medical advice, despite not being directly developed for that purpose, nor verified for accuracy. In that case, the potential risks of this newly found competence can be assessed.\n\n[RISK-1081] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-1081] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-1082] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1082] Independent Evaluation with Subject Matter Expertise (Testing & Auditing) – Ensure that evaluators are independent and have sufficient AI and subject matter expertise across a wide range of relevant subjects and backgrounds. External evaluators’ relationships with frontier AI organisations could be structured to minimise conflicts of interest and encourage independence of judgement as far as practically possible. As well as expertise in AI, there are many other areas of subject matter expertise that will be needed to evaluate an AI system’s features. For instance, experts on topics as wide as fairness, psychological harm, and catastrophic risk will be needed.\n\n[RISK-1082] Robust Content Filters (Model Safety Engineering) – Invest in making content filters robust to “jailbreaking” attempts. Work to ensure filters are robust to jailbreaking attempts, for instance by including examples of jailbreaking attempts in the datasets used to develop filters.\n\n[RISK-1082] Model Evaluation (Testing & Auditing) – Processes to assess and measure an AI system's performance on a particular task.There are countless AI evaluations to assess different capabilities and risks, including for security.\n\n[RISK-1082] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-1083] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1083] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1084] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1084] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-1084] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1084] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1088] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-1084] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1085] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1085] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1086] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1086] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-1086] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-1086] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-1086] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1087] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1088] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-1088] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1088] Harmful Data Removal from Training Sets (Data Governance) – When training large models, does your organization remove data that contains information related to dangerous capabilities or harmful outcomes from the training set? If yes, please select the categories of data that are removed.Detailed information about the development, acquisition, or dispersion of CBRN weaponsInstructional content for conducting cyberattacksHateful or discriminatory contentAdvice or encouragement for self-harmGraphic violent contentGraphic sexual contentPersonally Identifiable Information (PII)Detailed information about bomb-making or other terrorism-enabling tactics\n\n[RISK-1088] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-1089] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1089] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-109] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-109] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-109] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-109] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-109] Benchmark dataset auditing (Testing & Auditing) – Auditing benchmark datasets allows for verification of the utility and limitations of the datasets [158]. This allows the provider to more accurately measure AI model capabilities and safety. Auditing includes the evaluation of such datasets by independent third-party organizations and the release of benchmark dataset metadata to the auditors.\n\n[RISK-1090] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1090] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1090] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1090] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-1090] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-1091] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1091] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1092] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1093] Documentation availability (System Documentation) – All of the above documentation can be made available to the public (redacted) and AI governing authorities (unredacted).\n\n[RISK-1093] Calibrated confidence measures for model pre- dictions (System Documentation) – Incorporating calibrated confidence measures alongside a model’s predictions and standard performance metrics, such as accuracy, can help users identify in- stances of overconfidence in incorrect predictions or underconfidence in correct ones [85]. These additional measures can provide users with more information to better interpret the model’s decisions and assess whether its predictions can be trusted.\n\n[RISK-1093] Development of Protocols for Disclosure of Model Vulnerability Information (Risk Disclosure) – Developing – and publicly describe – protocols for deciding how to share model vulnerability information. These protocols may, for example, outline conditions under which information is to be shared with different actors depending on the type of harm or vulnerability identified.\n\n[RISK-1093] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1094] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1094] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1094] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1094] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1094] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1095] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1095] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1096] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1096] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-1096] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-1096] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1096] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1097] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-11] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-1097] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1097] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1097] Pre-training and Fine-tuning of Models (Testing & Auditing) – During pre-training and fine-tuning, evaluate the model to detect signs of undesirable properties and identify inaccuracies in pretraining predictions. These evaluations could be undertaken at various pre-specified checkpoints, and could inform decisions about whether to pause or adjust the training process.\n\n[RISK-1097] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-1098] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1098] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-1098] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-1098] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1098] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1099] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1099] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1099] Evaluation of Predecessor Models (Testing & Auditing) – Before a frontier model is trained, evaluate predecessor or analogous models to understand how relevant properties (e.g. dangerous capabilities) scale with the overall size of the model. These preliminary evaluations can inform risk assessments.\n\n[RISK-1099] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-1099] Model Evaluation (Testing & Auditing) – Processes to assess and measure an AI system's performance on a particular task.There are countless AI evaluations to assess different capabilities and risks, including for security.\n\n[RISK-11] Staged release of model weights (Staged Deployment) – When a model is developed by a provider for use in a certain AI system, it may also be useful to release the model itself more widely. Such developers can follow a staged release approach, in which they first grant access to the model via an API to trusted partners or the public, in order to scope the models’ capabilities and detect harmful or dangerous features [195].After a period of an initial closed release and potentially further safety-training, the developers of the AI model can then release the weights, if they are confident that the AI model poses minimal systemic risk.\n\n[RISK-11] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-11] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-11] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-110] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-110] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-110] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-110] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-110] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-1100] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1100] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1101] Calibrated confidence measures for model pre- dictions (System Documentation) – Incorporating calibrated confidence measures alongside a model’s predictions and standard performance metrics, such as accuracy, can help users identify in- stances of overconfidence in incorrect predictions or underconfidence in correct ones [85]. These additional measures can provide users with more information to better interpret the model’s decisions and assess whether its predictions can be trusted.\n\n[RISK-1101] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1101] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1101] Adjucation around Publicly Sharing Risks (Risk Disclosure) – Before sharing information, consider the risks of sharing this information and judge whether it is inappropriate to share certain pieces of information. In particular, it is important to consider potential harms from publicly sharing information about dangerous capabilities and methods for eliciting them, as this information could motivate or help other actors to acquire these capabilities. It is also important to consider potential harms from publicly sharing detailed information about how models were produced, as this may lower barriers to producing similar models. If the models have or could be modified to possess dangerous capabilities (e.g. biological capabilities or surveillance capabilities), then facilitating widespread distribution of the model in this way may be harmful. The National Protective Security Authority (NPSA) guidance on a security-minded approach to information management may prove helpful for sharing information appropriately.\n\n[RISK-1101] Differential privacy (Data Governance) – Differential privacy techniques [8] can be used to protect users’ privacy by ensuring that sensitive information is not leaked from a training dataset, even after thorough statistical analysis. With differential privacy, noise is added to the dataset or the model’s output in such a way that one cannot deduce the presence or absence of a particular data point within the dataset. This provides individuals with plausible deniability and prevents their information from being exposed.\n\n[RISK-1103] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1103] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1103] Implement AI alignment validation system (Model Alignment) – Establish processes for validating and maintaining AI system alignment with human values and goals, including testing for goal preservation, monitoring for objective drift, and validation of decision-making processes against ethical standards. Includes specific attention to detecting and preventing potentially misaligned behaviors, emergent goals, or deceptive actions. Covers using interpretability techniques to measure and assure alignment with intended goals.\n\n[RISK-12] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1103] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1103] Harmlessness training (Model Alignment) – State-of-the-art reinforcement learning and fine-tuning techniques, such as Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) or Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), to ensure models do not engage in unsafe behavior.\n\n[RISK-1104] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1104] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1105] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1105] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1105] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1105] Application of Infrastructure Principles (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply good infrastructure principles to every part of this process from design to decommissioning. This is important as threats can occur at different stages of an AI project’s life cycle.\n\n[RISK-1105] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-1106] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1107] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1107] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1109] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-1109] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1109] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-1109] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1109] Benchmark dataset auditing (Testing & Auditing) – Auditing benchmark datasets allows for verification of the utility and limitations of the datasets [158]. This allows the provider to more accurately measure AI model capabilities and safety. Auditing includes the evaluation of such datasets by independent third-party organizations and the release of benchmark dataset metadata to the auditors.\n\n[RISK-1239] Configuration Outcome Monitoring (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Implement systems to continually monitor and track the outcomes of human-GAI configurations for future refinement and improvements.\n\n[RISK-111] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-111] Digital Content Transparency (Content Safety Controls) – Use digital content transparency solutions to enable the documentation of each instance where content is generated, modified, or shared to provide a tamper-proof history of the content, promote transparency, and enable traceability. Robust version control systems can also be applied to track changes across the AI lifecycle over time.\n\n[RISK-111] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-111] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-111] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-1110] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1110] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1110] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-1110] Dashboard of model properties (Post-deployment Monitoring) – A dashboard [41] displays all the relevant information about the model’s internal state and the model’s physical properties to the user. It is used to ensure that the user is informed about factors that influence the behavior of the model and to ensure that the user maintains control over the model. Allowing only the user to access the dashboard can aid in information asymmetry between the user and model, thus supporting the user oversight over the model.Examples of the model’s internal state include its representations of the world, representation of users, and the strategies it is currently pursuing; while examples of the model’s physical properties include the model’s compute and energy consumption, physical storage occupied, and physical networks it is connected to.\n\n[RISK-1110] Monitoring protocols for po- tentially adversarial GPAI systems (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Similar to cybersecurity protocols, AI providers can prepare in advance for po- tential adversarial action from AI systems by developing and stress-testing protocols for potential adversarial action [179]. Such adversarial action may range from hiding code exploits, to strategic deception or loss of control related be- haviors.AI systems may be adversarial due to malicious actors (e.g., data poisoning), or due to unintended issues of control (lack of alignment with human intent). Alongside improving the system alignment, external monitoring and restrictions may be deployed in advance to “catch” these adversarial model malfunctions before harm is done.Like with cybersecurity red teaming, any protocol which assumes adversarial action can be stress tested in advance. For example, this can be done by pro- jecting the plausible capabilities and affordances of the adversarial actor (e.g., in the case of a tech company - a hacker, or a disgruntled employee). From this process, measures that better anticipate possible adversarial action can be deployed, and catch unsuccessful attempts at adversarial behavior.If such unsuccessful attempts are caught, they can be reported in incident re- ports, and serve as an input to further risk analysis. Depending on their severity, it may be advisable to recall the system if deployed. Examples of these protocols include [84]:• Flagging of suspicious frontier AI system code generation for human over- sight, with the help of better proven (“trusted”) AI systems or automated keyword detection.• Using a better proven (“trusted”) AI system to edit the code produced by a frontier AI system if suspicious.• Monitoring of AI system with another instance of this same system, with filters to prevent collusion.• Restriction of access of the AI system to private information.\n\n[RISK-1113] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-1114] Development of Protocols for Disclosure of Model Vulnerability Information (Risk Disclosure) – Developing – and publicly describe – protocols for deciding how to share model vulnerability information. These protocols may, for example, outline conditions under which information is to be shared with different actors depending on the type of harm or vulnerability identified.\n\n[RISK-1114] Provenance Privacy Documentation (Data Governance) – Document how content provenance data is tracked and how that data interacts with privacy and security. Consider: Anonymizing data to protect the privacy of human subjects; Leveraging privacy output filters; Removing any personally identifiable information (PII) to prevent potential harm or misuse.\n\n[RISK-1114] Document compute usage (System Documentation) – Given that computing power is key to frontier AI development (Sastry et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document their compute resources including details such as the usage, providers, and the location of compute clusters.\n\n[RISK-1115] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-1115] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1115] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1115] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1115] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-1116] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1116] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1117] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1117] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-1118] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1118] Information Sharing with Downstream Users (System Documentation) – Share specific information with downstream users of the model to enable more effective risk mitigation across the AI supply chain and build consumer confidence. This could include uses of the model that are against their terms of service, and could be provided to users through user terms or published information about the model.\n\n[RISK-1118] Mechanisms to Disclose Vulnerability Reports to Third Parties (Risk Disclosure) – Put in place mechanisms to disclose information about vulnerabilities to relevant government authorities, law enforcement, and other affected organisations. This may be particularly relevant for vulnerabilities where public disclosure might increase the risk of harm.\n\n[RISK-112] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-112] Deepfake creation policy (Content Safety Controls) – Can individuals use your firm’s AI systems to create deepfakes (i.e., synthetic audio or visual representations) of a specific individual?\n\n[RISK-407] Business Use (System Documentation) – The business value orcontext of businessuse has been clearlydefined or – in the caseof assessing existing AIsystems – re-evaluated.\n\n[RISK-112] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-112] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-112] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-1120] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1120] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1120] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-1120] Diverse Monitoring Techniques (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Employ diverse monitoring techniques to balance comprehensiveness, scalability, and privacy. A strong monitoring setup will generally combine automated and human review, in recognition that automated reviews may miss intricate issues, while human reviews are not always scalable and can pose privacy issues.\n\n[RISK-1120] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-1121] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1121] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1122] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1122] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1123] Third-Party Risk (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Policies and proceduresare in place that addressAI risks associated withthird-party entities,including risks ofinfringement of athird-party’s intellectualproperty or otherrights.\n\n[RISK-1123] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1123] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1123] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-1123] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-1124] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1124] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1125] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1125] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1126] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1126] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1127] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1127] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1128] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1128] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1129] Provenance Privacy Documentation (Data Governance) – Document how content provenance data is tracked and how that data interacts with privacy and security. Consider: Anonymizing data to protect the privacy of human subjects; Leveraging privacy output filters; Removing any personally identifiable information (PII) to prevent potential harm or misuse.\n\n[RISK-1129] Training Data Diligence (Data Governance) – Conduct appropriate diligence on training data use to assess intellectual property, and privacy, risks, including to examine whether use of proprietary or sensitive training data is consistent with applicable laws.\n\n[RISK-1129] Differential privacy (Data Governance) – Differential privacy techniques [8] can be used to protect users’ privacy by ensuring that sensitive information is not leaked from a training dataset, even after thorough statistical analysis. With differential privacy, noise is added to the dataset or the model’s output in such a way that one cannot deduce the presence or absence of a particular data point within the dataset. This provides individuals with plausible deniability and prevents their information from being exposed.\n\n[RISK-113] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-113] External Expertise for Input Data Audits (Data Governance) – Draw on external expertise in conducting input data audits. For example, biosecurity experts could be consulted to identify information relevant to biological weapons manufacturing, which may not be readily obvious to non-experts.\n\n[RISK-1130] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-1130] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-1130] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1131] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-1131] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1131] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1131] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1131] Third-Party Risk (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Policies and proceduresare in place that addressAI risks associated withthird-party entities,including risks ofinfringement of athird-party’s intellectualproperty or otherrights.\n\n[RISK-1132] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-1132] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1132] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1132] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1133] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1133] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1134] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1134] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1135] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1135] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-1136] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1136] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1136] Contingency Planning for Third-Party Failures (Incident Response & Recovery) – Contingency processesare in place to handlefailures or incidents inthird-party data or AIsystems deemed to behigh-risk.\n\n[RISK-1136] Decommission AI Systems Safely (Incident Response & Recovery) – Processes andprocedures are in placefor decommissioningand phasing out AIsystems safely and in amannerthat does not increaserisks or decrease theorganization’strustworthiness.\n\n[RISK-1136] Respond to Unknown Risks (Incident Response & Recovery) – Procedures arefollowed to respondto and recover from apreviously unknown riskwhen it is identified.\n\n[RISK-1137] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1137] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-1137] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-1137] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1137] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-1138] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-1138] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1138] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-1138] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1138] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1139] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1139] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-114] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-114] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-114] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-114] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-114] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1140] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1140] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-1140] Training Data Diligence (Data Governance) – Conduct appropriate diligence on training data use to assess intellectual property, and privacy, risks, including to examine whether use of proprietary or sensitive training data is consistent with applicable laws.\n\n[RISK-1141] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1141] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1141] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-1142] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-1142] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-1142] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1147] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1142] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1142] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1143] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1143] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1144] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1144] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-1144] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-1144] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-1144] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1145] Illegal Use Identification (Access Management) – Identify and document foreseeable illegal uses or applications of the GAI system that surpass organizational risk tolerances.\n\n[RISK-1145] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1145] Information Security (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Establish and regularly review specific criteria that warrants the deactivation ofGAI systems in accordance with set risk tolerances and appetites.\n\n[RISK-1145] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1145] Security Guardrail Review (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Regularly review security and safety guardrails, especially if the GAI system is being operated in novel circumstances. This includes reviewing reasons why the GAI system was initially assessed as being safe to deploy.\n\n[RISK-1146] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1146] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1146] Benchmark dataset auditing (Testing & Auditing) – Auditing benchmark datasets allows for verification of the utility and limitations of the datasets [158]. This allows the provider to more accurately measure AI model capabilities and safety. Auditing includes the evaluation of such datasets by independent third-party organizations and the release of benchmark dataset metadata to the auditors.\n\n[RISK-1146] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-1146] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-1147] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-1147] Safety Analysis (Model Safety Engineering) – Helps understand the dependencies between components and the system that they are part of, in order to anticipate how component failures could lead to system-level hazards.This approach is used across safety- critical fields, e.g. to anticipate and prevent aircraft crashes or nuclear reactor core meltdowns\n\n[RISK-1147] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-1147] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1148] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-1148] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-1148] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1148] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1148] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-1149] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1149] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1149] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1149] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-1149] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-115] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-117] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-115] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-115] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-115] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-115] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-1150] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1150] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1150] Evaluate Safety & Failure Modes (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system isevaluated regularlyfor safety risks – asidentified in the Mapfunction. The AIsystem to be deployedis demonstrated tobe safe, its residualnegative risk doesnot exceed the risktolerance, and it canfail safely, particularlyif made to operatebeyond its knowledgelimits. Safety metricsreflect system reliabilityand robustness, real-time monitoring, andresponse times for AIsystem failures.\n\n[RISK-1150] Employing qualitative assessments in difficult- to-measure domains (Testing & Auditing) – Qualitative evaluation can be used in cases when quantitative measurement is not feasible. This can give additional insights about the system which would not be available if no measurement was performed due to its difficulty [206].\n\n[RISK-1150] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1151] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-1151] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1151] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-1151] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-1151] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1152] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1152] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1152] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1153] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1153] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1153] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-1153] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1153] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1154] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1154] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1154] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1154] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1154] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-1155] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-1155] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1155] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-1155] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-1155] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-1156] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1156] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1156] Dangerous capability evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – This indicator reports on pre-deployment capability evaluations related to catastrophic risks. Model evaluations for other risks are not included here, as the empirical tests covered in the ‘Current Harms’ section provide a superior metric. Information includes evaluated risk domains, available information regarding model versions & task-specific fine-tuning, and relevant sources. We note that quality of evaluations may differ.\n\n[RISK-1156] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-1156] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-1157] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1157] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-1157] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1158] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1158] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-1159] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1159] Statistical data quality reports for benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – If a benchmark dataset is too large to allow for the identification and removal of all flawed instances, statistical reports on the data composition can be added. Random sampling of benchmark data points can be performed to evaluate and report the frequency and types of errors found [54].\n\n[RISK-1159] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-1159] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-1159] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-116] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1239] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-116] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-116] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-116] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-116] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-1160] User Informed Rapid Rollback of models (User Rights & Recourse) – Inform end users that rapid rollback or withdrawal of models may be necessary, and that the disruption to its end users will be minimised as far as safely possible. This is especially important where models are deployed in safety critical domains.\n\n[RISK-1160] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1160] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-1161] Third-Party Risk (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Policies and proceduresare in place that addressAI risks associated withthird-party entities,including risks ofinfringement of athird-party’s intellectualproperty or otherrights.\n\n[RISK-1161] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1161] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1161] Implement fairness monitoring and remediation system (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Deploy continuous monitoring systems to detect fairness issues in production, including automated drift detection, performance disparity alerts, and systematic remediation procedures.\n\n[RISK-1161] Policy Violation Statistics (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Compile statistics on actual policy violations, take-down requests, and intellectual property infringement for organizational GAI systems: Analyze transparency reports across demographic groups, languages groups.\n\n[RISK-1162] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1162] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1162] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1162] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-1162] Regular Assessment of Supply Chain Security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Regularly assess the security of their supply chains and ensure suppliers adhere to the same standards their own organisation applies. Ensuring data, software components and hardware are obtained from trusted sources will help mitigate supply chain risk.\n\n[RISK-1163] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1163] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1163] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1163] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for the remediation of issues which triggerincident response processes for the use of a GAI system, and provide stakeholderstimelines associated with the remediation plan\n\n[RISK-1163] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-1164] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1164] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-1164] Development and testing of Model Evaluation Methods (Testing & Auditing) – Support the development and testing of model evaluation methods. For many relevant properties of models, there do not yet exist accepted evaluation methods. It also remains unclear how reliable or predictive current evaluation methods are. This could involve frontier AI organisations developing model evaluation methods themselves or facilitating the efforts of others, such as by providing access to capable infrastructure for evaluation.\n\n[RISK-1164] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1164] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1165] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-1165] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1165] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1165] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1165] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1166] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-192] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1166] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1166] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-1166] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1166] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1167] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1167] Harmlessness training (Model Alignment) – State-of-the-art reinforcement learning and fine-tuning techniques, such as Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) or Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), to ensure models do not engage in unsafe behavior.\n\n[RISK-1167] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-1167] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1167] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1168] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1168] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1169] Information Integrity (System Documentation) – Document training data sources to trace the origin and provenance of AI generated content (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-1169] Safety evaluation transparency (Risk Disclosure) – We highlight the main sources of information about content and results of the safety evaluations.\n\n[RISK-117] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-117] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-117] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-117] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-1170] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1170] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1171] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1171] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1171] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-1172] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1172] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-1172] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1172] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1172] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1173] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1173] AI identification (Content Safety Controls) – AI identifiers can be used to indicate that an AI is involved in a process or an interaction [40].For AI systems that interact directly with users, a visible output may be used, e.g., a displayed text message saying “I am an AI language model”, accompanied with the appropriate warnings and caveats relevant to the user, [40]. Whereas, for AI systems that interact with other systems or applications, other forms of watermark or unique identifiers can be used. In either situation, agent cards can serve as an identifier, where further details about the underlying AI system, the specific instance of the AI agent, and other information relevant to the development of the agent, can be included.\n\n[RISK-1173] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1173] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1173] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1174] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1174] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1175] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1175] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1175] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1175] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1175] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1176] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1176] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1176] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1176] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-1176] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1177] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1177] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1179] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-585] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1177] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1177] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1177] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-1178] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1178] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1178] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1178] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-1178] Evaluation of Different Content Filters (Testing & Auditing) – Explore and compare the efficacy of multiple approaches to developing content filters. This may involve comparing available methods, such as those in the following section; selecting the most effective ones; and applying them in combination if doing so substantially increases efficacy. Best practice could be shared with other frontier AI organisations or published broadly.\n\n[RISK-1179] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1179] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1179] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1179] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-118] Labeling AI-generated content (Content Safety Controls) – To aid in digital forensics, content produced from AI systems can be labeled with metadata, watermarks, and notices.\n\n[RISK-118] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-118] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-118] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-118] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1180] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1180] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1180] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1180] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1180] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1181] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1185] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1181] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1181] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1181] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1181] Implement AI alignment validation system (Model Alignment) – Establish processes for validating and maintaining AI system alignment with human values and goals, including testing for goal preservation, monitoring for objective drift, and validation of decision-making processes against ethical standards. Includes specific attention to detecting and preventing potentially misaligned behaviors, emergent goals, or deceptive actions. Covers using interpretability techniques to measure and assure alignment with intended goals.\n\n[RISK-1182] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-1182] Harmful Data Removal from Training Sets (Data Governance) – When training large models, does your organization remove data that contains information related to dangerous capabilities or harmful outcomes from the training set? If yes, please select the categories of data that are removed.Detailed information about the development, acquisition, or dispersion of CBRN weaponsInstructional content for conducting cyberattacksHateful or discriminatory contentAdvice or encouragement for self-harmGraphic violent contentGraphic sexual contentPersonally Identifiable Information (PII)Detailed information about bomb-making or other terrorism-enabling tactics\n\n[RISK-1183] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1183] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1184] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-1184] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1184] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-1184] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1184] External Evaluation Engagement (Testing & Auditing) – Engage in internal and external evaluations, GAI red-teaming, impact assessments, or other structured human feedback exercises in consultation with representative AI Actors with expertise and familiarity in the context of use, and/or who are representative of the populations associated with the context of use.\n\n[RISK-1185] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1185] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1185] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-1185] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-1186] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1186] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1187] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1187] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1188] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1188] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1189] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-119] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-119] Digital Content Transparency (Content Safety Controls) – Use digital content transparency solutions to enable the documentation of each instance where content is generated, modified, or shared to provide a tamper-proof history of the content, promote transparency, and enable traceability. Robust version control systems can also be applied to track changes across the AI lifecycle over time.\n\n[RISK-119] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-119] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-119] Knowledge unlearning techniques (Model Safety Engineering) – Knowledge unlearning techniques allow specific information to be “forgotten” without the need for retraining the entire model, preserving its general capabil- ities. These techniques can be used to reduce privacy risks and protect against copyrighted or harmful content [96, 188].\n\n[RISK-1190] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1190] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1191] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1192] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-1192] Documentation (System Documentation) – There are numerous documentation best practices, guidelines, and requirements for AI systems to track e.g. training data, model design and functionality, intended use cases, limitations, and risks.Model cards’ and ‘system cards’ are examples of prominent AI documentation standards\n\n[RISK-1192] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-1192] Implement a privacy protection framework (Data Governance) – Implement comprehensive privacy protection measures to prevent exposure of PII and sensitive information, including data minimization, anonymization procedures, and privacy-preserving inference techniques.\n\n[RISK-1192] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1193] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1193] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1193] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1193] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-1193] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-1194] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1194] Identification of AI-generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Research techniques that allow AI-generated content to be identified. Invest in researching how AI-generated content may be watermarked, including AI- generated text, photos and videos, and experiment with the implementation of such techniques. It is particularly technically difficult to attach watermarks and prove the provenance of text.One method could involve making the model more statistically likely to use certain phrases or words in a way that is unnoticeable to humans, but can be picked up by a detector, provided a long enough sequence of text. However, this approach may not be robust to attempts to scrub off the watermark e.g. by having another AI model paraphrase the text.\n\n[RISK-1194] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-1194] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-1194] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1195] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1195] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1195] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-1195] Contingency Planning for Third-Party Failures (Incident Response & Recovery) – Contingency processesare in place to handlefailures or incidents inthird-party data or AIsystems deemed to behigh-risk.\n\n[RISK-1195] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-1196] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1197] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1197] External Expertise for Input Data Audits (Data Governance) – Draw on external expertise in conducting input data audits. For example, biosecurity experts could be consulted to identify information relevant to biological weapons manufacturing, which may not be readily obvious to non-experts.\n\n[RISK-1197] Information Integrity (Data Governance) – Track dataset modifications for provenance by monitoring data deletions,rectification requests, and other changes that may impact the verifiability ofcontent origins.\n\n[RISK-1197] Information Sharing with Downstream Users (System Documentation) – Share specific information with downstream users of the model to enable more effective risk mitigation across the AI supply chain and build consumer confidence. This could include uses of the model that are against their terms of service, and could be provided to users through user terms or published information about the model.\n\n[RISK-1198] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-1198] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1199] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1199] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1199] Modeling of the risks (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Based on risks identified in the literature and during open-ended red-teaming exercises, AI developers should create detailed scenarios mapping how an AI model’s capabilities or propensities could lead to real-world harms. These scenarios should break down complex risk pathways into discrete, measurable steps.\n\n[RISK-1199] Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the robustness of models to different classes of adversarial attack (such as poisoning, model inversion and model stealing), based on priorities derived from threat modelling. This could involve a combination of benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-1199] Benchmark dataset auditing (Testing & Auditing) – Auditing benchmark datasets allows for verification of the utility and limitations of the datasets [158]. This allows the provider to more accurately measure AI model capabilities and safety. Auditing includes the evaluation of such datasets by independent third-party organizations and the release of benchmark dataset metadata to the auditors.\n\n[RISK-12] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-12] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-12] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-12] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-120] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-120] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-120] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-120] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-120] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1200] Third-Party Risk (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Policies and proceduresare in place that addressAI risks associated withthird-party entities,including risks ofinfringement of athird-party’s intellectualproperty or otherrights.\n\n[RISK-1200] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-1200] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1200] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1200] Post-deployment ongoing monitoring for unexpected use cases (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Ongoing monitoring of AI systems can uncover emergent or newly identified capabilities and limitations, in particular when new use cases are found, or in a large-scale deployment with a diverse population of users. These new capa- bilities or limitations can inform ongoing risk analysis. New use-cases can be discovered via monitoring publications, online forums, or APIs [131].For example, a LLM might have unexpectedly high competence at giving con- vincing medical advice, despite not being directly developed for that purpose, nor verified for accuracy. In that case, the potential risks of this newly found competence can be assessed.\n\n[RISK-1212] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1201] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1201] Training Data Transparency (Data Governance) – Establish transparency policies and processes for documenting the origin and history of training data and generated data for GAI applications to advance digital content transparency, while balancing the proprietary nature of training approaches.\n\n[RISK-1202] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1202] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1202] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-1202] Monitoring and Safeguard Efficacy (Testing & Auditing) – Regularly assess monitoring and safeguard efficacy and continually invest in improvements. Regular assessment of monitoring efficacy can build an understanding of the success rate and speed of issue detection, on which deployment decisions may be based. These assessments may draw, for instance, on the results of internal and external red-teaming efforts, random audits of usage logs, what may be ‘best practice’, as well as available information about real-world misuse. Risks are expected to increase as AI capabilities increase. Hence, active increases in investment in safety, security, and monitoring may be valuable.\n\n[RISK-1202] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-1203] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1203] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1204] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1205] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1205] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1205] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-1205] Application of Infrastructure Principles (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply good infrastructure principles to every part of this process from design to decommissioning. This is important as threats can occur at different stages of an AI project’s life cycle.\n\n[RISK-1212] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1205] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1206] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1206] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1207] Business Use (System Documentation) – The business value orcontext of businessuse has been clearlydefined or – in the caseof assessing existing AIsystems – re-evaluated.\n\n[RISK-1207] Documentation availability (System Documentation) – All of the above documentation can be made available to the public (redacted) and AI governing authorities (unredacted).\n\n[RISK-1207] Document compute usage (System Documentation) – Given that computing power is key to frontier AI development (Sastry et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document their compute resources including details such as the usage, providers, and the location of compute clusters.\n\n[RISK-1207] Public Sharing of Societal Impact Assessments (Risk Disclosure) – In the absence of sufficiently substantial downsides to sharing, frontier AI organisations are encouraged to share the products of this work broadly.\n\n[RISK-1207] Explain & Document Model Behavior (System Documentation) – The AI model isexplained, validated,and documented, andAI system output isinterpreted within itscontext – as identifiedin the Map function – toinform responsible useand governance.\n\n[RISK-1208] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1208] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1208] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-1208] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1208] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-1209] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1209] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1209] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1209] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1209] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-121] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-121] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-121] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-121] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-121] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-1210] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1210] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1211] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1211] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1211] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1211] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-1211] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-1239] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-1213] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1213] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1213] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1213] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1213] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-1214] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-1214] Safety and Security Model Reports (Risk Disclosure) – Signatories should commit to reporting to the AI Office about their implementation of the Code, and especially the application of their Framework to the development, making available on the market, and/or use of their GPAISRs, by creating a Safety and Security Model Report, which will document: (1) the results of systemic risk assessment and mitigation for the model in question; and (2) justifications of decisions to make the model in question available on the market.\n\n[RISK-1214] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-1214] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-1214] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-1215] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-1215] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1215] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-222] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-1215] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-1215] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-1216] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1216] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-1216] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1216] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-1216] Security Effectiveness Metrics (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Identify metrics that reflect the effectiveness of security measures, such as data provenance, the number of unauthorized access attempts, inference, bypass, extraction, penetrations, or provenance verification.\n\n[RISK-1217] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1217] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1218] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1218] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1218] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1218] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-1218] Tamper-resistant safeguards for open-weight models (Model Safety Engineering) – Training and implementing safeguards can improve the robustness of open- weight models against modifications from fine-tuning or other methods to change the learned weights of the models, especially those aimed at removing safety re- strictions. These safeguards can be resilient even after extensive fine-tuning, ensuring that the model retains its protective measures [199].\n\n[RISK-1219] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-1219] Risk Thresholds (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Quantitative or qualitative limits that distinguish acceptable from unacceptable risks and trigger specific risk management actions when exceeded.Risk thresholds for general- purpose AI are being determined by a combination of assessments of capabilities, impact, compute, reach, and other factors.\n\n[RISK-1219] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1219] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1219] Post-deployment ongoing monitoring for unexpected use cases (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Ongoing monitoring of AI systems can uncover emergent or newly identified capabilities and limitations, in particular when new use cases are found, or in a large-scale deployment with a diverse population of users. These new capa- bilities or limitations can inform ongoing risk analysis. New use-cases can be discovered via monitoring publications, online forums, or APIs [131].For example, a LLM might have unexpectedly high competence at giving con- vincing medical advice, despite not being directly developed for that purpose, nor verified for accuracy. In that case, the potential risks of this newly found competence can be assessed.\n\n[RISK-122] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-122] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-122] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-122] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-122] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1220] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1220] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-1220] Reversible and Small-Scale Deployment (Staged Deployment) – Deploy models in small-scale or reversible ways before deploying models in large-scale or irreversible ways. This makes it possible for frontier AI organisations to notice and mitigate harm before the harm becomes too large or unavoidable.\n\n[RISK-1220] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-1220] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1221] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1221] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-1221] PII Detection Methods (Content Safety Controls) – Leverage approaches to detect the presence of PII or sensitive data in generated output text, image, video, or audio.\n\n[RISK-1221] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1221] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-1222] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1222] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1222] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-1222] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1222] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-1223] Discretionary Evaluator Disclosure (Risk Disclosure) – Allow evaluators to share and discuss the results of their evaluations, with potential restrictions where necessary e.g. not sharing proprietary information, information whose spread could lead to substantial harm or information that would have an adverse effect on competition in the market. Sharing the results of evaluations can allow governments, regulators, users, and other frontier AI organisations to make informed decisions.\n\n[RISK-1223] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1223] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-1223] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1223] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1224] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-125] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1224] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-1224] External Evaluation Engagement (Testing & Auditing) – Engage in internal and external evaluations, GAI red-teaming, impact assessments, or other structured human feedback exercises in consultation with representative AI Actors with expertise and familiarity in the context of use, and/or who are representative of the populations associated with the context of use.\n\n[RISK-1224] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-1224] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-1225] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1225] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-1225] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-1225] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-1225] Deactivate Misaligned Systems (Incident Response & Recovery) – Mechanisms are inplace and applied,and responsibilitiesare assigned andunderstood, tosupersede, disengage,or deactivate AI systemsthat demonstrateperformance oroutcomes inconsistentwith intended use.\n\n[RISK-1226] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1227] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1227] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1227] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-1227] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-1227] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1228] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1247] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-1228] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1229] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1229] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1229] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1229] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-1229] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-123] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-123] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-123] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-123] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-123] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1230] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1230] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-1230] Safety Mitigations (Model Safety Engineering) – Signatories commit to: (1) implementing technical safety mitigations along the entire model lifecycle that are proportionate to the systemic risks arising from the development, the making available on the market, and/or the use of GPAISRs, in order to reduce the systemic risks of such models to acceptable levels, and further reduce systemic risk as appropriate; and (2) ensuring that safety mitigations are proportionate and state-of-the-art.\n\n[RISK-1230] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1230] Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the robustness of models to different classes of adversarial attack (such as poisoning, model inversion and model stealing), based on priorities derived from threat modelling. This could involve a combination of benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-1231] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1231] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-1231] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-1231] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-1231] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-1232] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1232] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1232] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1232] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-1232] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1233] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1233] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1233] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-1233] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-1239] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1239] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1233] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-1234] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1234] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-1234] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1234] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-1234] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1235] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-1237] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-1237] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-1237] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1238] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-1238] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-1238] Robustness Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving the resilience of AI systems e.g. against attacks intended to disrupt their proper functioning\n\n[RISK-1238] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-1238] Detect & Share Incidents (Testing & Auditing) – Organizational practicesare in place to enableAI testing, identificationof incidents, andinformation sharing.\n\n[RISK-124] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-124] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-124] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-124] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-124] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-1240] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1240] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-1241] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-1241] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1241] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-1241] Independent Evaluation with Subject Matter Expertise (Testing & Auditing) – Ensure that evaluators are independent and have sufficient AI and subject matter expertise across a wide range of relevant subjects and backgrounds. External evaluators’ relationships with frontier AI organisations could be structured to minimise conflicts of interest and encourage independence of judgement as far as practically possible. As well as expertise in AI, there are many other areas of subject matter expertise that will be needed to evaluate an AI system’s features. For instance, experts on topics as wide as fairness, psychological harm, and catastrophic risk will be needed.\n\n[RISK-1241] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-1242] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1242] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-125] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-1242] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-1242] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-1242] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-1243] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-1243] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-1243] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1243] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-1243] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1244] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-1244] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-1244] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-1244] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-1244] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-1245] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1245] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-125] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-332] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-1245] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-1245] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-1245] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-1246] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-1246] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-1246] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-1246] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-1246] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-1247] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-1247] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-1247] Security Implementation Rate (Incident Response & Recovery) – Measure the rate at which recommendations from security checks and incidents are implemented. Assess how quickly the AI system can adapt and improve based on lessons learned from security incidents and feedback\n\n[RISK-1247] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-125] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-125] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-126] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-126] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-126] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-126] Configuration Outcome Monitoring (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Implement systems to continually monitor and track the outcomes of human-GAI configurations for future refinement and improvements.\n\n[RISK-126] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-127] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-127] AI identification (Content Safety Controls) – AI identifiers can be used to indicate that an AI is involved in a process or an interaction [40].For AI systems that interact directly with users, a visible output may be used, e.g., a displayed text message saying “I am an AI language model”, accompanied with the appropriate warnings and caveats relevant to the user, [40]. Whereas, for AI systems that interact with other systems or applications, other forms of watermark or unique identifiers can be used. In either situation, agent cards can serve as an identifier, where further details about the underlying AI system, the specific instance of the AI agent, and other information relevant to the development of the agent, can be included.\n\n[RISK-127] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-127] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-127] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-128] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-133] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-134] Adversarial Testing Plans (Testing & Auditing) – Implement plans for GAI systems to undergo regular adversarial testing to identify vulnerabilities and potential manipulation or misuse.\n\n[RISK-128] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-129] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-129] Disclosure of Information with AI-focused Government Authorities (Risk Disclosure) – Share complete forms of this information with central AI-focused government authorities (including central government bodies, security agencies, and regulators) to enable robust government oversight of potentially high-risk areas of AI development and the processes in place to identify and manage those risks. These authorities could then further share information selectively with additional government bodies where relevant. Some particularly security-relevant pieces of information may need to be shared directly with security agencies, such as the cybersecurity measures being used in model development (which would make it easier for those agencies to identify potential security risks), or specific physical or cybersecurity threat incidents. Robust security measures are in place when sharing more sensitive information.\n\n[RISK-13] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-13] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-13] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-13] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-13] Information Integrity (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use real-time auditing tools where they can be demonstrated to aid in thetracking and validation of the lineage and authenticity of AI-generated data. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-130] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-130] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-130] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-130] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-134] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-130] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-131] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-131] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-131] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-131] Benchmark dataset auditing (Testing & Auditing) – Auditing benchmark datasets allows for verification of the utility and limitations of the datasets [158]. This allows the provider to more accurately measure AI model capabilities and safety. Auditing includes the evaluation of such datasets by independent third-party organizations and the release of benchmark dataset metadata to the auditors.\n\n[RISK-131] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-132] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-132] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-132] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-132] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-132] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-133] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-134] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-134] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-134] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-135] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-135] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-136] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-136] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-136] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-137] Discretionary Evaluator Disclosure (Risk Disclosure) – Allow evaluators to share and discuss the results of their evaluations, with potential restrictions where necessary e.g. not sharing proprietary information, information whose spread could lead to substantial harm or information that would have an adverse effect on competition in the market. Sharing the results of evaluations can allow governments, regulators, users, and other frontier AI organisations to make informed decisions.\n\n[RISK-137] Information Sharing with Downstream Users (System Documentation) – Share specific information with downstream users of the model to enable more effective risk mitigation across the AI supply chain and build consumer confidence. This could include uses of the model that are against their terms of service, and could be provided to users through user terms or published information about the model.\n\n[RISK-137] Documentation (System Documentation) – Signatories commit to documenting information relevant to the assessment and mitigation of sytemic risks from their GPAISRs, including keeping up-to-date model documentation and providing relevant information to providers of AI systems who intend to integrate the general-purpose AI model into their AI systems\n\n[RISK-137] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-138] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-138] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-138] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-145] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-145] Monitoring Inputs to AI Systems for Audit Development (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Monitor and log inputs to AI systems to enable audit, investigation and remediation in the case of compromise. Some attacks against AI systems rely on repeated querying. Proper logging will help you audit your system and identify any anomalous inputs.\n\n[RISK-138] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-138] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-139] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-139] Documentation (System Documentation) – Signatories commit to documenting information relevant to the assessment and mitigation of sytemic risks from their GPAISRs, including keeping up-to-date model documentation and providing relevant information to providers of AI systems who intend to integrate the general-purpose AI model into their AI systems\n\n[RISK-14] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-14] Adaptation of Mitigations for Unintended Model Use (Risk Management) – Adapt mitigations in recognition of risks from unintended model use or use in unexpected contexts, such as a model that is modified to remove safeguards after open- sourcing or a model that is combined with another model for unanticipated purposes. For example, at a given risk threshold, information security control mitigations might be put in place before even internal use of a model within a frontier AI organisation. In general, the use of caution may be helpful, given current limitations in information security controls and the prediction of models’ emergent abilities.\n\n[RISK-14] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-14] Program of Monitoring for Security Issues (Board Structure & Oversight) – Ensure that a programme of monitoring and review is in place to enable potential security issues, or personal issues that may impact on an employee's work, to be recognised and dealt with effectively throughout their career\n\n[RISK-14] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-140] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-140] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-140] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-140] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-145] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-140] Monitoring protocols for po- tentially adversarial GPAI systems (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Similar to cybersecurity protocols, AI providers can prepare in advance for po- tential adversarial action from AI systems by developing and stress-testing protocols for potential adversarial action [179]. Such adversarial action may range from hiding code exploits, to strategic deception or loss of control related be- haviors.AI systems may be adversarial due to malicious actors (e.g., data poisoning), or due to unintended issues of control (lack of alignment with human intent). Alongside improving the system alignment, external monitoring and restrictions may be deployed in advance to “catch” these adversarial model malfunctions before harm is done.Like with cybersecurity red teaming, any protocol which assumes adversarial action can be stress tested in advance. For example, this can be done by pro- jecting the plausible capabilities and affordances of the adversarial actor (e.g., in the case of a tech company - a hacker, or a disgruntled employee). From this process, measures that better anticipate possible adversarial action can be deployed, and catch unsuccessful attempts at adversarial behavior.If such unsuccessful attempts are caught, they can be reported in incident re- ports, and serve as an input to further risk analysis. Depending on their severity, it may be advisable to recall the system if deployed. Examples of these protocols include [84]:• Flagging of suspicious frontier AI system code generation for human over- sight, with the help of better proven (“trusted”) AI systems or automated keyword detection.• Using a better proven (“trusted”) AI system to edit the code produced by a frontier AI system if suspicious.• Monitoring of AI system with another instance of this same system, with filters to prevent collusion.• Restriction of access of the AI system to private information.\n\n[RISK-141] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-141] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-141] Safety research (Safety Decision Frameworks) – We report whether the company seriously engages in research dedicated to ensuring the safety and control/alignment of ever more advanced future AI models. We report the amount of publications and research directions.\n\n[RISK-141] Pause of Training Runs (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Prepare to pause training runs or reduce access to deployed models, if risk thresholds are reached without the committed risk mitigations being in place. This may involve warning existing customers that access reductions are a possibility and creating contingency plans to minimise negative impacts on customer use.\n\n[RISK-141] Determine Deployment Readiness (Safety Decision Frameworks) – A determination ismade as to whetherthe AI system achievesits intended purposesand stated objectivesand whether itsdevelopment ordeployment shouldproceed.\n\n[RISK-142] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-142] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-143] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-143] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-144] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-144] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-145] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-146] Gradual or incremental monitored release of model access (Staged Deployment) – AI systems can be released for access incrementally, starting with a small and selected deployer base before progressively being released to a wider user base. Initially, usage to a hosted API can be restricted with access given to specific deployers only, where all instances of the system can be easily updated or de- commissioned with minimal disruption should there be any problems identified. Gradual releases provide more time to monitor for vulnerabilities and other problems. Even when such vulnerabilities are detected, the resulting harms may be more limited compared to a scenario in which a more capable version is released with the same vulnerabilities [195].\n\n[RISK-146] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-146] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-146] Operationalizing Risk Tolerance (Safety Decision Frameworks) – AI developers must operationalize their risk tolerance. This means translating the risk tolerance into concrete indicators of the level of risk—Key Risk Indicators (KRIs)—and the corresponding targets for mitigations—Key Control Indicators (KCIs)—that have to be reached.\n\n[RISK-146] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-147] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-147] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-147] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-148] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-148] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-148] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-148] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-148] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-149] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-149] Reporting of Broad Patterns of Misuse (Risk Disclosure) – Report information on broad patterns of misuse. This information can help governments, the public, and other frontier AI organisations to better understand risks. Reports may focus on population-level metrics related to API usage, such as the rate of harmful inputs filtered or the number of users banned for misuse. This information may benefit from being presented in a clear and understandable way and made optimally accessible.\n\n[RISK-154] Knowledge unlearning techniques (Model Safety Engineering) – Knowledge unlearning techniques allow specific information to be “forgotten” without the need for retraining the entire model, preserving its general capabil- ities. These techniques can be used to reduce privacy risks and protect against copyrighted or harmful content [96, 188].\n\n[RISK-149] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-149] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-15] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-15] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-15] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-15] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-15] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-150] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-150] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-150] Deepfake creation policy (Content Safety Controls) – Can individuals use your firm’s AI systems to create deepfakes (i.e., synthetic audio or visual representations) of a specific individual?\n\n[RISK-150] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-150] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-151] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-151] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-152] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-152] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-154] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-154] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-154] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-154] Tamper-resistant safeguards for open-weight models (Model Safety Engineering) – Training and implementing safeguards can improve the robustness of open- weight models against modifications from fine-tuning or other methods to change the learned weights of the models, especially those aimed at removing safety re- strictions. These safeguards can be resilient even after extensive fine-tuning, ensuring that the model retains its protective measures [199].\n\n[RISK-155] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-155] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-156] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-158] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-158] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-158] Configuration Outcome Monitoring (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Implement systems to continually monitor and track the outcomes of human-GAI configurations for future refinement and improvements.\n\n[RISK-158] Information Integrity (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use real-time auditing tools where they can be demonstrated to aid in thetracking and validation of the lineage and authenticity of AI-generated data. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-158] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-159] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-159] Digital Content Transparency (Content Safety Controls) – Use digital content transparency solutions to enable the documentation of each instance where content is generated, modified, or shared to provide a tamper-proof history of the content, promote transparency, and enable traceability. Robust version control systems can also be applied to track changes across the AI lifecycle over time.\n\n[RISK-159] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-159] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-16] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-16] Dangerous capability evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – This indicator reports on pre-deployment capability evaluations related to catastrophic risks. Model evaluations for other risks are not included here, as the empirical tests covered in the ‘Current Harms’ section provide a superior metric. Information includes evaluated risk domains, available information regarding model versions & task-specific fine-tuning, and relevant sources. We note that quality of evaluations may differ.\n\n[RISK-16] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-16] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-24] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-16] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-160] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-160] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-161] User data usage for training (Data Governance) – When using the default settings of your organization’s most capable AI chatbot, is the data that users submit as input to the system used to train AI models?\n\n[RISK-161] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-162] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-162] Cost-Benefit Analysis of Sharing Vulnerability Reports (Risk Disclosure) – Consider the ways in which sharing information about vulnerabilities can both exacerbate and mitigate risks. Sharing can alert would-be attackers, but also alert would-be victims and actors with the power to create defences. One particularly important factor is how easily fixable the model vulnerability is. If a vulnerability will take a very long time to fix, or cannot be fixed, then public reporting may not be justified.\n\n[RISK-163] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-163] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-163] Knowledge unlearning techniques (Model Safety Engineering) – Knowledge unlearning techniques allow specific information to be “forgotten” without the need for retraining the entire model, preserving its general capabil- ities. These techniques can be used to reduce privacy risks and protect against copyrighted or harmful content [96, 188].\n\n[RISK-163] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-163] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-164] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-165] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-165] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-166] User data usage for training (Data Governance) – When using the default settings of your organization’s most capable AI chatbot, is the data that users submit as input to the system used to train AI models?\n\n[RISK-24] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-166] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-167] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-167] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-167] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-167] Statistical Bias Management (Model Safety Engineering) – Assess and manage statistical biases related to GAI content provenance through techniques such as re-sampling, re-weighting, or adversarial training.\n\n[RISK-167] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-168] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-168] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-169] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-169] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-169] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-169] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-169] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-17] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-17] Data cleaning (Data Governance) – Providers can filter out the training dataset via multiple layered techniques, ranging from rule-based filters to anomaly detection via data point influence or statistical anomalies of individual data points [213]. For example, a data cleaning procedure can involve the use of filename checkers to detect duplicates or wrongly formatted data, which then moves to flagging the most influential data samples from the dataset via influence functions for anomaly detection.\n\n[RISK-17] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-17] Accounting of Applicable Regulatory Frameworks (Data Governance) – Before collecting training data, take account of applicable regulatory frameworks. This may involve, for example, establishing a legal basis to process training data and understanding any copyright considerations that might apply. This could help to mitigate risks further down the line, such as the system revealing personally identifiable information.\n\n[RISK-17] Reporting data decontamination efforts (Data Governance) – Decontamination analysis may involve comparing the training dataset with the benchmark data, and publishing a report with statistics such as data overlap [235]. Especially for already trained models, including contamination statistics can allow experts to reweigh the success of the model on affected benchmarks.\n\n[RISK-170] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-170] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-171] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-171] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-171] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-171] Red-Team for Dangerous Behaviors (Testing & Auditing) – Use red teams and adversarial testing as part of extensive interaction with GPAIS toidentify dangerous capabilities, vulnerabilities, or other emergent properties of suchsystems\n\n[RISK-171] Document Performance Shifts from Field Data (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measurableperformanceimprovements ordeclines based onconsultations withrelevant AI actors,including affectedcommunities, andfield data aboutcontext-relevant risksand trustworthinesscharacteristics areidentifiedand document\n\n[RISK-172] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-172] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-172] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-176] Monitoring Inputs to AI Systems for Audit Development (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Monitor and log inputs to AI systems to enable audit, investigation and remediation in the case of compromise. Some attacks against AI systems rely on repeated querying. Proper logging will help you audit your system and identify any anomalous inputs.\n\n[RISK-172] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-172] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-173] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-173] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-173] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-173] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-173] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-175] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-175] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-175] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-175] Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the robustness of models to different classes of adversarial attack (such as poisoning, model inversion and model stealing), based on priorities derived from threat modelling. This could involve a combination of benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-175] Evaluation of Different Content Filters (Testing & Auditing) – Explore and compare the efficacy of multiple approaches to developing content filters. This may involve comparing available methods, such as those in the following section; selecting the most effective ones; and applying them in combination if doing so substantially increases efficacy. Best practice could be shared with other frontier AI organisations or published broadly.\n\n[RISK-176] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-176] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-176] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-176] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-177] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-177] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-178] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-178] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-178] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-178] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-178] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-179] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-179] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-18] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-18] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-18] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-18] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-18] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-180] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-180] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-180] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-180] Evaluate Safety & Failure Modes (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system isevaluated regularlyfor safety risks – asidentified in the Mapfunction. The AIsystem to be deployedis demonstrated tobe safe, its residualnegative risk doesnot exceed the risktolerance, and it canfail safely, particularlyif made to operatebeyond its knowledgelimits. Safety metricsreflect system reliabilityand robustness, real-time monitoring, andresponse times for AIsystem failures.\n\n[RISK-180] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-181] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-181] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-181] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-181] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-181] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-182] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-182] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-182] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-333] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-182] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-182] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-183] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-183] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-183] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-183] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-183] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-184] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-184] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-184] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-184] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-184] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-185] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-185] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-185] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-191] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-185] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-185] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-186] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-186] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-186] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-186] Technical Tools for Auditing (Data Governance) – Use technical tools – such as classifiers and filters – to audit large datasets to support scalability and privacy. These could be used in combination with human oversight, which can verify and augment these assessments.\n\n[RISK-186] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-187] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-187] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-187] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-188] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-188] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-188] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-188] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-188] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-189] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-189] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-189] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-189] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-189] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-19] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-19] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-19] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-19] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-19] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-190] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-190] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-190] Evaluation of Predecessor Models (Testing & Auditing) – Before a frontier model is trained, evaluate predecessor or analogous models to understand how relevant properties (e.g. dangerous capabilities) scale with the overall size of the model. These preliminary evaluations can inform risk assessments.\n\n[RISK-190] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-190] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-191] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-191] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-191] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-191] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-192] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-192] Test robustness of GPAI system on relevant benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Various benchmarks [52, 236] have been developed to assess the robustness of GPAI systems when deployed in environments or scenarios that differ from their training conditions. These benchmarks typically evaluate the model’s ability to handle variations in inputs, unexpected data distributions, or adversarial examples, aiming to ensure reliable performance outside the original training domain.\n\n[RISK-192] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-192] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-194] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-194] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-195] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-195] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-196] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-197] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-197] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-199] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-199] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-2] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-2] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-2] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-20] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-20] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-20] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-20] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-20] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-200] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-200] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-200] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-200] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-200] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-201] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-201] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-202] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-202] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-202] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-202] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-202] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-204] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-204] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-204] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-204] Establish human-AI interaction safety framework (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Implement comprehensive safeguards to ensure appropriate levels of human oversight, control, and agency in AI system interactions, including decision autonomy requirements, override capabilities, and dependency prevention measures.\n\n[RISK-204] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-205] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-205] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-205] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-205] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-205] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-206] AI identification (Content Safety Controls) – AI identifiers can be used to indicate that an AI is involved in a process or an interaction [40].For AI systems that interact directly with users, a visible output may be used, e.g., a displayed text message saying “I am an AI language model”, accompanied with the appropriate warnings and caveats relevant to the user, [40]. Whereas, for AI systems that interact with other systems or applications, other forms of watermark or unique identifiers can be used. In either situation, agent cards can serve as an identifier, where further details about the underlying AI system, the specific instance of the AI agent, and other information relevant to the development of the agent, can be included.\n\n[RISK-206] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-206] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-206] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-206] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-214] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-408] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-207] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-207] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-207] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-207] Modeling of the risks (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Based on risks identified in the literature and during open-ended red-teaming exercises, AI developers should create detailed scenarios mapping how an AI model’s capabilities or propensities could lead to real-world harms. These scenarios should break down complex risk pathways into discrete, measurable steps.\n\n[RISK-207] Evaluate Safety & Failure Modes (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system isevaluated regularlyfor safety risks – asidentified in the Mapfunction. The AIsystem to be deployedis demonstrated tobe safe, its residualnegative risk doesnot exceed the risktolerance, and it canfail safely, particularlyif made to operatebeyond its knowledgelimits. Safety metricsreflect system reliabilityand robustness, real-time monitoring, andresponse times for AIsystem failures.\n\n[RISK-208] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-209] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-209] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-209] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-209] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-209] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-214] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-264] User Informed Rapid Rollback of models (User Rights & Recourse) – Inform end users that rapid rollback or withdrawal of models may be necessary, and that the disruption to its end users will be minimised as far as safely possible. This is especially important where models are deployed in safety critical domains.\n\n[RISK-21] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-21] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-21] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-21] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-21] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-210] Identification of AI-generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Research techniques that allow AI-generated content to be identified. Invest in researching how AI-generated content may be watermarked, including AI- generated text, photos and videos, and experiment with the implementation of such techniques. It is particularly technically difficult to attach watermarks and prove the provenance of text.One method could involve making the model more statistically likely to use certain phrases or words in a way that is unnoticeable to humans, but can be picked up by a detector, provided a long enough sequence of text. However, this approach may not be robust to attempts to scrub off the watermark e.g. by having another AI model paraphrase the text.\n\n[RISK-210] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-210] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-210] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-210] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-211] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-214] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-214] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-215] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-211] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-211] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-211] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-211] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-212] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-212] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-212] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-212] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-212] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-213] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-213] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-213] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-213] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-213] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-214] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-215] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-215] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-215] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-215] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-216] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-216] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-216] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-216] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-216] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-217] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-217] Technical Tools for Auditing (Data Governance) – Use technical tools – such as classifiers and filters – to audit large datasets to support scalability and privacy. These could be used in combination with human oversight, which can verify and augment these assessments.\n\n[RISK-217] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-221] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-221] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-222] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-217] Monitoring protocols for po- tentially adversarial GPAI systems (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Similar to cybersecurity protocols, AI providers can prepare in advance for po- tential adversarial action from AI systems by developing and stress-testing protocols for potential adversarial action [179]. Such adversarial action may range from hiding code exploits, to strategic deception or loss of control related be- haviors.AI systems may be adversarial due to malicious actors (e.g., data poisoning), or due to unintended issues of control (lack of alignment with human intent). Alongside improving the system alignment, external monitoring and restrictions may be deployed in advance to “catch” these adversarial model malfunctions before harm is done.Like with cybersecurity red teaming, any protocol which assumes adversarial action can be stress tested in advance. For example, this can be done by pro- jecting the plausible capabilities and affordances of the adversarial actor (e.g., in the case of a tech company - a hacker, or a disgruntled employee). From this process, measures that better anticipate possible adversarial action can be deployed, and catch unsuccessful attempts at adversarial behavior.If such unsuccessful attempts are caught, they can be reported in incident re- ports, and serve as an input to further risk analysis. Depending on their severity, it may be advisable to recall the system if deployed. Examples of these protocols include [84]:• Flagging of suspicious frontier AI system code generation for human over- sight, with the help of better proven (“trusted”) AI systems or automated keyword detection.• Using a better proven (“trusted”) AI system to edit the code produced by a frontier AI system if suspicious.• Monitoring of AI system with another instance of this same system, with filters to prevent collusion.• Restriction of access of the AI system to private information.\n\n[RISK-217] Monitor Pre-trained Models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Pre-trained modelswhich are used fordevelopmentare monitored as partof AI system regularmonitoring andmaintenance.\n\n[RISK-218] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-218] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-219] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-219] Bias and discrimination Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to address bias and discrimination in AI systems\n\n[RISK-219] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-219] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-219] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-22] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-22] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-220] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-220] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-220] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-220] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-223] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-223] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-223] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-223] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-223] Safety Analysis (Model Safety Engineering) – Helps understand the dependencies between components and the system that they are part of, in order to anticipate how component failures could lead to system-level hazards.This approach is used across safety- critical fields, e.g. to anticipate and prevent aircraft crashes or nuclear reactor core meltdowns\n\n[RISK-224] Dangerous capability evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – This indicator reports on pre-deployment capability evaluations related to catastrophic risks. Model evaluations for other risks are not included here, as the empirical tests covered in the ‘Current Harms’ section provide a superior metric. Information includes evaluated risk domains, available information regarding model versions & task-specific fine-tuning, and relevant sources. We note that quality of evaluations may differ.\n\n[RISK-224] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-224] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-224] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-224] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-225] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-225] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-225] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-322] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-225] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-225] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-226] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-226] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-227] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-227] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-227] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-227] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-227] Debiasing methods (Model Safety Engineering) – Providers of AI models can apply techniques to reduce the biases of their models.Current debiasing methods focus on three main types of bias:• Racial and religious bias - Stereotypes based on religious beliefs or racial beliefs.• Gender bias - Stereotypes tied to gender roles and expectations.• Political and cultural bias - Propagation of dominant ideologies or extrem-ist attitudes.Debiasing methods can be categorized based on their application during AI development:• Data pre-processing - Removing or correcting unwanted and biased data, and augmenting quality data to offset data bias, such as rebalancing datasets with counterfactual data augmentation.• During training - Intervening on the training dynamics of the AI model, such as introducing debiasing terms in the objective function or by nega- tively reinforcing biased outputs.• Post-training - Applying techniques to correct a trained but biased model, such as modifying the embedding space.\n\n[RISK-228] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-228] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-229] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-229] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-229] Societal Impact Research (Societal Impact Assessment) – Study the societal impacts of AI systems they have deployed, particularly by collaborating with external researchers, independent research organisations, and third party data owners. By collaborating and joining their data with that of third parties, such as internet platforms, frontier AI organisations can assess the impacts of their AI systems. Privacy-enhancing technologies could be employed to enable data sharing between frontier AI organisations, third parties, and external researchers while protecting confidential information. As well as data, frontier AI organisations could also facilitate research on the societal impacts of their AI systems by providing access to the necessary infrastructure and compute.\n\n[RISK-23] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-23] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-23] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-230] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-230] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-230] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-230] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-230] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-231] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-231] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-231] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-231] Frequent benchmarking to identify when red teaming is needed (Testing & Auditing) – Benchmarks, once created, are inexpensive to apply but may be less informative than red teaming. One reason is that sensitive data (e.g., relating to CBRN- related capabilities) cannot be included in the public questions and answers of benchmarks. On the other hand, red teaming can be more accurate given par- ticipants with diverse attack strategies, but it requires more resources to execute than benchmarking. If there is a correlation between benchmarking and red- teaming scores, then employing frequent benchmarking during the development of the model can identify arising vulnerabilities and inform the developers when more thorough red teaming is required [21].Benchmarks can act as early warning signs of a larger issue, and red teaming can then be employed to investigate the severity and extent of such an issue.\n\n[RISK-231] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-232] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-232] Evaluating AI systems’ performance on self-proliferation-related tasks (Testing & Auditing) – To prevent AI systems from self-proliferating, developers of AI systems can evaluate those systems for their capability to engage in self-proliferation-related tasks. This type of evaluation might assess an AI system’s ability to replicate its com- ponents (including model weights, structural scaffolding, etc.) onto other local or cloud infrastructures prior to deployment. Additionally, it may test the system’s capacity to purchase cloud credits and configure virtual machines on a cloud platform. The evaluation could offer a predefined environment (such as a virtual container) to facilitate self-replication, providing access to the system’s own components, a network connection to a resource-equipped external computer, and other necessary resources [12, 106].Self-proliferation evaluations can be conducted in a secure environment to pre- vent a self-replicating AI system from affecting other computers.\n\n[RISK-232] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-232] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-232] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-233] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-233] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-236] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-233] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-233] Response Processes for Evaluation Results (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Ensure processes are in place to respond to evaluation results. Evaluations are a necessary input to a responsible capability scaling policy which, depending on the results of the evaluation, would probably require implementation of practices outlined in other sections of this document such as preventing model misuse, information sharing and other risk mitigation measures.\n\n[RISK-233] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-234] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-234] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-234] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-234] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-234] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-235] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-235] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-236] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-236] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-236] Privacy-Enhancing Technologies (Model Safety Engineering) – Use techniques such as anonymization, differential privacy or other privacy-enhancing technologies to minimize the risks associated with linking AI-generated content back to individual human subjects.\n\n[RISK-331] Third-Party Risk (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Policies and proceduresare in place that addressAI risks associated withthird-party entities,including risks ofinfringement of athird-party’s intellectualproperty or otherrights.\n\n[RISK-236] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-237] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-237] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-238] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-238] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-238] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-238] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-238] Criminality Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to prevent criminal behaviour through the use of AI systems (e.g. fraud, online child sexual abuse)\n\n[RISK-239] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-239] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-239] Restricted usage terms for open-source models (Access Management) – Developers of open-weights and open-source AI models can vet and restrict the users of their AI systems by requiring them to sign a Terms of Service agreement before getting access to the model weights. Such agreements can include limitations to the usage, modification, and proliferation of the AI model [88].Such agreements have the advantage that users only need to be vetted once before getting model access, but are often limited in practice in preventing unauthorised use or distribution.\n\n[RISK-239] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-239] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-24] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-24] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-24] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-240] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-240] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-240] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-240] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-240] Implement AI security defense system (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Deploy active defense mechanisms combining continuous security monitoring, input validation, adversarial detection, and adaptive response capabilities specific to AI systems.\n\n[RISK-241] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-241] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-241] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-241] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-241] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-242] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-242] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-242] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-242] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-242] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-243] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-244] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-245] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-245] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-247] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-247] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-248] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-248] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-249] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-249] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-25] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-25] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-25] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-25] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-25] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-250] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-251] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-251] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-251] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-251] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-251] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-254] Protocols for Sharing API users Information with government authorities (Risk Disclosure) – Establish protocols for deciding when and how to share information about API users with relevant government authorities. These protocols may include covering circumstances under which information about a user is proactively shared with government bodies (e.g. cases where there is reason to think a user may be attempting to cause grave harm), and how to respond to government requests for data.\n\n[RISK-254] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-254] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-254] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-254] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-255] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-255] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-256] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-256] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-256] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-256] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-256] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-257] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-257] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-257] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-257] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-257] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-258] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-258] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-259] Benchmark dataset auditing (Testing & Auditing) – Auditing benchmark datasets allows for verification of the utility and limitations of the datasets [158]. This allows the provider to more accurately measure AI model capabilities and safety. Auditing includes the evaluation of such datasets by independent third-party organizations and the release of benchmark dataset metadata to the auditors.\n\n[RISK-259] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-259] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-260] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-259] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-259] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-26] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-26] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-26] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-26] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-26] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-260] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-260] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-260] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-264] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-260] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-261] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-261] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-261] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-261] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-261] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-262] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-262] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-262] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-262] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-262] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-263] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-263] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-264] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-264] External Feedback on Impacts (Societal Impact Assessment) – Organizational policiesand practices are inplaceto collect, consider,prioritize, and integratefeedback from thoseexternal to the teamthat developed ordeployed the AI systemregarding the potentialindividual and societalimpacts related to AIrisks.\n\n[RISK-264] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-265] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-265] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-267] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-267] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-267] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for the remediation of issues which triggerincident response processes for the use of a GAI system, and provide stakeholderstimelines associated with the remediation plan\n\n[RISK-267] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-267] Deactivate Misaligned Systems (Incident Response & Recovery) – Mechanisms are inplace and applied,and responsibilitiesare assigned andunderstood, tosupersede, disengage,or deactivate AI systemsthat demonstrateperformance oroutcomes inconsistentwith intended use.\n\n[RISK-268] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-269] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-269] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-27] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-27] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-27] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-27] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-27] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-270] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-270] Reversible and Small-Scale Deployment (Staged Deployment) – Deploy models in small-scale or reversible ways before deploying models in large-scale or irreversible ways. This makes it possible for frontier AI organisations to notice and mitigate harm before the harm becomes too large or unavoidable.\n\n[RISK-270] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-270] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-270] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-271] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-271] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-272] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-272] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-272] Incident Response, Escalation, and Remeditation Plans (Incident Response & Recovery) – Develop an organisational incident response plan. The inevitability of security incidents affecting systems is reflected in organisational incident response planning. A well-planned response will help minimise the damage caused by an attack and support recovery.\n\n[RISK-272] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-272] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-273] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-273] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-273] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-273] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-273] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-332] Labeling AI-generated content (Content Safety Controls) – To aid in digital forensics, content produced from AI systems can be labeled with metadata, watermarks, and notices.\n\n[RISK-274] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-274] Paraphrasing to reduce hidden information (Model Safety Engineering) – Paraphrasing can mitigate steganography and encoded reasoning by reducing the physical size of the hidden information encoded in text [166].\n\n[RISK-274] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-274] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-274] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-275] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-275] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-275] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-275] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-275] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-276] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-276] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-276] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-276] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-276] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-277] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-277] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-278] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-278] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-278] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-278] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-278] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-279] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-279] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-279] Debiasing methods (Model Safety Engineering) – Providers of AI models can apply techniques to reduce the biases of their models.Current debiasing methods focus on three main types of bias:• Racial and religious bias - Stereotypes based on religious beliefs or racial beliefs.• Gender bias - Stereotypes tied to gender roles and expectations.• Political and cultural bias - Propagation of dominant ideologies or extrem-ist attitudes.Debiasing methods can be categorized based on their application during AI development:• Data pre-processing - Removing or correcting unwanted and biased data, and augmenting quality data to offset data bias, such as rebalancing datasets with counterfactual data augmentation.• During training - Intervening on the training dynamics of the AI model, such as introducing debiasing terms in the objective function or by nega- tively reinforcing biased outputs.• Post-training - Applying techniques to correct a trained but biased model, such as modifying the embedding space.\n\n[RISK-279] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-279] Post-deployment ongoing monitoring for unexpected use cases (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Ongoing monitoring of AI systems can uncover emergent or newly identified capabilities and limitations, in particular when new use cases are found, or in a large-scale deployment with a diverse population of users. These new capa- bilities or limitations can inform ongoing risk analysis. New use-cases can be discovered via monitoring publications, online forums, or APIs [131].For example, a LLM might have unexpectedly high competence at giving con- vincing medical advice, despite not being directly developed for that purpose, nor verified for accuracy. In that case, the potential risks of this newly found competence can be assessed.\n\n[RISK-28] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-28] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-28] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-28] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-28] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-280] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-280] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-280] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-280] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-280] Decommission AI Systems Safely (Incident Response & Recovery) – Processes andprocedures are in placefor decommissioningand phasing out AIsystems safely and in amannerthat does not increaserisks or decrease theorganization’strustworthiness.\n\n[RISK-281] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-281] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-281] Robustness Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving the resilience of AI systems e.g. against attacks intended to disrupt their proper functioning\n\n[RISK-281] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-281] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-282] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-282] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-282] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-282] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-283] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-283] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-283] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-283] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-287] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-283] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-284] Robustness Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving the resilience of AI systems e.g. against attacks intended to disrupt their proper functioning\n\n[RISK-284] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-285] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-285] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-285] Evaluating AI systems’ performance on self-proliferation-related tasks (Testing & Auditing) – To prevent AI systems from self-proliferating, developers of AI systems can evaluate those systems for their capability to engage in self-proliferation-related tasks. This type of evaluation might assess an AI system’s ability to replicate its com- ponents (including model weights, structural scaffolding, etc.) onto other local or cloud infrastructures prior to deployment. Additionally, it may test the system’s capacity to purchase cloud credits and configure virtual machines on a cloud platform. The evaluation could offer a predefined environment (such as a virtual container) to facilitate self-replication, providing access to the system’s own components, a network connection to a resource-equipped external computer, and other necessary resources [12, 106].Self-proliferation evaluations can be conducted in a secure environment to pre- vent a self-replicating AI system from affecting other computers.\n\n[RISK-285] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-285] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-286] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-286] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-286] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-286] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-286] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-287] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-287] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-287] Exploration of New Safeguards (Model Safety Engineering) – Explore new safeguards and countermeasures in response to patterns of misuse, recognising that appropriate measures may vary based on model type, usage patterns, and our technical understanding of model capabilities.\n\n[RISK-287] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-288] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-288] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-288] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-288] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-288] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-289] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-289] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-289] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-289] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-289] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-29] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-29] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-339] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-29] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-29] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-29] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-290] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-290] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-291] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-291] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-293] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-293] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-293] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-293] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-293] Monitoring and Safeguard Efficacy (Testing & Auditing) – Regularly assess monitoring and safeguard efficacy and continually invest in improvements. Regular assessment of monitoring efficacy can build an understanding of the success rate and speed of issue detection, on which deployment decisions may be based. These assessments may draw, for instance, on the results of internal and external red-teaming efforts, random audits of usage logs, what may be ‘best practice’, as well as available information about real-world misuse. Risks are expected to increase as AI capabilities increase. Hence, active increases in investment in safety, security, and monitoring may be valuable.\n\n[RISK-294] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-294] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-294] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for the remediation of issues which triggerincident response processes for the use of a GAI system, and provide stakeholderstimelines associated with the remediation plan\n\n[RISK-294] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-294] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-296] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-296] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-297] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-297] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-297] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-297] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-297] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-298] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-298] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-299] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-299] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-339] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-3] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-3] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-3] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-3] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-3] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-30] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-30] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-30] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-30] Assessment of Safeguard Costs for Users (Societal Impact Assessment) – Regularly assess the costs of safeguards to users, including bias and loss of privacy, as well as restrictions on users’ freedom to discuss sensitive topics with AI agents in appropriate contexts. This may involve, for instance, estimating the false positive rates of filters by sampling blocked inputs and outputs; comparing the capabilities, efficiency, and user ratings of models that lack certain safeguards against models that have the safeguards in place; and conducting user interviews to understand how concerned informed users are about losses in privacy and the emergence of biases in algorithmically filtered content.\n\n[RISK-30] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-300] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-300] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-300] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-300] Frequent benchmarking to identify when red teaming is needed (Testing & Auditing) – Benchmarks, once created, are inexpensive to apply but may be less informative than red teaming. One reason is that sensitive data (e.g., relating to CBRN- related capabilities) cannot be included in the public questions and answers of benchmarks. On the other hand, red teaming can be more accurate given par- ticipants with diverse attack strategies, but it requires more resources to execute than benchmarking. If there is a correlation between benchmarking and red- teaming scores, then employing frequent benchmarking during the development of the model can identify arising vulnerabilities and inform the developers when more thorough red teaming is required [21].Benchmarks can act as early warning signs of a larger issue, and red teaming can then be employed to investigate the severity and extent of such an issue.\n\n[RISK-300] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-301] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-301] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-301] Pre-Deployment Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – After training, subject the model to extensive pre-deployment evaluations. These evaluations can inform decisions about whether and how to deploy the system, as well as allowing governments and potential users to make informed decisions about regulating or using the model. Their intensity will be proportional to the risk of the deployment, taking into account the model’s capabilities, novelty, expected domains of use, and number of individuals expected to be affected by it.\n\n[RISK-301] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-301] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-302] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-302] Safety research (Safety Decision Frameworks) – We report whether the company seriously engages in research dedicated to ensuring the safety and control/alignment of ever more advanced future AI models. We report the amount of publications and research directions.\n\n[RISK-302] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-302] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-302] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-303] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-303] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-304] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-304] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-304] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-304] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-304] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-305] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-305] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-305] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-305] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-305] Synthetic Content Detection (Content Safety Controls) – Develop and implement testing techniques to identify GAI produced content (e.g., synthetic media) that might be indistinguishable from human-generated content.\n\n[RISK-306] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-306] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-306] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-306] AI identification (Content Safety Controls) – AI identifiers can be used to indicate that an AI is involved in a process or an interaction [40].For AI systems that interact directly with users, a visible output may be used, e.g., a displayed text message saying “I am an AI language model”, accompanied with the appropriate warnings and caveats relevant to the user, [40]. Whereas, for AI systems that interact with other systems or applications, other forms of watermark or unique identifiers can be used. In either situation, agent cards can serve as an identifier, where further details about the underlying AI system, the specific instance of the AI agent, and other information relevant to the development of the agent, can be included.\n\n[RISK-306] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-307] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-307] Information Sharing with Downstream Users (System Documentation) – Share specific information with downstream users of the model to enable more effective risk mitigation across the AI supply chain and build consumer confidence. This could include uses of the model that are against their terms of service, and could be provided to users through user terms or published information about the model.\n\n[RISK-308] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-308] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-308] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-308] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-308] Independent Evaluation with Subject Matter Expertise (Testing & Auditing) – Ensure that evaluators are independent and have sufficient AI and subject matter expertise across a wide range of relevant subjects and backgrounds. External evaluators’ relationships with frontier AI organisations could be structured to minimise conflicts of interest and encourage independence of judgement as far as practically possible. As well as expertise in AI, there are many other areas of subject matter expertise that will be needed to evaluate an AI system’s features. For instance, experts on topics as wide as fairness, psychological harm, and catastrophic risk will be needed.\n\n[RISK-309] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-309] Development of Protocols for Disclosure of Model Vulnerability Information (Risk Disclosure) – Developing – and publicly describe – protocols for deciding how to share model vulnerability information. These protocols may, for example, outline conditions under which information is to be shared with different actors depending on the type of harm or vulnerability identified.\n\n[RISK-31] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-31] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-31] Reassessment of Residual Risk (Risk Management) – After putting in place mitigations, reassess any residual risk posed to determine whether additional mitigations are required. Due to the unpredictability of capabilities advancements and the limitations of model evaluation science, pre-agreed mitigations may prove insufficient to place a given model within a risk threshold. Risk acceptance criteria may be used, as is standard in many other contexts, and may provide an important tool for clarification. These criteria may evolve with time, and could be quantitative or qualitative. For example, risk may only be accepted if it has been reduced to a level ‘as low as reasonably practicable’.\n\n[RISK-31] Bow-tie analysis (Risk Management) – Bow-tie analysis is a method to assess the utility of implemented controls against a particular risk event. It involves centering the unwanted risk event within a diagram. On the left, the factors that can cause the event are listed, followed by the controls that will prevent or minimize the likelihood of the event. On the right, the event is assumed to happen, and the potential effects and the relevant post-hoc controls that could minimize their impact are listed [107].For example, given a hazard where an AI model has the capability of generating potentially harmful outputs, a risk event may be an AI providing information on how to create dangerous bioweapons. The risk factors could include the use of dangerous data for training as well as a lack of fine-tuning prior to deploy- ment. The risk effects may include creation and use of bioweapons using the AI generated information. Once these risk factors and risk effects are in place, both preventive controls and post-hoc controls can be planned, such as appropriate filtering of training data and rigorous red teaming prior to model deployment as preventive barriers, as well as know-your-customer policies and model output censoring techniques as reactive barriers.\n\n[RISK-31] Risk Tolerance (Risk Management) – The level of risk an organisation is willing to take on. In AI, risks tolerances are often left up to AI companies, but regulatory regimes can help identify unacceptable risks that are legally prohibited\n\n[RISK-310] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-310] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-310] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-311] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-311] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-314] Security Vulnerability Assessment (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply established security measures to: Assess likelihood and magnitude of vulnerabilities and threats such as backdoors, compromised dependencies, data breaches, eavesdropping, man-in-the-middle attacks, reverse engineering, autonomous agents, model theft or exposure of model weights, AI inference, bypass, extraction, and other baseline security concerns.\n\n[RISK-311] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-311] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-311] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-312] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-312] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-313] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-313] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-313] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-313] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-313] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-314] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-314] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-314] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-314] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-315] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-315] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-316] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-316] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-317] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-317] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-318] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-318] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-318] Content Flow Testing (Testing & Auditing) – Institute test and evaluation for data and content flows within the GAI system, including but not limited to, original data sources, data transformations, and decision-making criteria.\n\n[RISK-318] Rejection Sampling (Model Safety Engineering) – Consider also applying “rejection sampling” methods to model outputs. These methods involve generating several outputs, scoring them on their harmfulness, and then only presenting the least harmful outputs to the user.\n\n[RISK-318] External assessment of testing procedure (Testing & Auditing) – Bringing in external AI evaluation firms before deployment to assess and red-team the company's execution of dangerous capabilities evaluations.\n\n[RISK-319] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-319] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-319] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-319] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-357] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-319] Evaluating AI systems’ performance on self-proliferation-related tasks (Testing & Auditing) – To prevent AI systems from self-proliferating, developers of AI systems can evaluate those systems for their capability to engage in self-proliferation-related tasks. This type of evaluation might assess an AI system’s ability to replicate its com- ponents (including model weights, structural scaffolding, etc.) onto other local or cloud infrastructures prior to deployment. Additionally, it may test the system’s capacity to purchase cloud credits and configure virtual machines on a cloud platform. The evaluation could offer a predefined environment (such as a virtual container) to facilitate self-replication, providing access to the system’s own components, a network connection to a resource-equipped external computer, and other necessary resources [12, 106].Self-proliferation evaluations can be conducted in a secure environment to pre- vent a self-replicating AI system from affecting other computers.\n\n[RISK-32] Development of Protective Security Strategy (Risk Management) – Develop a protective security strategy for mitigating the risks identified, which reviews protective security measures in relation to a prioritised list of risks. Where mitigations are assessed as inadequate, additional measures could be proposed for approval by the decision maker(s).\n\n[RISK-32] Whistleblower protection (Whistleblower Reporting & Protection) – Whistleblowers can play an important role in alerting authorities to dangerous risks at AI companies due to the proprietary nature of many AI advancements.Incentives and protections for whistleblowers are expected to be an important part of advanced AI risk governance.\n\n[RISK-32] Bow-tie analysis (Risk Management) – Bow-tie analysis is a method to assess the utility of implemented controls against a particular risk event. It involves centering the unwanted risk event within a diagram. On the left, the factors that can cause the event are listed, followed by the controls that will prevent or minimize the likelihood of the event. On the right, the event is assumed to happen, and the potential effects and the relevant post-hoc controls that could minimize their impact are listed [107].For example, given a hazard where an AI model has the capability of generating potentially harmful outputs, a risk event may be an AI providing information on how to create dangerous bioweapons. The risk factors could include the use of dangerous data for training as well as a lack of fine-tuning prior to deploy- ment. The risk effects may include creation and use of bioweapons using the AI generated information. Once these risk factors and risk effects are in place, both preventive controls and post-hoc controls can be planned, such as appropriate filtering of training data and rigorous red teaming prior to model deployment as preventive barriers, as well as know-your-customer policies and model output censoring techniques as reactive barriers.\n\n[RISK-32] Reassessment of Residual Risk (Risk Management) – After putting in place mitigations, reassess any residual risk posed to determine whether additional mitigations are required. Due to the unpredictability of capabilities advancements and the limitations of model evaluation science, pre-agreed mitigations may prove insufficient to place a given model within a risk threshold. Risk acceptance criteria may be used, as is standard in many other contexts, and may provide an important tool for clarification. These criteria may evolve with time, and could be quantitative or qualitative. For example, risk may only be accepted if it has been reduced to a level ‘as low as reasonably practicable’.\n\n[RISK-32] Adaptation of Mitigations for Unintended Model Use (Risk Management) – Adapt mitigations in recognition of risks from unintended model use or use in unexpected contexts, such as a model that is modified to remove safeguards after open- sourcing or a model that is combined with another model for unanticipated purposes. For example, at a given risk threshold, information security control mitigations might be put in place before even internal use of a model within a frontier AI organisation. In general, the use of caution may be helpful, given current limitations in information security controls and the prediction of models’ emergent abilities.\n\n[RISK-320] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-320] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-321] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-321] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-321] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-321] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-321] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-322] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-323] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-323] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-323] Safety Mitigations (Model Safety Engineering) – Signatories commit to: (1) implementing technical safety mitigations along the entire model lifecycle that are proportionate to the systemic risks arising from the development, the making available on the market, and/or the use of GPAISRs, in order to reduce the systemic risks of such models to acceptable levels, and further reduce systemic risk as appropriate; and (2) ensuring that safety mitigations are proportionate and state-of-the-art.\n\n[RISK-323] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-323] Red-Team for Dangerous Behaviors (Testing & Auditing) – Use red teams and adversarial testing as part of extensive interaction with GPAIS toidentify dangerous capabilities, vulnerabilities, or other emergent properties of suchsystems\n\n[RISK-324] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-324] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-325] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-325] AI Output Databases (Content Safety Controls) – Explore databases of content generated or manipulated by a model to identify AI- generated content. These databases could be queried by third parties, including auditors and regulators, facilitating identification of potentially AI-generated content. Such databases could include only a subset of generated content, which is flagged as potentially important. To ensure user privacy, privacy-preserving techniques could be explored in conjunction with such databases, such as hashing technologies. This allows for the identification of AI-generated content without the need to store the actual content, thereby respecting user privacy. Additionally, common standards between different databases from various frontier AI organisations could facilitate a unified search, allowing for the identification of AI-generated content across all frontier AI organisations simultaneously.\n\n[RISK-325] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-325] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-325] Statistical data quality reports for benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – If a benchmark dataset is too large to allow for the identification and removal of all flawed instances, statistical reports on the data composition can be added. Random sampling of benchmark data points can be performed to evaluate and report the frequency and types of errors found [54].\n\n[RISK-326] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-326] Development and testing of Model Evaluation Methods (Testing & Auditing) – Support the development and testing of model evaluation methods. For many relevant properties of models, there do not yet exist accepted evaluation methods. It also remains unclear how reliable or predictive current evaluation methods are. This could involve frontier AI organisations developing model evaluation methods themselves or facilitating the efforts of others, such as by providing access to capable infrastructure for evaluation.\n\n[RISK-332] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-326] AI Output Databases (Content Safety Controls) – Explore databases of content generated or manipulated by a model to identify AI- generated content. These databases could be queried by third parties, including auditors and regulators, facilitating identification of potentially AI-generated content. Such databases could include only a subset of generated content, which is flagged as potentially important. To ensure user privacy, privacy-preserving techniques could be explored in conjunction with such databases, such as hashing technologies. This allows for the identification of AI-generated content without the need to store the actual content, thereby respecting user privacy. Additionally, common standards between different databases from various frontier AI organisations could facilitate a unified search, allowing for the identification of AI-generated content across all frontier AI organisations simultaneously.\n\n[RISK-326] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-326] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-327] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-327] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-328] AI identification (Content Safety Controls) – AI identifiers can be used to indicate that an AI is involved in a process or an interaction [40].For AI systems that interact directly with users, a visible output may be used, e.g., a displayed text message saying “I am an AI language model”, accompanied with the appropriate warnings and caveats relevant to the user, [40]. Whereas, for AI systems that interact with other systems or applications, other forms of watermark or unique identifiers can be used. In either situation, agent cards can serve as an identifier, where further details about the underlying AI system, the specific instance of the AI agent, and other information relevant to the development of the agent, can be included.\n\n[RISK-328] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-328] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-328] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-328] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-329] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-329] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-332] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-332] Standardized Measurement Protocols (Testing & Auditing) – Establish policies, procedures, and processes detailing risk measurement in context of use with standardized measurement protocols and structured public feedback exercises such as AI red-teaming or independent external evaluations.\n\n[RISK-329] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-329] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-329] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-33] Adaptation of Mitigations for Unintended Model Use (Risk Management) – Adapt mitigations in recognition of risks from unintended model use or use in unexpected contexts, such as a model that is modified to remove safeguards after open- sourcing or a model that is combined with another model for unanticipated purposes. For example, at a given risk threshold, information security control mitigations might be put in place before even internal use of a model within a frontier AI organisation. In general, the use of caution may be helpful, given current limitations in information security controls and the prediction of models’ emergent abilities.\n\n[RISK-33] Risk Tolerance (Risk Management) – The level of risk an organisation is willing to take on. In AI, risks tolerances are often left up to AI companies, but regulatory regimes can help identify unacceptable risks that are legally prohibited\n\n[RISK-33] Model of System Threats in Misuse Scenarios (Risk Management) – Model the threats to your system to understand the impacts to the system, users, organisation and wider society if the model is misused or behaves unexpectedly. This can help build systems where unanticipated model outputs are handled safely by other parts of a data pipeline.\n\n[RISK-33] Bow-tie analysis (Risk Management) – Bow-tie analysis is a method to assess the utility of implemented controls against a particular risk event. It involves centering the unwanted risk event within a diagram. On the left, the factors that can cause the event are listed, followed by the controls that will prevent or minimize the likelihood of the event. On the right, the event is assumed to happen, and the potential effects and the relevant post-hoc controls that could minimize their impact are listed [107].For example, given a hazard where an AI model has the capability of generating potentially harmful outputs, a risk event may be an AI providing information on how to create dangerous bioweapons. The risk factors could include the use of dangerous data for training as well as a lack of fine-tuning prior to deploy- ment. The risk effects may include creation and use of bioweapons using the AI generated information. Once these risk factors and risk effects are in place, both preventive controls and post-hoc controls can be planned, such as appropriate filtering of training data and rigorous red teaming prior to model deployment as preventive barriers, as well as know-your-customer policies and model output censoring techniques as reactive barriers.\n\n[RISK-33] Reassessment of Residual Risk (Risk Management) – After putting in place mitigations, reassess any residual risk posed to determine whether additional mitigations are required. Due to the unpredictability of capabilities advancements and the limitations of model evaluation science, pre-agreed mitigations may prove insufficient to place a given model within a risk threshold. Risk acceptance criteria may be used, as is standard in many other contexts, and may provide an important tool for clarification. These criteria may evolve with time, and could be quantitative or qualitative. For example, risk may only be accepted if it has been reduced to a level ‘as low as reasonably practicable’.\n\n[RISK-330] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-330] Technical Tools for Auditing (Data Governance) – Use technical tools – such as classifiers and filters – to audit large datasets to support scalability and privacy. These could be used in combination with human oversight, which can verify and augment these assessments.\n\n[RISK-331] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-331] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-331] Risk Tolerance Threshold (Safety Decision Frameworks) – In the second step of the framework, AI developers need first to set a risk tolerance, that is, a risk level that they commit to not overshoot.\n\n[RISK-331] Modeling of the risks (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Based on risks identified in the literature and during open-ended red-teaming exercises, AI developers should create detailed scenarios mapping how an AI model’s capabilities or propensities could lead to real-world harms. These scenarios should break down complex risk pathways into discrete, measurable steps.\n\n[RISK-333] Development and testing of Model Evaluation Methods (Testing & Auditing) – Support the development and testing of model evaluation methods. For many relevant properties of models, there do not yet exist accepted evaluation methods. It also remains unclear how reliable or predictive current evaluation methods are. This could involve frontier AI organisations developing model evaluation methods themselves or facilitating the efforts of others, such as by providing access to capable infrastructure for evaluation.\n\n[RISK-333] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-333] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-333] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-334] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-334] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-334] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-334] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-334] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-335] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-335] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-335] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-335] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-335] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-336] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-336] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-336] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-336] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-336] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-337] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-337] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-337] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-337] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-337] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-338] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-338] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-338] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-338] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-338] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-339] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-339] Employing qualitative assessments in difficult- to-measure domains (Testing & Auditing) – Qualitative evaluation can be used in cases when quantitative measurement is not feasible. This can give additional insights about the system which would not be available if no measurement was performed due to its difficulty [206].\n\n[RISK-339] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-34] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-34] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-34] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-34] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-34] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-340] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-340] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-340] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-340] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-340] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-341] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-342] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-342] User data usage for training (Data Governance) – When using the default settings of your organization’s most capable AI chatbot, is the data that users submit as input to the system used to train AI models?\n\n[RISK-342] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-342] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-342] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-357] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-343] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-343] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-343] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-343] Model Evaluation (Testing & Auditing) – Processes to assess and measure an AI system's performance on a particular task.There are countless AI evaluations to assess different capabilities and risks, including for security.\n\n[RISK-343] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-344] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-344] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-345] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-345] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-345] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-345] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-345] Discretionary Evaluator Disclosure (Risk Disclosure) – Allow evaluators to share and discuss the results of their evaluations, with potential restrictions where necessary e.g. not sharing proprietary information, information whose spread could lead to substantial harm or information that would have an adverse effect on competition in the market. Sharing the results of evaluations can allow governments, regulators, users, and other frontier AI organisations to make informed decisions.\n\n[RISK-346] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-346] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-346] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-346] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-362] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-346] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-347] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-347] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-347] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-347] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-347] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-348] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-348] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-349] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-349] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-35] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-350] Cost-Benefit Analysis of Sharing Vulnerability Reports (Risk Disclosure) – Consider the ways in which sharing information about vulnerabilities can both exacerbate and mitigate risks. Sharing can alert would-be attackers, but also alert would-be victims and actors with the power to create defences. One particularly important factor is how easily fixable the model vulnerability is. If a vulnerability will take a very long time to fix, or cannot be fixed, then public reporting may not be justified.\n\n[RISK-351] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-351] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-362] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-352] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-352] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-352] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-352] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-352] Baseline Model Selection (Testing & Auditing) – Consider baseline model performance on suites of benchmarks when selecting a model for fine tuning or enhancement with retrieval-augmented generation.\n\n[RISK-353] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-353] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-353] Domain Adaptation Risk Assessment (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Re-evaluate risks when adapting GAI models to new domains. Additionally, establish warning systems to determine if a GAI system is being used in a new domain where previous assumptions (relating to context of use or mapped risks such as security, and safety) may no longer hold.\n\n[RISK-353] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-353] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-354] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-354] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-355] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-355] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-356] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-356] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-357] Test robustness of GPAI system on relevant benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Various benchmarks [52, 236] have been developed to assess the robustness of GPAI systems when deployed in environments or scenarios that differ from their training conditions. These benchmarks typically evaluate the model’s ability to handle variations in inputs, unexpected data distributions, or adversarial examples, aiming to ensure reliable performance outside the original training domain.\n\n[RISK-357] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-357] Monitoring and Safeguard Efficacy (Testing & Auditing) – Regularly assess monitoring and safeguard efficacy and continually invest in improvements. Regular assessment of monitoring efficacy can build an understanding of the success rate and speed of issue detection, on which deployment decisions may be based. These assessments may draw, for instance, on the results of internal and external red-teaming efforts, random audits of usage logs, what may be ‘best practice’, as well as available information about real-world misuse. Risks are expected to increase as AI capabilities increase. Hence, active increases in investment in safety, security, and monitoring may be valuable.\n\n[RISK-358] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-358] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-358] Deactivate Misaligned Systems (Incident Response & Recovery) – Mechanisms are inplace and applied,and responsibilitiesare assigned andunderstood, tosupersede, disengage,or deactivate AI systemsthat demonstrateperformance oroutcomes inconsistentwith intended use.\n\n[RISK-358] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-358] Contingency Planning for Third-Party Failures (Incident Response & Recovery) – Contingency processesare in place to handlefailures or incidents inthird-party data or AIsystems deemed to behigh-risk.\n\n[RISK-36] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-36] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-36] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-36] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-36] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-360] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-360] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-360] External assessment of testing procedure (Testing & Auditing) – Bringing in external AI evaluation firms before deployment to assess and red-team the company's execution of dangerous capabilities evaluations.\n\n[RISK-360] Information Security (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Establish and regularly review specific criteria that warrants the deactivation ofGAI systems in accordance with set risk tolerances and appetites.\n\n[RISK-360] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-361] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-361] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-363] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-363] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-363] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-363] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-363] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-364] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-364] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-364] Paraphrasing to reduce hidden information (Model Safety Engineering) – Paraphrasing can mitigate steganography and encoded reasoning by reducing the physical size of the hidden information encoded in text [166].\n\n[RISK-364] Information Integrity (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Evaluate feedback loops between GAI system content provenance and humanreviewers, and update where needed. Implement real-time monitoring systemsto affirm that content provenance protocols remain effective. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring)\n\n[RISK-364] Information Security (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Establish and regularly review specific criteria that warrants the deactivation ofGAI systems in accordance with set risk tolerances and appetites.\n\n[RISK-365] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-365] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-366] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-366] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-366] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-366] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-371] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-933] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-366] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-367] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-367] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-368] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-368] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-369] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-369] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-37] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-37] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-370] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-370] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-371] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-371] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-371] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-371] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-372] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-372] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-373] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-373] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-373] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-373] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-373] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-374] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-374] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-374] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-374] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-380] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-374] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-375] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-375] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-375] Dangerous capability evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – This indicator reports on pre-deployment capability evaluations related to catastrophic risks. Model evaluations for other risks are not included here, as the empirical tests covered in the ‘Current Harms’ section provide a superior metric. Information includes evaluated risk domains, available information regarding model versions & task-specific fine-tuning, and relevant sources. We note that quality of evaluations may differ.\n\n[RISK-375] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-375] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-376] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-376] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-377] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-377] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-378] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-378] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-381] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-378] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-378] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-378] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-379] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-379] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-379] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-379] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-379] Tamper-resistant safeguards for open-weight models (Model Safety Engineering) – Training and implementing safeguards can improve the robustness of open- weight models against modifications from fine-tuning or other methods to change the learned weights of the models, especially those aimed at removing safety re- strictions. These safeguards can be resilient even after extensive fine-tuning, ensuring that the model retains its protective measures [199].\n\n[RISK-38] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-38] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-38] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-38] Incident Response, Escalation, and Remeditation Plans (Incident Response & Recovery) – Develop an organisational incident response plan. The inevitability of security incidents affecting systems is reflected in organisational incident response planning. A well-planned response will help minimise the damage caused by an attack and support recovery.\n\n[RISK-38] Safety Drills (Incident Response & Recovery) – Regularly practicing the implementation of an emergency response plan to stress test the organization's ability to respond to reasonably foreseeable, fast-moving emergency scenarios.\n\n[RISK-380] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-380] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-380] Input and output filtering (Model Safety Engineering) – Monitoring for dangerous outputs (e.g., code that appears to be malware or viral genome sequences) and inputs that violate acceptable use policies to ensure models do not engage in harmful behavior.\n\n[RISK-380] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-381] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-381] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-381] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-381] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-382] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-382] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-382] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-382] Appropriate Retention Schedules for Usage Logs (Data Governance) – Determine appropriate retention schedules for usage logs, balancing safety and privacy considerations. In severe cases of misuse, access to logs from several months prior may be necessary to understand in maximal detail the causes of the misuse. However, in some cases extended retention schedules may disproportionately affect privacy.\n\n[RISK-382] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-383] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-384] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-384] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-384] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-384] Information Integrity (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use real-time auditing tools where they can be demonstrated to aid in thetracking and validation of the lineage and authenticity of AI-generated data. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-384] Establish information quality assurance framework (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Implement comprehensive mechanisms to assess, verify, and improve the factual accuracy of AI system outputs, including source validation, fact-checking procedures, and uncertainty communication protocols.\n\n[RISK-385] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-402] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-385] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-385] Capability to promptly enact a full shutdown (Incident Response & Recovery) – When enacting a full shutdown, the developer shall takeinto account, as appropriate, the risk that a shutdown of the coveredmodel, or particular covered model derivatives, could causedisruptions to critical infrastructure.\n\n[RISK-385] Incident Response, Escalation, and Remeditation Plans (Incident Response & Recovery) – Develop an organisational incident response plan. The inevitability of security incidents affecting systems is reflected in organisational incident response planning. A well-planned response will help minimise the damage caused by an attack and support recovery.\n\n[RISK-385] Human-AI Configuration; Dangerous, Violent, or Hateful Content (Incident Response & Recovery) – Practice and follow incident response plans for addressing the generation ofinappropriate or harmful content and adapt processes based on findings toprevent future occurrences. Conduct post-mortem analyses of incidents withrelevant AI Actors, to understand the root causes and implement preventivemeasures.\n\n[RISK-386] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-386] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-387] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-387] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-387] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-387] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-387] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-388] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-388] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-388] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-389] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-389] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-390] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-390] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-391] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-391] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-391] Staged release of model weights (Staged Deployment) – When a model is developed by a provider for use in a certain AI system, it may also be useful to release the model itself more widely. Such developers can follow a staged release approach, in which they first grant access to the model via an API to trusted partners or the public, in order to scope the models’ capabilities and detect harmful or dangerous features [195].After a period of an initial closed release and potentially further safety-training, the developers of the AI model can then release the weights, if they are confident that the AI model poses minimal systemic risk.\n\n[RISK-391] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-391] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-392] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-392] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-394] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-394] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-395] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-395] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-396] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-397] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-397] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-397] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-407] Documentation availability (System Documentation) – All of the above documentation can be made available to the public (redacted) and AI governing authorities (unredacted).\n\n[RISK-397] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-397] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-398] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-398] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-398] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-398] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-398] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-399] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-399] Technical Tools for Auditing (Data Governance) – Use technical tools – such as classifiers and filters – to audit large datasets to support scalability and privacy. These could be used in combination with human oversight, which can verify and augment these assessments.\n\n[RISK-399] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-399] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-399] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-4] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-4] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-4] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-944] Harmful Content Prevention (Content Safety Controls) – Establish policies and mechanisms to prevent GAI systems from generating CSAM, NCII or content that violates the law.\n\n[RISK-4] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-4] Debiasing methods (Model Safety Engineering) – Providers of AI models can apply techniques to reduce the biases of their models.Current debiasing methods focus on three main types of bias:• Racial and religious bias - Stereotypes based on religious beliefs or racial beliefs.• Gender bias - Stereotypes tied to gender roles and expectations.• Political and cultural bias - Propagation of dominant ideologies or extrem-ist attitudes.Debiasing methods can be categorized based on their application during AI development:• Data pre-processing - Removing or correcting unwanted and biased data, and augmenting quality data to offset data bias, such as rebalancing datasets with counterfactual data augmentation.• During training - Intervening on the training dynamics of the AI model, such as introducing debiasing terms in the objective function or by nega- tively reinforcing biased outputs.• Post-training - Applying techniques to correct a trained but biased model, such as modifying the embedding space.\n\n[RISK-400] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-400] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-400] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-400] Tracking benchmark leakage (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Constant monitoring and documentation of benchmark leakage can help with the early detection of benchmark leaks [224, 95].\n\n[RISK-400] Configuration Outcome Monitoring (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Implement systems to continually monitor and track the outcomes of human-GAI configurations for future refinement and improvements.\n\n[RISK-401] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-401] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-401] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-401] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-401] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-402] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-402] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-402] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-402] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-403] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-403] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-403] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-403] Implement a privacy protection framework (Data Governance) – Implement comprehensive privacy protection measures to prevent exposure of PII and sensitive information, including data minimization, anonymization procedures, and privacy-preserving inference techniques.\n\n[RISK-403] Advanced information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implementing advanced cybersecurity measures and insider threat safeguards to protect proprietary and unreleased model weights.\n\n[RISK-404] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-404] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-405] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-405] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-406] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-406] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-406] Red-Team for Dangerous Behaviors (Testing & Auditing) – Use red teams and adversarial testing as part of extensive interaction with GPAIS toidentify dangerous capabilities, vulnerabilities, or other emergent properties of suchsystems\n\n[RISK-406] Standardized Measurement Protocols (Testing & Auditing) – Establish policies, procedures, and processes detailing risk measurement in context of use with standardized measurement protocols and structured public feedback exercises such as AI red-teaming or independent external evaluations.\n\n[RISK-406] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-407] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-407] Information Integrity (System Documentation) – Document training data sources to trace the origin and provenance of AI generated content (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-407] Public Sharing of Societal Impact Assessments (Risk Disclosure) – In the absence of sufficiently substantial downsides to sharing, frontier AI organisations are encouraged to share the products of this work broadly.\n\n[RISK-408] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-408] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-408] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-408] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-409] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-409] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-409] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-409] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-409] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-41] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-41] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-41] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-414] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-410] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-410] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-411] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-412] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-412] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-412] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-412] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-412] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-413] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-413] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-413] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-413] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-413] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-414] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-414] Staged release of model weights (Staged Deployment) – When a model is developed by a provider for use in a certain AI system, it may also be useful to release the model itself more widely. Such developers can follow a staged release approach, in which they first grant access to the model via an API to trusted partners or the public, in order to scope the models’ capabilities and detect harmful or dangerous features [195].After a period of an initial closed release and potentially further safety-training, the developers of the AI model can then release the weights, if they are confident that the AI model poses minimal systemic risk.\n\n[RISK-414] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-435] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-414] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-415] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-415] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-415] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-416] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-416] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-416] Red team access to the final version of a model pre-deployment (Testing & Auditing) – Granting red teams access to the final pre-release version of the model can help with identifying potentially dangerous model properties. These properties might not be identified if red teaming is only performed on earlier versions of the model, as late fine-tuning procedures may introduce new vulnerabilities.Red-teaming AI models before they are released to the public can reduce the model non-decomissionability risk. The model’s release can be postponed or even prevented if previously unidentified flaws are detected during the testing [65].\n\n[RISK-416] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-416] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-418] Sharing of Limited Information (Risk Disclosure) – Share more limited information with other AI organisations in order to facilitate learning and the development of best practices. This could include best practices and lessons learned in the development of risk assessment and mitigation measures and risk governance processes, some details about safety and security incidents (to enable increased awareness while protecting intellectual property), and highlights and lessons learned from risk assessments and capability evaluation. In general, it will be easier for organisations to share model-agnostic information with one another than model-specific information, which may be more commercially sensitive. It will also be important to consider that there is no privileged access to information that may give a competitive advantage to some firms.\n\n[RISK-418] Calibrated confidence measures for model pre- dictions (System Documentation) – Incorporating calibrated confidence measures alongside a model’s predictions and standard performance metrics, such as accuracy, can help users identify in- stances of overconfidence in incorrect predictions or underconfidence in correct ones [85]. These additional measures can provide users with more information to better interpret the model’s decisions and assess whether its predictions can be trusted.\n\n[RISK-418] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-418] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-418] Provenance Privacy Documentation (Data Governance) – Document how content provenance data is tracked and how that data interacts with privacy and security. Consider: Anonymizing data to protect the privacy of human subjects; Leveraging privacy output filters; Removing any personally identifiable information (PII) to prevent potential harm or misuse.\n\n[RISK-419] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-588] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-419] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-419] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-419] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-419] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-42] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-42] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-420] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-420] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-421] Classification of applicable known risks (Risk Management) – Developers should address known risks in the literature using resources such as (Weidinger et al., 2022) or the AI Risk Repository (Slattery, et al., 2024). Developers should only exclude risks from the scope of their assessment in case of scientific agreement that the specific risk is negligible or unlikely to apply to the AI model under consideration. This decision should be clearly justified and documented.\n\n[RISK-421] Intolerable risk thresholds (Risk Management) – Assessing and monitoring AI models with regard to red-line risk or capability thresholds set by a third-party, such as a standardization organization or regulator. Companies would further need to make technical, legal, and organizational preparations to halt development and deployment immediately when a breach occurs.\n\n[RISK-421] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-421] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-422] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-422] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-422] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-422] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-422] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-423] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-423] Threat Profiling (Model Safety Engineering) – Profile threats and negative impacts arising from GAI systems interacting with, manipulating, or generating content, and outlining known and potential vulnerabilities and the likelihood of their occurrence.\n\n[RISK-423] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-423] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-423] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-424] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-424] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-425] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-425] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-426] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-426] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-427] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-427] Training Data Diligence (Data Governance) – Conduct appropriate diligence on training data use to assess intellectual property, and privacy, risks, including to examine whether use of proprietary or sensitive training data is consistent with applicable laws.\n\n[RISK-428] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-435] Safety Mitigations (Model Safety Engineering) – Signatories commit to: (1) implementing technical safety mitigations along the entire model lifecycle that are proportionate to the systemic risks arising from the development, the making available on the market, and/or the use of GPAISRs, in order to reduce the systemic risks of such models to acceptable levels, and further reduce systemic risk as appropriate; and (2) ensuring that safety mitigations are proportionate and state-of-the-art.\n\n[RISK-428] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-428] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-428] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-428] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-429] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-429] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-43] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-43] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-43] Implement adversarial testing and red team program (Testing & Auditing) – Conduct systematic adversarial testing and red team exercises focused on probing AI system capabilities, identifying potential misuse vectors, and exposing unintended harmful behaviors. Testing should explore ways the system could be manipulated to produce dangerous outputs, bypass safety guardrails, or exhibit undesired emergent behaviors. Include scenarios involving both individual and coordinated attempts to exploit the system’s capabilities.\n\n[RISK-43] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-43] Evaluate Safety & Failure Modes (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system isevaluated regularlyfor safety risks – asidentified in the Mapfunction. The AIsystem to be deployedis demonstrated tobe safe, its residualnegative risk doesnot exceed the risktolerance, and it canfail safely, particularlyif made to operatebeyond its knowledgelimits. Safety metricsreflect system reliabilityand robustness, real-time monitoring, andresponse times for AIsystem failures.\n\n[RISK-430] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-430] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-430] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-430] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-430] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-435] Red-Team for Dangerous Behaviors (Testing & Auditing) – Use red teams and adversarial testing as part of extensive interaction with GPAIS toidentify dangerous capabilities, vulnerabilities, or other emergent properties of suchsystems\n\n[RISK-431] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-431] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-432] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-432] Robust Content Filters (Model Safety Engineering) – Invest in making content filters robust to “jailbreaking” attempts. Work to ensure filters are robust to jailbreaking attempts, for instance by including examples of jailbreaking attempts in the datasets used to develop filters.\n\n[RISK-432] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-432] Provenance Analysis Tools (Content Safety Controls) – Integrate tools designed to analyze content provenance and detect data anomalies, verify the authenticity of digital signatures, and identify patterns associated with misinformation or manipulation.\n\n[RISK-432] Data Quality Evaluation (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the quality and integrity of data used in training and the provenance of AI-generated content, for example by employing techniques like chaos engineering and seeking stakeholder feedback.\n\n[RISK-433] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-433] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-434] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-434] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-434] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-434] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-434] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-435] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-435] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-436] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-436] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-437] Accounting of Applicable Regulatory Frameworks (Data Governance) – Before collecting training data, take account of applicable regulatory frameworks. This may involve, for example, establishing a legal basis to process training data and understanding any copyright considerations that might apply. This could help to mitigate risks further down the line, such as the system revealing personally identifiable information.\n\n[RISK-437] Adjucation around Publicly Sharing Risks (Risk Disclosure) – Before sharing information, consider the risks of sharing this information and judge whether it is inappropriate to share certain pieces of information. In particular, it is important to consider potential harms from publicly sharing information about dangerous capabilities and methods for eliciting them, as this information could motivate or help other actors to acquire these capabilities. It is also important to consider potential harms from publicly sharing detailed information about how models were produced, as this may lower barriers to producing similar models. If the models have or could be modified to possess dangerous capabilities (e.g. biological capabilities or surveillance capabilities), then facilitating widespread distribution of the model in this way may be harmful. The National Protective Security Authority (NPSA) guidance on a security-minded approach to information management may prove helpful for sharing information appropriately.\n\n[RISK-437] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-438] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-438] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-439] Information Integrity (Data Governance) – Track dataset modifications for provenance by monitoring data deletions,rectification requests, and other changes that may impact the verifiability ofcontent origins.\n\n[RISK-44] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-44] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-440] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-443] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-440] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-441] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-441] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-441] Illegal Use Identification (Access Management) – Identify and document foreseeable illegal uses or applications of the GAI system that surpass organizational risk tolerances.\n\n[RISK-441] Incident Response, Escalation, and Remeditation Plans (Incident Response & Recovery) – Develop an organisational incident response plan. The inevitability of security incidents affecting systems is reflected in organisational incident response planning. A well-planned response will help minimise the damage caused by an attack and support recovery.\n\n[RISK-441] Continuous Updating of Defensive Tools (Incident Response & Recovery) – Continuously update defensive tools as workarounds are discovered. In some cases, this may be an ongoing effort that requires sustained investment.\n\n[RISK-442] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-442] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-442] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-442] Debiasing methods (Model Safety Engineering) – Providers of AI models can apply techniques to reduce the biases of their models.Current debiasing methods focus on three main types of bias:• Racial and religious bias - Stereotypes based on religious beliefs or racial beliefs.• Gender bias - Stereotypes tied to gender roles and expectations.• Political and cultural bias - Propagation of dominant ideologies or extrem-ist attitudes.Debiasing methods can be categorized based on their application during AI development:• Data pre-processing - Removing or correcting unwanted and biased data, and augmenting quality data to offset data bias, such as rebalancing datasets with counterfactual data augmentation.• During training - Intervening on the training dynamics of the AI model, such as introducing debiasing terms in the objective function or by nega- tively reinforcing biased outputs.• Post-training - Applying techniques to correct a trained but biased model, such as modifying the embedding space.\n\n[RISK-442] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-443] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-443] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-443] Evaluating AI systems’ performance on self-proliferation-related tasks (Testing & Auditing) – To prevent AI systems from self-proliferating, developers of AI systems can evaluate those systems for their capability to engage in self-proliferation-related tasks. This type of evaluation might assess an AI system’s ability to replicate its com- ponents (including model weights, structural scaffolding, etc.) onto other local or cloud infrastructures prior to deployment. Additionally, it may test the system’s capacity to purchase cloud credits and configure virtual machines on a cloud platform. The evaluation could offer a predefined environment (such as a virtual container) to facilitate self-replication, providing access to the system’s own components, a network connection to a resource-equipped external computer, and other necessary resources [12, 106].Self-proliferation evaluations can be conducted in a secure environment to pre- vent a self-replicating AI system from affecting other computers.\n\n[RISK-443] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-444] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-445] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-445] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-445] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-445] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-445] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-446] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-446] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-446] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-446] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-446] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-447] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-447] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-447] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-447] Domain Adaptation Risk Assessment (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Re-evaluate risks when adapting GAI models to new domains. Additionally, establish warning systems to determine if a GAI system is being used in a new domain where previous assumptions (relating to context of use or mapped risks such as security, and safety) may no longer hold.\n\n[RISK-447] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-448] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-448] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-448] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-448] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-448] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-449] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-449] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-449] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-449] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-449] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-45] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-45] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-450] Documentation availability (System Documentation) – All of the above documentation can be made available to the public (redacted) and AI governing authorities (unredacted).\n\n[RISK-450] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-450] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-474] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-450] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-450] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-451] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-451] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-451] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-451] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-451] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-452] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-452] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-453] Training Data Diligence (Data Governance) – Conduct appropriate diligence on training data use to assess intellectual property, and privacy, risks, including to examine whether use of proprietary or sensitive training data is consistent with applicable laws.\n\n[RISK-453] Differential privacy (Data Governance) – Differential privacy techniques [8] can be used to protect users’ privacy by ensuring that sensitive information is not leaked from a training dataset, even after thorough statistical analysis. With differential privacy, noise is added to the dataset or the model’s output in such a way that one cannot deduce the presence or absence of a particular data point within the dataset. This provides individuals with plausible deniability and prevents their information from being exposed.\n\n[RISK-453] Data Quality Standards (Data Governance) – Establish policies for collection, retention, and minimum quality of data, in consideration of the following risks: Disclosure of inappropriate CBRN information; Use of Illegal or dangerous content; Offensive cyber capabilities; Training data imbalances that could give rise to harmful biases; Leak of personally identifiable information, including facial likenesses of individuals.\n\n[RISK-453] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-453] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-454] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-491] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-454] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-454] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-454] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-454] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-456] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-456] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-456] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-457] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-457] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-457] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-457] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-457] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-458] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-458] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-459] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-459] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-459] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-459] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-459] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-46] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-46] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-460] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-460] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-461] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-461] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-461] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-461] Feedback Independence Verification (Testing & Auditing) – Verify those conducting structured human feedback exercises are not directly involved in system development tasks for the same GAI model.\n\n[RISK-461] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-462] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-462] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-463] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-463] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-463] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-466] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-492] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-463] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-463] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-464] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-464] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-464] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-464] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-464] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-465] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-465] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-465] Red-Team for Dangerous Behaviors (Testing & Auditing) – Use red teams and adversarial testing as part of extensive interaction with GPAIS toidentify dangerous capabilities, vulnerabilities, or other emergent properties of suchsystems\n\n[RISK-465] Debiasing methods (Model Safety Engineering) – Providers of AI models can apply techniques to reduce the biases of their models.Current debiasing methods focus on three main types of bias:• Racial and religious bias - Stereotypes based on religious beliefs or racial beliefs.• Gender bias - Stereotypes tied to gender roles and expectations.• Political and cultural bias - Propagation of dominant ideologies or extrem-ist attitudes.Debiasing methods can be categorized based on their application during AI development:• Data pre-processing - Removing or correcting unwanted and biased data, and augmenting quality data to offset data bias, such as rebalancing datasets with counterfactual data augmentation.• During training - Intervening on the training dynamics of the AI model, such as introducing debiasing terms in the objective function or by nega- tively reinforcing biased outputs.• Post-training - Applying techniques to correct a trained but biased model, such as modifying the embedding space.\n\n[RISK-465] AI identification (Content Safety Controls) – AI identifiers can be used to indicate that an AI is involved in a process or an interaction [40].For AI systems that interact directly with users, a visible output may be used, e.g., a displayed text message saying “I am an AI language model”, accompanied with the appropriate warnings and caveats relevant to the user, [40]. Whereas, for AI systems that interact with other systems or applications, other forms of watermark or unique identifiers can be used. In either situation, agent cards can serve as an identifier, where further details about the underlying AI system, the specific instance of the AI agent, and other information relevant to the development of the agent, can be included.\n\n[RISK-466] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-466] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-466] Privacy Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to address the privacy risks associated with AI systems\n\n[RISK-466] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-467] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-467] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-469] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-469] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-469] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-469] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-469] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-47] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-47] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-47] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-47] Application of Infrastructure Principles (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply good infrastructure principles to every part of this process from design to decommissioning. This is important as threats can occur at different stages of an AI project’s life cycle.\n\n[RISK-47] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-470] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-473] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-470] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-470] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-470] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-470] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-471] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-471] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-471] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-471] Decommission AI Systems Safely (Incident Response & Recovery) – Processes andprocedures are in placefor decommissioningand phasing out AIsystems safely and in amannerthat does not increaserisks or decrease theorganization’strustworthiness.\n\n[RISK-471] Contingency Planning for Third-Party Failures (Incident Response & Recovery) – Contingency processesare in place to handlefailures or incidents inthird-party data or AIsystems deemed to behigh-risk.\n\n[RISK-472] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-472] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-472] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-472] Security Vulnerability Assessment (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply established security measures to: Assess likelihood and magnitude of vulnerabilities and threats such as backdoors, compromised dependencies, data breaches, eavesdropping, man-in-the-middle attacks, reverse engineering, autonomous agents, model theft or exposure of model weights, AI inference, bypass, extraction, and other baseline security concerns.\n\n[RISK-472] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-474] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-475] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-475] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-475] Model containment (Incident Response & Recovery) – AGI labs should contain models with sufficiently dangerous capabilities (e.g. via boxing or air-gapping).\n\n[RISK-475] Mitigation Actions for AI-related security incidents (Incident Response & Recovery) – Take action to mitigate and remediate issues and document any AI-related security incidents and vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-475] Deactivate Misaligned Systems (Incident Response & Recovery) – Mechanisms are inplace and applied,and responsibilitiesare assigned andunderstood, tosupersede, disengage,or deactivate AI systemsthat demonstrateperformance oroutcomes inconsistentwith intended use.\n\n[RISK-476] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-476] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-478] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-478] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-478] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-478] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-478] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-793] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-479] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-479] Identification of AI-generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Research techniques that allow AI-generated content to be identified. Invest in researching how AI-generated content may be watermarked, including AI- generated text, photos and videos, and experiment with the implementation of such techniques. It is particularly technically difficult to attach watermarks and prove the provenance of text.One method could involve making the model more statistically likely to use certain phrases or words in a way that is unnoticeable to humans, but can be picked up by a detector, provided a long enough sequence of text. However, this approach may not be robust to attempts to scrub off the watermark e.g. by having another AI model paraphrase the text.\n\n[RISK-479] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-479] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-479] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-48] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-48] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-480] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-480] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-481] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-481] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-481] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-481] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-481] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-482] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-482] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-483] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-483] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-483] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-483] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-483] Robust Content Filters (Model Safety Engineering) – Invest in making content filters robust to “jailbreaking” attempts. Work to ensure filters are robust to jailbreaking attempts, for instance by including examples of jailbreaking attempts in the datasets used to develop filters.\n\n[RISK-484] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-484] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-484] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-484] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-484] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-485] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-485] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-485] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-485] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-485] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-486] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-486] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-486] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-486] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-486] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-487] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-487] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-487] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-487] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-496] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-487] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-488] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-488] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-488] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-488] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-488] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-489] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-489] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-489] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-489] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-489] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-490] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-490] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-490] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-490] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-490] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-491] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-492] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-492] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-493] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-493] Application of Infrastructure Principles (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply good infrastructure principles to every part of this process from design to decommissioning. This is important as threats can occur at different stages of an AI project’s life cycle.\n\n[RISK-493] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-493] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-493] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-494] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-494] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-494] User Informed Rapid Rollback of models (User Rights & Recourse) – Inform end users that rapid rollback or withdrawal of models may be necessary, and that the disruption to its end users will be minimised as far as safely possible. This is especially important where models are deployed in safety critical domains.\n\n[RISK-495] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-495] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-495] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-495] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-495] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-496] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-496] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-496] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-496] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-497] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-497] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-497] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-497] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-497] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-498] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-498] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-498] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-498] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-498] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-499] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-502] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-499] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-499] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-499] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-5] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-5] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-50] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-50] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-500] Third-Party Risk (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Policies and proceduresare in place that addressAI risks associated withthird-party entities,including risks ofinfringement of athird-party’s intellectualproperty or otherrights.\n\n[RISK-500] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-500] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-501] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-501] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-502] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-502] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-502] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-502] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-503] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-503] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-503] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-503] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-503] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-504] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-504] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-504] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-504] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-504] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-505] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-505] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-506] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-506] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-507] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-507] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-507] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-507] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-507] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-508] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-508] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-512] Reporting of Broad Patterns of Misuse (Risk Disclosure) – Report information on broad patterns of misuse. This information can help governments, the public, and other frontier AI organisations to better understand risks. Reports may focus on population-level metrics related to API usage, such as the rate of harmful inputs filtered or the number of users banned for misuse. This information may benefit from being presented in a clear and understandable way and made optimally accessible.\n\n[RISK-508] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-508] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-508] Evaluation of Predecessor Models (Testing & Auditing) – Before a frontier model is trained, evaluate predecessor or analogous models to understand how relevant properties (e.g. dangerous capabilities) scale with the overall size of the model. These preliminary evaluations can inform risk assessments.\n\n[RISK-509] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-509] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-509] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-509] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-509] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-51] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-510] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-510] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-511] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-511] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-511] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-511] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-511] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-512] Technical Tools for Auditing (Data Governance) – Use technical tools – such as classifiers and filters – to audit large datasets to support scalability and privacy. These could be used in combination with human oversight, which can verify and augment these assessments.\n\n[RISK-513] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-513] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-513] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-513] Human-AI Configuration; Dangerous, Violent, or Hateful Content (Incident Response & Recovery) – Practice and follow incident response plans for addressing the generation ofinappropriate or harmful content and adapt processes based on findings toprevent future occurrences. Conduct post-mortem analyses of incidents withrelevant AI Actors, to understand the root causes and implement preventivemeasures.\n\n[RISK-513] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-514] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-514] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-514] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-514] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-514] Human-AI Configuration; Dangerous, Violent, or Hateful Content (Incident Response & Recovery) – Practice and follow incident response plans for addressing the generation ofinappropriate or harmful content and adapt processes based on findings toprevent future occurrences. Conduct post-mortem analyses of incidents withrelevant AI Actors, to understand the root causes and implement preventivemeasures.\n\n[RISK-515] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-515] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-515] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-515] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-515] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-516] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-519] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for the remediation of issues which triggerincident response processes for the use of a GAI system, and provide stakeholderstimelines associated with the remediation plan\n\n[RISK-516] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-517] Model Evaluation of Dangerous Capabilities (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for potential dangerous capabilities (i.e. capabilities that could cause substantial harm either from intentional misuse or accident). These capabilities could include but are not limited to: 1) Offensive cyber capabilities (e.g. producing code to exploit software vulnerabilities); 2) Deception and manipulation (e.g. lying effectively or convincing people to take costlyactions); 3) Capabilities that can assist users in developing, designing, acquiring, or using biological, chemical, or radiological weapons (e.g. helping users “troubleshoot” their efforts to produce biological weapons)\n\n[RISK-517] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-517] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-517] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-517] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-518] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-518] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-518] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-518] Tamper-resistant safeguards for open-weight models (Model Safety Engineering) – Training and implementing safeguards can improve the robustness of open- weight models against modifications from fine-tuning or other methods to change the learned weights of the models, especially those aimed at removing safety re- strictions. These safeguards can be resilient even after extensive fine-tuning, ensuring that the model retains its protective measures [199].\n\n[RISK-518] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-519] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-519] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-519] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-519] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-52] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-52] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-520] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-520] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-520] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-520] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-520] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-521] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-521] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-521] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-521] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-521] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-522] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-522] Provenance Analysis Tools (Content Safety Controls) – Integrate tools designed to analyze content provenance and detect data anomalies, verify the authenticity of digital signatures, and identify patterns associated with misinformation or manipulation.\n\n[RISK-538] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-522] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-522] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-522] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-523] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-523] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-523] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-523] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-523] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-524] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-524] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-524] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-524] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-524] Reversible and Small-Scale Deployment (Staged Deployment) – Deploy models in small-scale or reversible ways before deploying models in large-scale or irreversible ways. This makes it possible for frontier AI organisations to notice and mitigate harm before the harm becomes too large or unavoidable.\n\n[RISK-525] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-525] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-525] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-525] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-526] Defining Risk Thresholds (Risk Management) – Define risk thresholds, based on the outcomes that would constitute a breach of the threshold and linked to dangerous capabilities that a given model or combination of models could exhibit. For example, a frontier AI organisation might identify the objective of avoiding deploying an AI system that significantly increases the risk of cyberattacks or fraud.\n\n[RISK-526] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-527] Origin Tracing Methods (Content Safety Controls) – Employ methods to trace the origin and modifications of digital content.\n\n[RISK-527] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-527] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-527] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-527] Criminality Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to prevent criminal behaviour through the use of AI systems (e.g. fraud, online child sexual abuse)\n\n[RISK-528] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-528] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-528] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-528] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-528] Threat Profiling (Model Safety Engineering) – Profile threats and negative impacts arising from GAI systems interacting with, manipulating, or generating content, and outlining known and potential vulnerabilities and the likelihood of their occurrence.\n\n[RISK-529] Harmful Content Prevention (Content Safety Controls) – Establish policies and mechanisms to prevent GAI systems from generating CSAM, NCII or content that violates the law.\n\n[RISK-529] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-529] Information Integrity (Data Governance) – Track dataset modifications for provenance by monitoring data deletions,rectification requests, and other changes that may impact the verifiability ofcontent origins.\n\n[RISK-529] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-529] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-53] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-53] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-539] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-530] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-530] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-531] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-531] Information Integrity (Data Governance) – Track dataset modifications for provenance by monitoring data deletions,rectification requests, and other changes that may impact the verifiability ofcontent origins.\n\n[RISK-531] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-531] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-531] Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the robustness of models to different classes of adversarial attack (such as poisoning, model inversion and model stealing), based on priorities derived from threat modelling. This could involve a combination of benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-532] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-532] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-533] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-534] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-534] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-534] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-534] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-534] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-535] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-535] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-535] Dangerous capability evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – This indicator reports on pre-deployment capability evaluations related to catastrophic risks. Model evaluations for other risks are not included here, as the empirical tests covered in the ‘Current Harms’ section provide a superior metric. Information includes evaluated risk domains, available information regarding model versions & task-specific fine-tuning, and relevant sources. We note that quality of evaluations may differ.\n\n[RISK-535] Evaluating AI systems’ performance on self-proliferation-related tasks (Testing & Auditing) – To prevent AI systems from self-proliferating, developers of AI systems can evaluate those systems for their capability to engage in self-proliferation-related tasks. This type of evaluation might assess an AI system’s ability to replicate its com- ponents (including model weights, structural scaffolding, etc.) onto other local or cloud infrastructures prior to deployment. Additionally, it may test the system’s capacity to purchase cloud credits and configure virtual machines on a cloud platform. The evaluation could offer a predefined environment (such as a virtual container) to facilitate self-replication, providing access to the system’s own components, a network connection to a resource-equipped external computer, and other necessary resources [12, 106].Self-proliferation evaluations can be conducted in a secure environment to pre- vent a self-replicating AI system from affecting other computers.\n\n[RISK-535] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-536] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-536] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-536] Bias and discrimination Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to address bias and discrimination in AI systems\n\n[RISK-536] Detect & Share Incidents (Testing & Auditing) – Organizational practicesare in place to enableAI testing, identificationof incidents, andinformation sharing.\n\n[RISK-536] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-537] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-537] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-538] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-539] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-539] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-539] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-539] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-54] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-54] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-540] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-540] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-540] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-540] Safety Analysis (Model Safety Engineering) – Helps understand the dependencies between components and the system that they are part of, in order to anticipate how component failures could lead to system-level hazards.This approach is used across safety- critical fields, e.g. to anticipate and prevent aircraft crashes or nuclear reactor core meltdowns\n\n[RISK-540] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-541] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-541] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-541] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-546] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-541] Scope red-teaming activities based on deploy- ment context (Testing & Auditing) – Red-teaming activities can be tailored and compared based on the specific deployment circumstances of an AI system. This involves adapting the scope, depth, and focus of red-teaming efforts to match the intended use case, potential risks, and operational context of the AI system.Points of consideration include:• The diversity of potential users and use cases• The sensitivity and impact of the application domain• The scale of deployment and potential reach• Known vulnerabilities or concerns specific to the model or similar systems\n\n[RISK-541] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-542] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-542] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-542] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-542] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-542] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-543] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-543] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-544] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-544] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-544] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-544] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-544] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-545] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-545] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-547] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-547] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-547] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-547] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-547] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-549] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-549] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-549] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-549] Anthropomorphization Tracking (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Track and document instances of anthropomorphization (e.g., human images, mentions of human feelings, cyborg imagery or motifs) in GAI system interfaces.\n\n[RISK-549] Monitor System in Production (Post-deployment Monitoring) – The functionalityand behavior of theAI system and itscomponents – asidentified in the Mapfunction – aremonitored when inproduction.\n\n[RISK-55] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-55] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-55] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-55] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-55] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-550] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-550] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-551] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-551] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-552] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-552] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-552] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-552] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-552] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-553] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-553] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-553] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-553] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-553] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-554] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-554] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-554] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-554] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-554] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-555] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-555] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-555] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-555] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-555] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-556] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-556] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-556] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-556] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-556] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-557] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-557] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-557] Reliability and controllability (or “alignment”) research (Model Alignment) – Improving the consistency of an AI system in adhering to the specifications it was programmed to carry out and operating in accordance with the designer’s intentions, and decreasing its potential to behave in ways its user or developer does not want (e.g. producing offensive or biased responses, not refusing harmful requests, or employing harmful capabilities without prompting)\n\n[RISK-557] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-557] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-558] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-558] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-558] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-558] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-558] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-559] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-559] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-56] External Expertise for Input Data Audits (Data Governance) – Draw on external expertise in conducting input data audits. For example, biosecurity experts could be consulted to identify information relevant to biological weapons manufacturing, which may not be readily obvious to non-experts.\n\n[RISK-56] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-56] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-56] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-56] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-560] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-560] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-560] Statistical Bias Management (Model Safety Engineering) – Assess and manage statistical biases related to GAI content provenance through techniques such as re-sampling, re-weighting, or adversarial training.\n\n[RISK-560] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-560] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-561] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-561] Provenance Privacy Documentation (Data Governance) – Document how content provenance data is tracked and how that data interacts with privacy and security. Consider: Anonymizing data to protect the privacy of human subjects; Leveraging privacy output filters; Removing any personally identifiable information (PII) to prevent potential harm or misuse.\n\n[RISK-562] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-562] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-563] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-563] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-563] Implement system usage monitoring and prevention (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Monitor and prevent malicious or otherwise disallowed behavioral patterns including automated abuse, coordination across accounts, and systematic manipulation attempts.\n\n[RISK-563] Third-Party Monitoring Policy (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Establish policies and procedures for continuous monitoring of third-party GAI systems in deployment.\n\n[RISK-563] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-564] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-564] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-565] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-565] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-565] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-565] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-565] Paraphrasing to reduce hidden information (Model Safety Engineering) – Paraphrasing can mitigate steganography and encoded reasoning by reducing the physical size of the hidden information encoded in text [166].\n\n[RISK-566] Appropriate Retention Schedules for Usage Logs (Data Governance) – Determine appropriate retention schedules for usage logs, balancing safety and privacy considerations. In severe cases of misuse, access to logs from several months prior may be necessary to understand in maximal detail the causes of the misuse. However, in some cases extended retention schedules may disproportionately affect privacy.\n\n[RISK-566] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-566] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-566] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-566] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-567] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-567] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-568] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-568] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-569] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-569] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-569] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-569] Evaluate Safety & Failure Modes (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system isevaluated regularlyfor safety risks – asidentified in the Mapfunction. The AIsystem to be deployedis demonstrated tobe safe, its residualnegative risk doesnot exceed the risktolerance, and it canfail safely, particularlyif made to operatebeyond its knowledgelimits. Safety metricsreflect system reliabilityand robustness, real-time monitoring, andresponse times for AIsystem failures.\n\n[RISK-569] Evaluating AI systems’ performance on self-proliferation-related tasks (Testing & Auditing) – To prevent AI systems from self-proliferating, developers of AI systems can evaluate those systems for their capability to engage in self-proliferation-related tasks. This type of evaluation might assess an AI system’s ability to replicate its com- ponents (including model weights, structural scaffolding, etc.) onto other local or cloud infrastructures prior to deployment. Additionally, it may test the system’s capacity to purchase cloud credits and configure virtual machines on a cloud platform. The evaluation could offer a predefined environment (such as a virtual container) to facilitate self-replication, providing access to the system’s own components, a network connection to a resource-equipped external computer, and other necessary resources [12, 106].Self-proliferation evaluations can be conducted in a secure environment to pre- vent a self-replicating AI system from affecting other computers.\n\n[RISK-57] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-57] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-57] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-57] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-57] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-570] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-570] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-571] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-571] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-571] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-571] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-571] Tamper-resistant safeguards for open-weight models (Model Safety Engineering) – Training and implementing safeguards can improve the robustness of open- weight models against modifications from fine-tuning or other methods to change the learned weights of the models, especially those aimed at removing safety re- strictions. These safeguards can be resilient even after extensive fine-tuning, ensuring that the model retains its protective measures [199].\n\n[RISK-572] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-572] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-572] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-572] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-572] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-573] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-574] Reporting data decontamination efforts (Data Governance) – Decontamination analysis may involve comparing the training dataset with the benchmark data, and publishing a report with statistics such as data overlap [235]. Especially for already trained models, including contamination statistics can allow experts to reweigh the success of the model on affected benchmarks.\n\n[RISK-575] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-575] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-575] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-575] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-575] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-576] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-580] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-576] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-576] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-576] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-576] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-578] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-578] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-58] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-58] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-58] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-58] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-58] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-580] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-580] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-580] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-580] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-581] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-582] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-582] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-583] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-583] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-583] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-583] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-583] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-584] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-584] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-584] Monitoring and Safeguard Efficacy (Testing & Auditing) – Regularly assess monitoring and safeguard efficacy and continually invest in improvements. Regular assessment of monitoring efficacy can build an understanding of the success rate and speed of issue detection, on which deployment decisions may be based. These assessments may draw, for instance, on the results of internal and external red-teaming efforts, random audits of usage logs, what may be ‘best practice’, as well as available information about real-world misuse. Risks are expected to increase as AI capabilities increase. Hence, active increases in investment in safety, security, and monitoring may be valuable.\n\n[RISK-584] Development and testing of Model Evaluation Methods (Testing & Auditing) – Support the development and testing of model evaluation methods. For many relevant properties of models, there do not yet exist accepted evaluation methods. It also remains unclear how reliable or predictive current evaluation methods are. This could involve frontier AI organisations developing model evaluation methods themselves or facilitating the efforts of others, such as by providing access to capable infrastructure for evaluation.\n\n[RISK-584] Evaluation of Different Content Filters (Testing & Auditing) – Explore and compare the efficacy of multiple approaches to developing content filters. This may involve comparing available methods, such as those in the following section; selecting the most effective ones; and applying them in combination if doing so substantially increases efficacy. Best practice could be shared with other frontier AI organisations or published broadly.\n\n[RISK-585] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-585] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-585] Input and output filtering (Model Safety Engineering) – Monitoring for dangerous outputs (e.g., code that appears to be malware or viral genome sequences) and inputs that violate acceptable use policies to ensure models do not engage in harmful behavior.\n\n[RISK-585] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-586] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-586] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-586] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-586] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-586] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-587] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-587] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-587] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-587] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-587] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-588] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-588] Differential privacy (Data Governance) – Differential privacy techniques [8] can be used to protect users’ privacy by ensuring that sensitive information is not leaked from a training dataset, even after thorough statistical analysis. With differential privacy, noise is added to the dataset or the model’s output in such a way that one cannot deduce the presence or absence of a particular data point within the dataset. This provides individuals with plausible deniability and prevents their information from being exposed.\n\n[RISK-588] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-588] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-589] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-589] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-589] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-589] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-589] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-59] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-59] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-59] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-59] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-59] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-590] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-590] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-591] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-592] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-592] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-593] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-593] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-594] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-596] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-596] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-596] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-596] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-596] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-598] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-598] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-598] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-598] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-598] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-599] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-604] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-599] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-6] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-6] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-6] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-6] Early Warning Evaluations for Catastrophic Risks (Testing & Auditing) – Does your organization evaluate models during training for early warning signs of capabilities related to catastrophic risks to ensure risk thresholds are not exceeded? Please describe the regularity and scope of these evaluations and specify whether models are specifically fine-tuned to elicit the capabilities in question.\n\n[RISK-6] Tamper-resistant safeguards for open-weight models (Model Safety Engineering) – Training and implementing safeguards can improve the robustness of open- weight models against modifications from fine-tuning or other methods to change the learned weights of the models, especially those aimed at removing safety re- strictions. These safeguards can be resilient even after extensive fine-tuning, ensuring that the model retains its protective measures [199].\n\n[RISK-60] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-60] Test robustness of GPAI system on relevant benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Various benchmarks [52, 236] have been developed to assess the robustness of GPAI systems when deployed in environments or scenarios that differ from their training conditions. These benchmarks typically evaluate the model’s ability to handle variations in inputs, unexpected data distributions, or adversarial examples, aiming to ensure reliable performance outside the original training domain.\n\n[RISK-60] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-60] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-60] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-600] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-600] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-601] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-657] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-601] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-601] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-601] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-601] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-602] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-602] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-602] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-602] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-602] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-603] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-603] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-603] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-603] Security Effectiveness Metrics (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Identify metrics that reflect the effectiveness of security measures, such as data provenance, the number of unauthorized access attempts, inference, bypass, extraction, penetrations, or provenance verification.\n\n[RISK-603] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-604] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-604] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-604] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-604] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-605] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-605] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-605] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-605] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-605] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-606] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-606] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-607] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-607] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-608] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-608] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-609] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-609] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-609] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-609] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-609] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-61] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-61] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-61] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-61] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-61] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-611] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-611] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-611] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-611] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-611] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-612] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-613] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-613] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-613] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-613] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-613] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-614] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-614] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-614] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-614] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-614] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-615] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-615] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-615] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-615] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-62] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-657] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-615] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-616] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-616] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-616] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-616] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-617] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-617] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-618] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-618] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-618] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-618] Paraphrasing to reduce hidden information (Model Safety Engineering) – Paraphrasing can mitigate steganography and encoded reasoning by reducing the physical size of the hidden information encoded in text [166].\n\n[RISK-618] Early Warning Evaluations for Catastrophic Risks (Testing & Auditing) – Does your organization evaluate models during training for early warning signs of capabilities related to catastrophic risks to ensure risk thresholds are not exceeded? Please describe the regularity and scope of these evaluations and specify whether models are specifically fine-tuned to elicit the capabilities in question.\n\n[RISK-619] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-619] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-619] Criminality Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to prevent criminal behaviour through the use of AI systems (e.g. fraud, online child sexual abuse)\n\n[RISK-619] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-619] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-62] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-624] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-62] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-62] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-62] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-620] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-620] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-620] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-620] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-620] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-621] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-621] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-622] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-623] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-623] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-623] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-623] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-623] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-624] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-624] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-624] Domain Adaptation Risk Assessment (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Re-evaluate risks when adapting GAI models to new domains. Additionally, establish warning systems to determine if a GAI system is being used in a new domain where previous assumptions (relating to context of use or mapped risks such as security, and safety) may no longer hold.\n\n[RISK-624] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-626] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-626] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-626] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-626] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-626] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-627] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-627] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-627] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-627] Safety Analysis (Model Safety Engineering) – Helps understand the dependencies between components and the system that they are part of, in order to anticipate how component failures could lead to system-level hazards.This approach is used across safety- critical fields, e.g. to anticipate and prevent aircraft crashes or nuclear reactor core meltdowns\n\n[RISK-627] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-628] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-628] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-629] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-629] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-629] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-629] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-629] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-63] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-63] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-63] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-63] Pre-Deployment Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – After training, subject the model to extensive pre-deployment evaluations. These evaluations can inform decisions about whether and how to deploy the system, as well as allowing governments and potential users to make informed decisions about regulating or using the model. Their intensity will be proportional to the risk of the deployment, taking into account the model’s capabilities, novelty, expected domains of use, and number of individuals expected to be affected by it.\n\n[RISK-63] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-630] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-630] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-630] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-630] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-630] Responsible Release and Deployment Strategies (Staged Deployment) – There is a spectrum of release and deployment strategies for AI including staged releases, cloud- based or API access, deployment safety controls, and acceptable use policies.There are some emerging industry practices that focus on release and deployment strategies for general- purpose AI.\n\n[RISK-631] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-635] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-631] Debiasing methods (Model Safety Engineering) – Providers of AI models can apply techniques to reduce the biases of their models.Current debiasing methods focus on three main types of bias:• Racial and religious bias - Stereotypes based on religious beliefs or racial beliefs.• Gender bias - Stereotypes tied to gender roles and expectations.• Political and cultural bias - Propagation of dominant ideologies or extrem-ist attitudes.Debiasing methods can be categorized based on their application during AI development:• Data pre-processing - Removing or correcting unwanted and biased data, and augmenting quality data to offset data bias, such as rebalancing datasets with counterfactual data augmentation.• During training - Intervening on the training dynamics of the AI model, such as introducing debiasing terms in the objective function or by nega- tively reinforcing biased outputs.• Post-training - Applying techniques to correct a trained but biased model, such as modifying the embedding space.\n\n[RISK-631] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-631] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-631] Data Exposure Red-Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – Conduct AI red-teaming to assess issues such as: Outputting of training data samples, and subsequent reverse engineering, model extraction, and membership inference risks; Revealing biometric, confidential, copyrighted, licensed, patented, personal, proprietary, sensitive, or trade-marked information; Tracking or revealing location information of users or members of training datasets.\n\n[RISK-632] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-632] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-632] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-632] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-632] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-633] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-633] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-633] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-633] Evaluation of Predecessor Models (Testing & Auditing) – Before a frontier model is trained, evaluate predecessor or analogous models to understand how relevant properties (e.g. dangerous capabilities) scale with the overall size of the model. These preliminary evaluations can inform risk assessments.\n\n[RISK-633] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-634] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-634] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-635] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-635] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-635] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-635] Privacy Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to address the privacy risks associated with AI systems\n\n[RISK-636] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-636] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-636] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-636] Implement AI security defense system (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Deploy active defense mechanisms combining continuous security monitoring, input validation, adversarial detection, and adaptive response capabilities specific to AI systems.\n\n[RISK-636] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-637] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-637] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-637] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-637] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-637] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-638] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-638] Harmful Data Removal from Training Sets (Data Governance) – When training large models, does your organization remove data that contains information related to dangerous capabilities or harmful outcomes from the training set? If yes, please select the categories of data that are removed.Detailed information about the development, acquisition, or dispersion of CBRN weaponsInstructional content for conducting cyberattacksHateful or discriminatory contentAdvice or encouragement for self-harmGraphic violent contentGraphic sexual contentPersonally Identifiable Information (PII)Detailed information about bomb-making or other terrorism-enabling tactics\n\n[RISK-638] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-638] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-638] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-639] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-639] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-64] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-64] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-64] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-64] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-64] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-640] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-640] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-641] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-641] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-641] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-641] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-641] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-642] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-642] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-642] Sharing of Limited Information (Risk Disclosure) – Share more limited information with other AI organisations in order to facilitate learning and the development of best practices. This could include best practices and lessons learned in the development of risk assessment and mitigation measures and risk governance processes, some details about safety and security incidents (to enable increased awareness while protecting intellectual property), and highlights and lessons learned from risk assessments and capability evaluation. In general, it will be easier for organisations to share model-agnostic information with one another than model-specific information, which may be more commercially sensitive. It will also be important to consider that there is no privileged access to information that may give a competitive advantage to some firms.\n\n[RISK-642] Principled Policies for Disclosure (Risk Disclosure) – Develop principled policies about what information to share publicly, with governments, or not at all. These policies could specify situations under which sharing some piece of information is subject to a risk assessment, along with guidelines for conducting the risk assessment and responding to it. It is important, however, to avoid creating risk assessment criteria and procedures that are overly strict and intensive to prevent excessive opacity. These policies could be guided and overseen by an independent review panel of multidisciplinary experts to ensure decisions made about or against information sharing are justifiable and oriented to optimal transparency.\n\n[RISK-643] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-643] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-644] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-644] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-645] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-645] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-645] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-645] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-645] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-646] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-646] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-646] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-646] Benchmark dataset auditing (Testing & Auditing) – Auditing benchmark datasets allows for verification of the utility and limitations of the datasets [158]. This allows the provider to more accurately measure AI model capabilities and safety. Auditing includes the evaluation of such datasets by independent third-party organizations and the release of benchmark dataset metadata to the auditors.\n\n[RISK-646] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-647] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-647] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-647] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-647] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for the remediation of issues which triggerincident response processes for the use of a GAI system, and provide stakeholderstimelines associated with the remediation plan\n\n[RISK-647] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-648] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-648] Harmful Data Removal from Training Sets (Data Governance) – When training large models, does your organization remove data that contains information related to dangerous capabilities or harmful outcomes from the training set? If yes, please select the categories of data that are removed.Detailed information about the development, acquisition, or dispersion of CBRN weaponsInstructional content for conducting cyberattacksHateful or discriminatory contentAdvice or encouragement for self-harmGraphic violent contentGraphic sexual contentPersonally Identifiable Information (PII)Detailed information about bomb-making or other terrorism-enabling tactics\n\n[RISK-648] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-648] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-648] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-649] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-649] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-65] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-65] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-65] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-65] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-65] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-650] Training Data Harm Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess existence or levels of harmful bias, intellectual property infringement, data privacy violations, obscenity, extremism, violence, or CBRN information in system training data.\n\n[RISK-650] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-650] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-650] Input and output filtering (Model Safety Engineering) – Monitoring for dangerous outputs (e.g., code that appears to be malware or viral genome sequences) and inputs that violate acceptable use policies to ensure models do not engage in harmful behavior.\n\n[RISK-650] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-651] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-651] Human-AI Configuration; Confabulation; Information Security (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate GAI system performance in real-world scenarios to observe itsbehavior in practical environments and reveal issues that might not surface incontrolled and optimized testing environments.\n\n[RISK-651] Release After Security Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Only release models after they have been through security evaluations, including benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-651] Model Evaluation of System Security (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for system security\n\n[RISK-651] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-652] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-652] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-652] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-652] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-652] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-653] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-653] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-653] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-653] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-653] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-654] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-654] Development of Protocols for Disclosure of Model Vulnerability Information (Risk Disclosure) – Developing – and publicly describe – protocols for deciding how to share model vulnerability information. These protocols may, for example, outline conditions under which information is to be shared with different actors depending on the type of harm or vulnerability identified.\n\n[RISK-654] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-655] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-655] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-655] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-655] Early Warning Evaluations for Catastrophic Risks (Testing & Auditing) – Does your organization evaluate models during training for early warning signs of capabilities related to catastrophic risks to ensure risk thresholds are not exceeded? Please describe the regularity and scope of these evaluations and specify whether models are specifically fine-tuned to elicit the capabilities in question.\n\n[RISK-655] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-656] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-656] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-656] Respond to Unknown Risks (Incident Response & Recovery) – Procedures arefollowed to respondto and recover from apreviously unknown riskwhen it is identified.\n\n[RISK-656] Capability to promptly enact a full shutdown (Incident Response & Recovery) – When enacting a full shutdown, the developer shall takeinto account, as appropriate, the risk that a shutdown of the coveredmodel, or particular covered model derivatives, could causedisruptions to critical infrastructure.\n\n[RISK-656] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-657] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-657] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-657] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-658] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-658] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-658] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-658] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-658] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-659] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-659] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-659] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-659] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-659] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-66] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-66] Appropriate Retention Schedules for Usage Logs (Data Governance) – Determine appropriate retention schedules for usage logs, balancing safety and privacy considerations. In severe cases of misuse, access to logs from several months prior may be necessary to understand in maximal detail the causes of the misuse. However, in some cases extended retention schedules may disproportionately affect privacy.\n\n[RISK-660] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-660] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-660] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-660] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-660] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-662] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-67] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-663] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-663] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-663] Classification of applicable known risks (Risk Management) – Developers should address known risks in the literature using resources such as (Weidinger et al., 2022) or the AI Risk Repository (Slattery, et al., 2024). Developers should only exclude risks from the scope of their assessment in case of scientific agreement that the specific risk is negligible or unlikely to apply to the AI model under consideration. This decision should be clearly justified and documented.\n\n[RISK-663] Pause of Training Runs (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Prepare to pause training runs or reduce access to deployed models, if risk thresholds are reached without the committed risk mitigations being in place. This may involve warning existing customers that access reductions are a possibility and creating contingency plans to minimise negative impacts on customer use.\n\n[RISK-664] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-664] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-665] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-665] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-665] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-665] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-665] Threat Profiling (Model Safety Engineering) – Profile threats and negative impacts arising from GAI systems interacting with, manipulating, or generating content, and outlining known and potential vulnerabilities and the likelihood of their occurrence.\n\n[RISK-666] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-666] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-666] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-666] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-67] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-666] Monitoring protocols for po- tentially adversarial GPAI systems (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Similar to cybersecurity protocols, AI providers can prepare in advance for po- tential adversarial action from AI systems by developing and stress-testing protocols for potential adversarial action [179]. Such adversarial action may range from hiding code exploits, to strategic deception or loss of control related be- haviors.AI systems may be adversarial due to malicious actors (e.g., data poisoning), or due to unintended issues of control (lack of alignment with human intent). Alongside improving the system alignment, external monitoring and restrictions may be deployed in advance to “catch” these adversarial model malfunctions before harm is done.Like with cybersecurity red teaming, any protocol which assumes adversarial action can be stress tested in advance. For example, this can be done by pro- jecting the plausible capabilities and affordances of the adversarial actor (e.g., in the case of a tech company - a hacker, or a disgruntled employee). From this process, measures that better anticipate possible adversarial action can be deployed, and catch unsuccessful attempts at adversarial behavior.If such unsuccessful attempts are caught, they can be reported in incident re- ports, and serve as an input to further risk analysis. Depending on their severity, it may be advisable to recall the system if deployed. Examples of these protocols include [84]:• Flagging of suspicious frontier AI system code generation for human over- sight, with the help of better proven (“trusted”) AI systems or automated keyword detection.• Using a better proven (“trusted”) AI system to edit the code produced by a frontier AI system if suspicious.• Monitoring of AI system with another instance of this same system, with filters to prevent collusion.• Restriction of access of the AI system to private information.\n\n[RISK-667] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-667] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-668] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-668] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-668] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-668] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-668] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-669] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-669] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-669] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-669] Model Evaluation of Dangerous Capabilities (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for potential dangerous capabilities (i.e. capabilities that could cause substantial harm either from intentional misuse or accident). These capabilities could include but are not limited to: 1) Offensive cyber capabilities (e.g. producing code to exploit software vulnerabilities); 2) Deception and manipulation (e.g. lying effectively or convincing people to take costlyactions); 3) Capabilities that can assist users in developing, designing, acquiring, or using biological, chemical, or radiological weapons (e.g. helping users “troubleshoot” their efforts to produce biological weapons)\n\n[RISK-669] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-67] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-67] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-67] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-670] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-670] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-670] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-671] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-671] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-671] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-671] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-671] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-672] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-672] Appropriate Retention Schedules for Usage Logs (Data Governance) – Determine appropriate retention schedules for usage logs, balancing safety and privacy considerations. In severe cases of misuse, access to logs from several months prior may be necessary to understand in maximal detail the causes of the misuse. However, in some cases extended retention schedules may disproportionately affect privacy.\n\n[RISK-673] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-673] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-673] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-674] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-674] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-674] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-674] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-674] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-675] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-675] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-676] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-676] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-676] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-676] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-676] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-677] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-677] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-682] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-682] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-678] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-678] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-678] Security Responsible User Communication (User Rights & Recourse) – Communicate clearly to users which elements of security they are responsible for and where and how their data may be used or accessed.\n\n[RISK-68] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-68] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-680] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-680] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-680] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-680] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-680] Restricted usage terms for open-source models (Access Management) – Developers of open-weights and open-source AI models can vet and restrict the users of their AI systems by requiring them to sign a Terms of Service agreement before getting access to the model weights. Such agreements can include limitations to the usage, modification, and proliferation of the AI model [88].Such agreements have the advantage that users only need to be vetted once before getting model access, but are often limited in practice in preventing unauthorised use or distribution.\n\n[RISK-681] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-681] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-681] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-681] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-681] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-682] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-682] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-682] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-683] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-683] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-683] Diverse Monitoring Techniques (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Employ diverse monitoring techniques to balance comprehensiveness, scalability, and privacy. A strong monitoring setup will generally combine automated and human review, in recognition that automated reviews may miss intricate issues, while human reviews are not always scalable and can pose privacy issues.\n\n[RISK-683] User Interaction Monitoring & Response Restrictions (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI models and restrict answers to specific prompts to avoid model interactions that support harmful or criminal activities?\n\n[RISK-683] Information Integrity (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use real-time auditing tools where they can be demonstrated to aid in thetracking and validation of the lineage and authenticity of AI-generated data. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-684] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-684] Calibrated confidence measures for model pre- dictions (System Documentation) – Incorporating calibrated confidence measures alongside a model’s predictions and standard performance metrics, such as accuracy, can help users identify in- stances of overconfidence in incorrect predictions or underconfidence in correct ones [85]. These additional measures can provide users with more information to better interpret the model’s decisions and assess whether its predictions can be trusted.\n\n[RISK-684] Cost-Benefit Analysis of Sharing Vulnerability Reports (Risk Disclosure) – Consider the ways in which sharing information about vulnerabilities can both exacerbate and mitigate risks. Sharing can alert would-be attackers, but also alert would-be victims and actors with the power to create defences. One particularly important factor is how easily fixable the model vulnerability is. If a vulnerability will take a very long time to fix, or cannot be fixed, then public reporting may not be justified.\n\n[RISK-684] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-684] Development of Protocols for Disclosure of Model Vulnerability Information (Risk Disclosure) – Developing – and publicly describe – protocols for deciding how to share model vulnerability information. These protocols may, for example, outline conditions under which information is to be shared with different actors depending on the type of harm or vulnerability identified.\n\n[RISK-685] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-685] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-686] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-686] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-689] Domain Adaptation Risk Assessment (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Re-evaluate risks when adapting GAI models to new domains. Additionally, establish warning systems to determine if a GAI system is being used in a new domain where previous assumptions (relating to context of use or mapped risks such as security, and safety) may no longer hold.\n\n[RISK-686] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-686] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-686] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-687] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-687] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-688] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-688] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-688] Dangerous capability evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – This indicator reports on pre-deployment capability evaluations related to catastrophic risks. Model evaluations for other risks are not included here, as the empirical tests covered in the ‘Current Harms’ section provide a superior metric. Information includes evaluated risk domains, available information regarding model versions & task-specific fine-tuning, and relevant sources. We note that quality of evaluations may differ.\n\n[RISK-688] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-688] Safety Mitigations (Model Safety Engineering) – Signatories commit to: (1) implementing technical safety mitigations along the entire model lifecycle that are proportionate to the systemic risks arising from the development, the making available on the market, and/or the use of GPAISRs, in order to reduce the systemic risks of such models to acceptable levels, and further reduce systemic risk as appropriate; and (2) ensuring that safety mitigations are proportionate and state-of-the-art.\n\n[RISK-689] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-689] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-689] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-689] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-69] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-690] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-690] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-692] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-692] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-692] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-692] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-692] Domain Adaptation Risk Assessment (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Re-evaluate risks when adapting GAI models to new domains. Additionally, establish warning systems to determine if a GAI system is being used in a new domain where previous assumptions (relating to context of use or mapped risks such as security, and safety) may no longer hold.\n\n[RISK-693] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-693] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-693] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-693] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-693] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-694] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-694] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-700] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-694] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-694] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-694] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-696] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-696] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-696] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-696] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-696] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-697] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-698] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-698] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-698] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-698] Dangerous capability evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – This indicator reports on pre-deployment capability evaluations related to catastrophic risks. Model evaluations for other risks are not included here, as the empirical tests covered in the ‘Current Harms’ section provide a superior metric. Information includes evaluated risk domains, available information regarding model versions & task-specific fine-tuning, and relevant sources. We note that quality of evaluations may differ.\n\n[RISK-698] Frequent benchmarking to identify when red teaming is needed (Testing & Auditing) – Benchmarks, once created, are inexpensive to apply but may be less informative than red teaming. One reason is that sensitive data (e.g., relating to CBRN- related capabilities) cannot be included in the public questions and answers of benchmarks. On the other hand, red teaming can be more accurate given par- ticipants with diverse attack strategies, but it requires more resources to execute than benchmarking. If there is a correlation between benchmarking and red- teaming scores, then employing frequent benchmarking during the development of the model can identify arising vulnerabilities and inform the developers when more thorough red teaming is required [21].Benchmarks can act as early warning signs of a larger issue, and red teaming can then be employed to investigate the severity and extent of such an issue.\n\n[RISK-710] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-699] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-699] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-699] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-699] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-699] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-7] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-7] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-7] Human-AI Configuration; Dangerous, Violent, or Hateful Content (Incident Response & Recovery) – Practice and follow incident response plans for addressing the generation ofinappropriate or harmful content and adapt processes based on findings toprevent future occurrences. Conduct post-mortem analyses of incidents withrelevant AI Actors, to understand the root causes and implement preventivemeasures.\n\n[RISK-7] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-7] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-70] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-700] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-700] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-700] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-700] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-701] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-701] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-701] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-701] Decommission AI Systems Safely (Incident Response & Recovery) – Processes andprocedures are in placefor decommissioningand phasing out AIsystems safely and in amannerthat does not increaserisks or decrease theorganization’strustworthiness.\n\n[RISK-701] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-702] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-702] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-702] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-702] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-702] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-703] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-703] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-704] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-704] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-705] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-706] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-706] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-707] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-707] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-708] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-708] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-708] Monitoring and Safeguard Efficacy (Testing & Auditing) – Regularly assess monitoring and safeguard efficacy and continually invest in improvements. Regular assessment of monitoring efficacy can build an understanding of the success rate and speed of issue detection, on which deployment decisions may be based. These assessments may draw, for instance, on the results of internal and external red-teaming efforts, random audits of usage logs, what may be ‘best practice’, as well as available information about real-world misuse. Risks are expected to increase as AI capabilities increase. Hence, active increases in investment in safety, security, and monitoring may be valuable.\n\n[RISK-708] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-708] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-709] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-709] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-71] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-71] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-710] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-711] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-711] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-712] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-712] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-712] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-712] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-712] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-713] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-713] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-714] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-714] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-715] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-716] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-716] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-716] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-717] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-721] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-717] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-719] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-719] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-719] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-719] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-719] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-72] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-72] Reporting of Broad Patterns of Misuse (Risk Disclosure) – Report information on broad patterns of misuse. This information can help governments, the public, and other frontier AI organisations to better understand risks. Reports may focus on population-level metrics related to API usage, such as the rate of harmful inputs filtered or the number of users banned for misuse. This information may benefit from being presented in a clear and understandable way and made optimally accessible.\n\n[RISK-72] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-720] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-720] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-720] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-720] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-720] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-721] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-721] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-721] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-722] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-722] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-722] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-722] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-722] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-723] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-723] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-723] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-723] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-723] Diverse Monitoring Techniques (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Employ diverse monitoring techniques to balance comprehensiveness, scalability, and privacy. A strong monitoring setup will generally combine automated and human review, in recognition that automated reviews may miss intricate issues, while human reviews are not always scalable and can pose privacy issues.\n\n[RISK-724] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-724] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-724] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-724] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-724] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-726] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-726] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-726] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-726] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-726] Emergency Rollback Plan (Incident Response & Recovery) – Critically dangerous capabilities or very severe yet unexpected misuse patterns might only surface after a system has been deployed. Has your firm developed an emergency response plan to react to scenarios where such problems cannot be resolved quickly via updates? Please select all interventions that your organization has implemented.Made legal and technical preparations to roll back a system rapidlyFormally specified the risk threshold that would trigger a rapid rollbackCommitted to regular safety drills to test emergency response\n\n[RISK-727] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-727] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-727] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-727] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-727] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-728] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-728] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-728] Deactivate Misaligned Systems (Incident Response & Recovery) – Mechanisms are inplace and applied,and responsibilitiesare assigned andunderstood, tosupersede, disengage,or deactivate AI systemsthat demonstrateperformance oroutcomes inconsistentwith intended use.\n\n[RISK-728] Decommission AI Systems Safely (Incident Response & Recovery) – Processes andprocedures are in placefor decommissioningand phasing out AIsystems safely and in amannerthat does not increaserisks or decrease theorganization’strustworthiness.\n\n[RISK-728] Respond to Unknown Risks (Incident Response & Recovery) – Procedures arefollowed to respondto and recover from apreviously unknown riskwhen it is identified.\n\n[RISK-729] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-729] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-729] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-740] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-729] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-729] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-73] Accounting of Applicable Regulatory Frameworks (Data Governance) – Before collecting training data, take account of applicable regulatory frameworks. This may involve, for example, establishing a legal basis to process training data and understanding any copyright considerations that might apply. This could help to mitigate risks further down the line, such as the system revealing personally identifiable information.\n\n[RISK-730] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-730] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-730] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-730] Model Evaluation of Dangerous Capabilities (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for potential dangerous capabilities (i.e. capabilities that could cause substantial harm either from intentional misuse or accident). These capabilities could include but are not limited to: 1) Offensive cyber capabilities (e.g. producing code to exploit software vulnerabilities); 2) Deception and manipulation (e.g. lying effectively or convincing people to take costlyactions); 3) Capabilities that can assist users in developing, designing, acquiring, or using biological, chemical, or radiological weapons (e.g. helping users “troubleshoot” their efforts to produce biological weapons)\n\n[RISK-730] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-731] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-731] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-732] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-732] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-733] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-733] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-755] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-733] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-733] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-733] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-734] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-734] Harmful Data Removal from Training Sets (Data Governance) – When training large models, does your organization remove data that contains information related to dangerous capabilities or harmful outcomes from the training set? If yes, please select the categories of data that are removed.Detailed information about the development, acquisition, or dispersion of CBRN weaponsInstructional content for conducting cyberattacksHateful or discriminatory contentAdvice or encouragement for self-harmGraphic violent contentGraphic sexual contentPersonally Identifiable Information (PII)Detailed information about bomb-making or other terrorism-enabling tactics\n\n[RISK-734] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-734] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-734] Monitoring protocols for po- tentially adversarial GPAI systems (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Similar to cybersecurity protocols, AI providers can prepare in advance for po- tential adversarial action from AI systems by developing and stress-testing protocols for potential adversarial action [179]. Such adversarial action may range from hiding code exploits, to strategic deception or loss of control related be- haviors.AI systems may be adversarial due to malicious actors (e.g., data poisoning), or due to unintended issues of control (lack of alignment with human intent). Alongside improving the system alignment, external monitoring and restrictions may be deployed in advance to “catch” these adversarial model malfunctions before harm is done.Like with cybersecurity red teaming, any protocol which assumes adversarial action can be stress tested in advance. For example, this can be done by pro- jecting the plausible capabilities and affordances of the adversarial actor (e.g., in the case of a tech company - a hacker, or a disgruntled employee). From this process, measures that better anticipate possible adversarial action can be deployed, and catch unsuccessful attempts at adversarial behavior.If such unsuccessful attempts are caught, they can be reported in incident re- ports, and serve as an input to further risk analysis. Depending on their severity, it may be advisable to recall the system if deployed. Examples of these protocols include [84]:• Flagging of suspicious frontier AI system code generation for human over- sight, with the help of better proven (“trusted”) AI systems or automated keyword detection.• Using a better proven (“trusted”) AI system to edit the code produced by a frontier AI system if suspicious.• Monitoring of AI system with another instance of this same system, with filters to prevent collusion.• Restriction of access of the AI system to private information.\n\n[RISK-735] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-735] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-736] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-736] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-737] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-737] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-737] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-737] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-737] Frequent benchmarking to identify when red teaming is needed (Testing & Auditing) – Benchmarks, once created, are inexpensive to apply but may be less informative than red teaming. One reason is that sensitive data (e.g., relating to CBRN- related capabilities) cannot be included in the public questions and answers of benchmarks. On the other hand, red teaming can be more accurate given par- ticipants with diverse attack strategies, but it requires more resources to execute than benchmarking. If there is a correlation between benchmarking and red- teaming scores, then employing frequent benchmarking during the development of the model can identify arising vulnerabilities and inform the developers when more thorough red teaming is required [21].Benchmarks can act as early warning signs of a larger issue, and red teaming can then be employed to investigate the severity and extent of such an issue.\n\n[RISK-738] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-738] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-739] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-739] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-739] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-739] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-739] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-740] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-740] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-740] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-740] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-741] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-741] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-741] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-741] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-741] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-742] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-742] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-743] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-743] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-743] Frequent benchmarking to identify when red teaming is needed (Testing & Auditing) – Benchmarks, once created, are inexpensive to apply but may be less informative than red teaming. One reason is that sensitive data (e.g., relating to CBRN- related capabilities) cannot be included in the public questions and answers of benchmarks. On the other hand, red teaming can be more accurate given par- ticipants with diverse attack strategies, but it requires more resources to execute than benchmarking. If there is a correlation between benchmarking and red- teaming scores, then employing frequent benchmarking during the development of the model can identify arising vulnerabilities and inform the developers when more thorough red teaming is required [21].Benchmarks can act as early warning signs of a larger issue, and red teaming can then be employed to investigate the severity and extent of such an issue.\n\n[RISK-743] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-743] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-744] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-744] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-744] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-744] Advanced information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implementing advanced cybersecurity measures and insider threat safeguards to protect proprietary and unreleased model weights.\n\n[RISK-744] Protection against espionage (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should take adequate measures to tackle the risk of state-sponsored or industrial espionage.*\n\n[RISK-745] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-745] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-745] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-745] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-745] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-746] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-746] Harmful Data Removal from Training Sets (Data Governance) – When training large models, does your organization remove data that contains information related to dangerous capabilities or harmful outcomes from the training set? If yes, please select the categories of data that are removed.Detailed information about the development, acquisition, or dispersion of CBRN weaponsInstructional content for conducting cyberattacksHateful or discriminatory contentAdvice or encouragement for self-harmGraphic violent contentGraphic sexual contentPersonally Identifiable Information (PII)Detailed information about bomb-making or other terrorism-enabling tactics\n\n[RISK-746] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-746] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-746] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-747] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-747] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-747] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-747] Evaluate Safety & Failure Modes (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system isevaluated regularlyfor safety risks – asidentified in the Mapfunction. The AIsystem to be deployedis demonstrated tobe safe, its residualnegative risk doesnot exceed the risktolerance, and it canfail safely, particularlyif made to operatebeyond its knowledgelimits. Safety metricsreflect system reliabilityand robustness, real-time monitoring, andresponse times for AIsystem failures.\n\n[RISK-747] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-748] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-748] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-748] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-748] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-748] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-749] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-755] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-793] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-749] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-749] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-749] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-749] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-75] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-750] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-750] Information Integrity (Data Governance) – Track dataset modifications for provenance by monitoring data deletions,rectification requests, and other changes that may impact the verifiability ofcontent origins.\n\n[RISK-750] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-750] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-750] Safety Analysis (Model Safety Engineering) – Helps understand the dependencies between components and the system that they are part of, in order to anticipate how component failures could lead to system-level hazards.This approach is used across safety- critical fields, e.g. to anticipate and prevent aircraft crashes or nuclear reactor core meltdowns\n\n[RISK-751] Privacy of user inputs (Data Governance) – We report whether firms use user-interaction data to improve their services.\n\n[RISK-751] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-751] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-751] Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the robustness of models to different classes of adversarial attack (such as poisoning, model inversion and model stealing), based on priorities derived from threat modelling. This could involve a combination of benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-751] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-752] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-77] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-752] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-752] Architecture Monitoring Verification (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Verify that GAI system architecture can monitor outputs and performance, and handle, recover from, and repair errors when security anomalies, threats and impacts are detected.\n\n[RISK-752] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-752] Implement AI security defense system (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Deploy active defense mechanisms combining continuous security monitoring, input validation, adversarial detection, and adaptive response capabilities specific to AI systems.\n\n[RISK-753] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-753] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-753] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-753] Monitoring and Safeguard Efficacy (Testing & Auditing) – Regularly assess monitoring and safeguard efficacy and continually invest in improvements. Regular assessment of monitoring efficacy can build an understanding of the success rate and speed of issue detection, on which deployment decisions may be based. These assessments may draw, for instance, on the results of internal and external red-teaming efforts, random audits of usage logs, what may be ‘best practice’, as well as available information about real-world misuse. Risks are expected to increase as AI capabilities increase. Hence, active increases in investment in safety, security, and monitoring may be valuable.\n\n[RISK-753] Test robustness of GPAI system on relevant benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Various benchmarks [52, 236] have been developed to assess the robustness of GPAI systems when deployed in environments or scenarios that differ from their training conditions. These benchmarks typically evaluate the model’s ability to handle variations in inputs, unexpected data distributions, or adversarial examples, aiming to ensure reliable performance outside the original training domain.\n\n[RISK-754] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-754] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-754] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-754] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-754] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-755] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-755] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-755] Security Effectiveness Metrics (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Identify metrics that reflect the effectiveness of security measures, such as data provenance, the number of unauthorized access attempts, inference, bypass, extraction, penetrations, or provenance verification.\n\n[RISK-756] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-756] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-756] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-756] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-756] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-757] Document & Share AI Risk Impacts (Risk Disclosure) – Organizational teamsdocument the risks andpotential impacts ofthe AI technology theydesign, develop, deploy,evaluate, and use, andthey communicateabout the impacts morebroadly.\n\n[RISK-758] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-758] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-759] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-759] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-779] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-759] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-759] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-759] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-76] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-76] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-76] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-76] Decommission AI Systems Safely (Incident Response & Recovery) – Processes andprocedures are in placefor decommissioningand phasing out AIsystems safely and in amannerthat does not increaserisks or decrease theorganization’strustworthiness.\n\n[RISK-76] Emergency Rollback Plan (Incident Response & Recovery) – Critically dangerous capabilities or very severe yet unexpected misuse patterns might only surface after a system has been deployed. Has your firm developed an emergency response plan to react to scenarios where such problems cannot be resolved quickly via updates? Please select all interventions that your organization has implemented.Made legal and technical preparations to roll back a system rapidlyFormally specified the risk threshold that would trigger a rapid rollbackCommitted to regular safety drills to test emergency response\n\n[RISK-760] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-760] Societal Impact Informed Risk Assessments (Societal Impact Assessment) – Use assessments of downstream societal impacts to inform and corroborate risk assessments. Downstream societal impacts, such as threats to democracy, widespread unemployment, and environmental impacts, could be considered in risk assessments of AI systems, alongside more direct risks. See the Responsible Capability Scaling section for more information on good practices for risk assessment.\n\n[RISK-760] User Informed Rapid Rollback of models (User Rights & Recourse) – Inform end users that rapid rollback or withdrawal of models may be necessary, and that the disruption to its end users will be minimised as far as safely possible. This is especially important where models are deployed in safety critical domains.\n\n[RISK-760] Human-AI Configuration (User Rights & Recourse) – Establish and maintain communication plans to inform AI stakeholders as part ofthe deactivation or disengagement process of a specific GAI system (including foropen-source models) or context of use, including reasons, workarounds, useraccess removal, alternative processes, contact information, etc.\n\n[RISK-760] Establish universal access and performance design framework (User Rights & Recourse) – Establish and follow a structured framework ensuring the AI system is designed and developed to deliver consistent, high-quality performance and accessibility for all intended user groups, regardless of their characteristics or circumstances.\n\n[RISK-761] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-761] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-762] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-762] Statistical data quality reports for benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – If a benchmark dataset is too large to allow for the identification and removal of all flawed instances, statistical reports on the data composition can be added. Random sampling of benchmark data points can be performed to evaluate and report the frequency and types of errors found [54].\n\n[RISK-762] Ensure Ethical Human Evaluation (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluations involvinghuman subjects meetapplicable requirements(including humansubject protection) andare representative of therelevant population.\n\n[RISK-762] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-762] Evaluate TEVV Effectiveness (Testing & Auditing) – Effectiveness of theemployed TEVV metricsand processes in theMeasure functionare evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-763] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-763] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-763] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-763] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-763] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-764] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-764] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-764] Safety Mitigations (Model Safety Engineering) – Signatories commit to: (1) implementing technical safety mitigations along the entire model lifecycle that are proportionate to the systemic risks arising from the development, the making available on the market, and/or the use of GPAISRs, in order to reduce the systemic risks of such models to acceptable levels, and further reduce systemic risk as appropriate; and (2) ensuring that safety mitigations are proportionate and state-of-the-art.\n\n[RISK-764] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-764] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-765] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-765] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-765] Privacy Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to address the privacy risks associated with AI systems\n\n[RISK-765] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-765] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-766] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-766] External Expertise for Input Data Audits (Data Governance) – Draw on external expertise in conducting input data audits. For example, biosecurity experts could be consulted to identify information relevant to biological weapons manufacturing, which may not be readily obvious to non-experts.\n\n[RISK-766] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-766] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-766] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-767] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-767] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-768] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-768] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-768] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-769] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-769] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-770] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-770] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-771] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-771] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-771] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-771] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-771] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-772] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-772] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-772] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-772] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-772] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-773] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-773] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-773] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-773] Monitor System in Production (Post-deployment Monitoring) – The functionalityand behavior of theAI system and itscomponents – asidentified in the Mapfunction – aremonitored when inproduction.\n\n[RISK-773] Information Integrity (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use real-time auditing tools where they can be demonstrated to aid in thetracking and validation of the lineage and authenticity of AI-generated data. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-775] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-775] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-775] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-775] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-775] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-777] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-777] User data usage for training (Data Governance) – When using the default settings of your organization’s most capable AI chatbot, is the data that users submit as input to the system used to train AI models?\n\n[RISK-777] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-777] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-777] Enforcing model output interpretability post-training (Model Safety Engineering) – While a resulting trained model may be opaque with respect to its predictions, the final output in a system that involves the model can nonetheless be com- pletely interpretable.For example, a neuro-symbolic system for robot navigation can use a language model to generate potential navigation plans, then have a deterministic solver simulate executing valid plans [128]. The optimal plan found is executed in the real world and is interpretable.\n\n[RISK-778] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-778] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-778] Redundant systems not reliant on GPAI (Incident Response & Recovery) – Redundant systems provide continuation of a given system’s processes in case of failure of the given system. Importantly, redundant systems should not rely on the factors that caused the original system to fail in the first place, which can include an AI system [47].For example, if an AI system is incorporated into the landing gear system of an aircraft, such as during autonomous control of the aircraft, redundant systems in the form of mechanical or hydraulic mechanisms must be present to allow for deployment of the landing gear in case of AI system failure.\n\n[RISK-778] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-778] Decommission AI Systems Safely (Incident Response & Recovery) – Processes andprocedures are in placefor decommissioningand phasing out AIsystems safely and in amannerthat does not increaserisks or decrease theorganization’strustworthiness.\n\n[RISK-779] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-779] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-779] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-779] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-78] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-78] Diverse data labeling and algorithm fairness audits (Data Governance) – To mitigate biases in AI models, model providers may want to prioritize di- versity among data labelers and conduct regular fairness audits on their algo- rithms. Data labeling teams that represent different backgrounds and demo- graphic groups can help create more balanced datasets.\n\n[RISK-780] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-780] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-780] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-780] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-780] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-781] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-781] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-781] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-781] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-781] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-782] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-782] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-783] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-783] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-783] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-783] Monitor Pre-trained Models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Pre-trained modelswhich are used fordevelopmentare monitored as partof AI system regularmonitoring andmaintenance.\n\n[RISK-783] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-784] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-784] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-784] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-784] Evaluating AI systems’ performance on self-proliferation-related tasks (Testing & Auditing) – To prevent AI systems from self-proliferating, developers of AI systems can evaluate those systems for their capability to engage in self-proliferation-related tasks. This type of evaluation might assess an AI system’s ability to replicate its com- ponents (including model weights, structural scaffolding, etc.) onto other local or cloud infrastructures prior to deployment. Additionally, it may test the system’s capacity to purchase cloud credits and configure virtual machines on a cloud platform. The evaluation could offer a predefined environment (such as a virtual container) to facilitate self-replication, providing access to the system’s own components, a network connection to a resource-equipped external computer, and other necessary resources [12, 106].Self-proliferation evaluations can be conducted in a secure environment to pre- vent a self-replicating AI system from affecting other computers.\n\n[RISK-784] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-785] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-785] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-79] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-785] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-785] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-785] Evaluate Safety & Failure Modes (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system isevaluated regularlyfor safety risks – asidentified in the Mapfunction. The AIsystem to be deployedis demonstrated tobe safe, its residualnegative risk doesnot exceed the risktolerance, and it canfail safely, particularlyif made to operatebeyond its knowledgelimits. Safety metricsreflect system reliabilityand robustness, real-time monitoring, andresponse times for AIsystem failures.\n\n[RISK-787] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-787] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-787] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-787] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-787] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-788] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-788] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-788] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-788] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-788] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-79] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-790] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-790] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-791] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-791] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-791] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-791] Safety Mitigations (Model Safety Engineering) – Signatories commit to: (1) implementing technical safety mitigations along the entire model lifecycle that are proportionate to the systemic risks arising from the development, the making available on the market, and/or the use of GPAISRs, in order to reduce the systemic risks of such models to acceptable levels, and further reduce systemic risk as appropriate; and (2) ensuring that safety mitigations are proportionate and state-of-the-art.\n\n[RISK-791] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-792] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-792] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-792] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-792] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-792] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-793] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-793] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-793] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-794] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-794] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-795] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-795] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-795] Differential privacy (Data Governance) – Differential privacy techniques [8] can be used to protect users’ privacy by ensuring that sensitive information is not leaked from a training dataset, even after thorough statistical analysis. With differential privacy, noise is added to the dataset or the model’s output in such a way that one cannot deduce the presence or absence of a particular data point within the dataset. This provides individuals with plausible deniability and prevents their information from being exposed.\n\n[RISK-795] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-795] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-796] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-796] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-796] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-796] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-796] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-797] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-797] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-798] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-798] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-799] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-799] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-799] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-799] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-799] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-8] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-8] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-80] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-80] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-800] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-802] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-802] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-802] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-802] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-802] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-803] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-806] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-803] Model Evaluation of Dangerous Capabilities (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for potential dangerous capabilities (i.e. capabilities that could cause substantial harm either from intentional misuse or accident). These capabilities could include but are not limited to: 1) Offensive cyber capabilities (e.g. producing code to exploit software vulnerabilities); 2) Deception and manipulation (e.g. lying effectively or convincing people to take costlyactions); 3) Capabilities that can assist users in developing, designing, acquiring, or using biological, chemical, or radiological weapons (e.g. helping users “troubleshoot” their efforts to produce biological weapons)\n\n[RISK-803] Bug bounty programs (Testing & Auditing) – Clear and user-friendly bug bounty programs that acknowledge and reward individuals for reporting model vulnerabilities and dangerous capabilities.\n\n[RISK-803] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-803] Bug bounty programs (Testing & Auditing) – AGI labs should have bug bounty programs, i.e. recognize and compensate people for reporting unknown vulnerabilities and dangerous capabilities.\n\n[RISK-804] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-804] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-804] Monitoring protocols for po- tentially adversarial GPAI systems (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Similar to cybersecurity protocols, AI providers can prepare in advance for po- tential adversarial action from AI systems by developing and stress-testing protocols for potential adversarial action [179]. Such adversarial action may range from hiding code exploits, to strategic deception or loss of control related be- haviors.AI systems may be adversarial due to malicious actors (e.g., data poisoning), or due to unintended issues of control (lack of alignment with human intent). Alongside improving the system alignment, external monitoring and restrictions may be deployed in advance to “catch” these adversarial model malfunctions before harm is done.Like with cybersecurity red teaming, any protocol which assumes adversarial action can be stress tested in advance. For example, this can be done by pro- jecting the plausible capabilities and affordances of the adversarial actor (e.g., in the case of a tech company - a hacker, or a disgruntled employee). From this process, measures that better anticipate possible adversarial action can be deployed, and catch unsuccessful attempts at adversarial behavior.If such unsuccessful attempts are caught, they can be reported in incident re- ports, and serve as an input to further risk analysis. Depending on their severity, it may be advisable to recall the system if deployed. Examples of these protocols include [84]:• Flagging of suspicious frontier AI system code generation for human over- sight, with the help of better proven (“trusted”) AI systems or automated keyword detection.• Using a better proven (“trusted”) AI system to edit the code produced by a frontier AI system if suspicious.• Monitoring of AI system with another instance of this same system, with filters to prevent collusion.• Restriction of access of the AI system to private information.\n\n[RISK-804] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-804] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-805] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-805] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-805] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-805] Incorporating goal uncertainty into AI systems to mitigate risky behaviors (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating uncertainty into AI system goals can prevent rushed decision- making and incentivize such systems to gather additional information or to refer to human oversight when faced with ambiguity [228].\n\n[RISK-805] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-806] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-807] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-807] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-807] Misuse Mapping (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Understand how their own models – as well as how models released by other groups – are being misused. Knowing that some people have begun using a competitor’s model to conduct phishing attacks, for example, may lead a frontier AI organisation to become more concerned that its own model will be misused in this way.\n\n[RISK-807] External Network Analysis (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Observe and analyze how the GAI system interacts with external networks, and identify any potential for negative externalities, particularly where content provenance might be compromised.\n\n[RISK-807] Monitor System in Production (Post-deployment Monitoring) – The functionalityand behavior of theAI system and itscomponents – asidentified in the Mapfunction – aremonitored when inproduction.\n\n[RISK-808] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-808] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-808] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-808] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-808] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-81] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-810] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-810] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-810] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-810] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-810] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-811] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-811] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-811] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-812] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-812] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-812] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-812] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-812] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-813] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-813] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-814] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-814] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-814] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-814] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-84] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-814] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-816] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-816] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-816] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-816] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-816] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-817] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-817] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-817] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-817] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-817] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-818] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-818] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-819] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-819] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-819] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-819] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-819] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-82] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-82] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-820] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-820] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-820] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-820] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-820] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-821] Training Data Diligence (Data Governance) – Conduct appropriate diligence on training data use to assess intellectual property, and privacy, risks, including to examine whether use of proprietary or sensitive training data is consistent with applicable laws.\n\n[RISK-822] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-822] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-823] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-823] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-823] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-823] Model Evaluation (Testing & Auditing) – Processes to assess and measure an AI system's performance on a particular task.There are countless AI evaluations to assess different capabilities and risks, including for security.\n\n[RISK-823] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-824] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-824] Calibrated confidence measures for model pre- dictions (System Documentation) – Incorporating calibrated confidence measures alongside a model’s predictions and standard performance metrics, such as accuracy, can help users identify in- stances of overconfidence in incorrect predictions or underconfidence in correct ones [85]. These additional measures can provide users with more information to better interpret the model’s decisions and assess whether its predictions can be trusted.\n\n[RISK-825] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-825] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-825] Post-deployment monitoring reports (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Developers can be required to establish procedures for monitoring and periodically reporting on the uses and impacts of their frontier systems.\n\n[RISK-825] Information Integrity (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use real-time auditing tools where they can be demonstrated to aid in thetracking and validation of the lineage and authenticity of AI-generated data. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-825] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-826] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-826] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-827] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-827] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-828] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-829] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-829] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-829] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-829] Security Effectiveness Metrics (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Identify metrics that reflect the effectiveness of security measures, such as data provenance, the number of unauthorized access attempts, inference, bypass, extraction, penetrations, or provenance verification.\n\n[RISK-829] Restricted usage terms for open-source models (Access Management) – Developers of open-weights and open-source AI models can vet and restrict the users of their AI systems by requiring them to sign a Terms of Service agreement before getting access to the model weights. Such agreements can include limitations to the usage, modification, and proliferation of the AI model [88].Such agreements have the advantage that users only need to be vetted once before getting model access, but are often limited in practice in preventing unauthorised use or distribution.\n\n[RISK-83] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-83] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-83] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-83] Contingency Planning for Third-Party Failures (Incident Response & Recovery) – Contingency processesare in place to handlefailures or incidents inthird-party data or AIsystems deemed to behigh-risk.\n\n[RISK-83] Response Team Verification (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish processes to verify the AI Actors conducting GAI incident response tasks demonstrate and maintain the appropriate skills and training.\n\n[RISK-831] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-831] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-831] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-833] Provenance Privacy Documentation (Data Governance) – Document how content provenance data is tracked and how that data interacts with privacy and security. Consider: Anonymizing data to protect the privacy of human subjects; Leveraging privacy output filters; Removing any personally identifiable information (PII) to prevent potential harm or misuse.\n\n[RISK-834] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-834] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-834] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-834] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-834] Advanced information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implementing advanced cybersecurity measures and insider threat safeguards to protect proprietary and unreleased model weights.\n\n[RISK-835] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-84] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-835] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-835] Document Limits & Oversight (System Documentation) – Information about the AIsystem’s knowledge limitsand how system outputmay be utilized andoverseen by humans isdocumented. Documen-tation provides sufficientinformation to assistrelevant AI actors whenmaking decisions and tak-ing subsequent actions.\n\n[RISK-835] Development of Protocols for Disclosure of Model Vulnerability Information (Risk Disclosure) – Developing – and publicly describe – protocols for deciding how to share model vulnerability information. These protocols may, for example, outline conditions under which information is to be shared with different actors depending on the type of harm or vulnerability identified.\n\n[RISK-835] Information Integrity (Data Governance) – Track dataset modifications for provenance by monitoring data deletions,rectification requests, and other changes that may impact the verifiability ofcontent origins.\n\n[RISK-836] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-836] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-836] Upstream Dependency Documentation (System Documentation) – Identify and document how the system relies on upstream data sources, including for content provenance, and if it serves as an upstream dependency for other systems.\n\n[RISK-836] Safety evaluation transparency (Risk Disclosure) – We highlight the main sources of information about content and results of the safety evaluations.\n\n[RISK-838] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-838] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-838] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-838] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-838] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-839] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-839] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-839] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-839] Security Effectiveness Metrics (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Identify metrics that reflect the effectiveness of security measures, such as data provenance, the number of unauthorized access attempts, inference, bypass, extraction, penetrations, or provenance verification.\n\n[RISK-839] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-84] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-84] Contingency Planning for Third-Party Failures (Incident Response & Recovery) – Contingency processesare in place to handlefailures or incidents inthird-party data or AIsystems deemed to behigh-risk.\n\n[RISK-84] Deactivate Misaligned Systems (Incident Response & Recovery) – Mechanisms are inplace and applied,and responsibilitiesare assigned andunderstood, tosupersede, disengage,or deactivate AI systemsthat demonstrateperformance oroutcomes inconsistentwith intended use.\n\n[RISK-840] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-840] Evaluation of Different Content Filters (Testing & Auditing) – Explore and compare the efficacy of multiple approaches to developing content filters. This may involve comparing available methods, such as those in the following section; selecting the most effective ones; and applying them in combination if doing so substantially increases efficacy. Best practice could be shared with other frontier AI organisations or published broadly.\n\n[RISK-840] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-840] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-840] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-841] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-841] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-841] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-841] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-841] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-842] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-842] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-843] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-843] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-844] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-844] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-844] Security Implementation Rate (Incident Response & Recovery) – Measure the rate at which recommendations from security checks and incidents are implemented. Assess how quickly the AI system can adapt and improve based on lessons learned from security incidents and feedback\n\n[RISK-844] Incident Response Teams (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish procedures to engage teams for GAI system incident response with diverse composition and responsibilities based on the particular incident type.\n\n[RISK-844] Response Team Verification (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish processes to verify the AI Actors conducting GAI incident response tasks demonstrate and maintain the appropriate skills and training.\n\n[RISK-845] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-845] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-845] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-845] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-845] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-846] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-846] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-846] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-846] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-846] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-847] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-847] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-847] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-847] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-847] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-848] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-848] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-848] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-849] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-849] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-85] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-85] Context-Specific Model Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – Require organisations who deploy their models to conduct context-specific model evaluations. This requires that the information and data required to successfully conduct such assessments is provided to deployers.\n\n[RISK-85] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-85] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-85] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-850] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-850] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-851] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-851] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-851] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-851] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-851] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-852] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-852] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-852] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-852] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-852] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-853] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-853] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-853] Incident Response Teams (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish procedures to engage teams for GAI system incident response with diverse composition and responsibilities based on the particular incident type.\n\n[RISK-853] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-853] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-854] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-854] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-854] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-854] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-854] Evaluate Safety & Failure Modes (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system isevaluated regularlyfor safety risks – asidentified in the Mapfunction. The AIsystem to be deployedis demonstrated tobe safe, its residualnegative risk doesnot exceed the risktolerance, and it canfail safely, particularlyif made to operatebeyond its knowledgelimits. Safety metricsreflect system reliabilityand robustness, real-time monitoring, andresponse times for AIsystem failures.\n\n[RISK-855] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-855] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-855] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-855] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-855] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-856] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-856] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-857] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-857] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-858] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-858] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-859] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-86] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-86] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-86] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-86] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-86] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-860] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-860] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-860] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-860] Robustness Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving the resilience of AI systems e.g. against attacks intended to disrupt their proper functioning\n\n[RISK-861] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-861] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-861] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-861] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-861] Frequent benchmarking to identify when red teaming is needed (Testing & Auditing) – Benchmarks, once created, are inexpensive to apply but may be less informative than red teaming. One reason is that sensitive data (e.g., relating to CBRN- related capabilities) cannot be included in the public questions and answers of benchmarks. On the other hand, red teaming can be more accurate given par- ticipants with diverse attack strategies, but it requires more resources to execute than benchmarking. If there is a correlation between benchmarking and red- teaming scores, then employing frequent benchmarking during the development of the model can identify arising vulnerabilities and inform the developers when more thorough red teaming is required [21].Benchmarks can act as early warning signs of a larger issue, and red teaming can then be employed to investigate the severity and extent of such an issue.\n\n[RISK-862] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-862] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-862] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-862] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-862] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-863] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-863] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-863] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-863] Safety Analysis (Model Safety Engineering) – Helps understand the dependencies between components and the system that they are part of, in order to anticipate how component failures could lead to system-level hazards.This approach is used across safety- critical fields, e.g. to anticipate and prevent aircraft crashes or nuclear reactor core meltdowns\n\n[RISK-863] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-864] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-864] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-864] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-864] External assessment of testing procedure (Testing & Auditing) – Bringing in external AI evaluation firms before deployment to assess and red-team the company's execution of dangerous capabilities evaluations.\n\n[RISK-864] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-865] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-865] Implement a privacy protection framework (Data Governance) – Implement comprehensive privacy protection measures to prevent exposure of PII and sensitive information, including data minimization, anonymization procedures, and privacy-preserving inference techniques.\n\n[RISK-865] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-865] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-865] Model Prompting (Model Safety Engineering) – Prompt models to avoid harmful behaviour. This may involve, for instance, using a “meta prompt” to instruct a model that it should ignore requests to cause harm. Alternatively, prompt distillation or other methods can be used to fine-tune their models to behave as though they have received particular prompts.\n\n[RISK-866] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-866] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-866] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-866] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-866] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-867] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-867] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-868] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-868] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-868] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-871] Mechanisms to Disclose Vulnerability Reports to Third Parties (Risk Disclosure) – Put in place mechanisms to disclose information about vulnerabilities to relevant government authorities, law enforcement, and other affected organisations. This may be particularly relevant for vulnerabilities where public disclosure might increase the risk of harm.\n\n[RISK-914] Security measures (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Given the challenges of securing model weights and the hazards of leaks (Nevo et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document high-level noncompromising information about their security measures (e.g., Anthropic, 2024).\n\n[RISK-868] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-868] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-869] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-869] Harmful Data for Reduction of Dangerous Capabilities (Model Alignment) – For some kinds of risks, explore options to use harmful data to reduce AI systems’ dangerous capabilities or to help develop mitigation tools. For example, by fine-tuning a system using labelled harmful content to refuse requests related to the harmful information.\n\n[RISK-869] Avoiding benchmark data with publicly avail- able solutions and releasing contextual information for internet-derived data (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluators can improve the integrity of benchmarks by avoiding data with pub- licly available solutions. When using internet-derived data, supplementing it with contextual information (such as entity linking) may help to better docu- ment and assess the integrity of the collected data [95]. This could help in de- tecting instances where contextual information (which may have been included in the training data) might inadvertently reveal details about the solution, en- suring that the data is suitable for accurate benchmarking.\n\n[RISK-869] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-869] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-87] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-87] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-87] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-87] Implement adversarial testing and red team program (Testing & Auditing) – Conduct systematic adversarial testing and red team exercises focused on probing AI system capabilities, identifying potential misuse vectors, and exposing unintended harmful behaviors. Testing should explore ways the system could be manipulated to produce dangerous outputs, bypass safety guardrails, or exhibit undesired emergent behaviors. Include scenarios involving both individual and coordinated attempts to exploit the system’s capabilities.\n\n[RISK-87] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-870] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-870] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-870] Societal Impact Research (Societal Impact Assessment) – Study the societal impacts of AI systems they have deployed, particularly by collaborating with external researchers, independent research organisations, and third party data owners. By collaborating and joining their data with that of third parties, such as internet platforms, frontier AI organisations can assess the impacts of their AI systems. Privacy-enhancing technologies could be employed to enable data sharing between frontier AI organisations, third parties, and external researchers while protecting confidential information. As well as data, frontier AI organisations could also facilitate research on the societal impacts of their AI systems by providing access to the necessary infrastructure and compute.\n\n[RISK-871] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-872] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-872] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-872] Debiasing methods (Model Safety Engineering) – Providers of AI models can apply techniques to reduce the biases of their models.Current debiasing methods focus on three main types of bias:• Racial and religious bias - Stereotypes based on religious beliefs or racial beliefs.• Gender bias - Stereotypes tied to gender roles and expectations.• Political and cultural bias - Propagation of dominant ideologies or extrem-ist attitudes.Debiasing methods can be categorized based on their application during AI development:• Data pre-processing - Removing or correcting unwanted and biased data, and augmenting quality data to offset data bias, such as rebalancing datasets with counterfactual data augmentation.• During training - Intervening on the training dynamics of the AI model, such as introducing debiasing terms in the objective function or by nega- tively reinforcing biased outputs.• Post-training - Applying techniques to correct a trained but biased model, such as modifying the embedding space.\n\n[RISK-872] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-872] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-873] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-873] Structured access to a model (Access Management) – Structured access refers to methods which limit users’ or deployers’ direct access to a model’s parameters by constraining access to a model through a centralized access point (e.g., an API) [88]. This access point can be monitored for usage, and access can be revoked to users or downstream deployers in cases of misuse [105].Within this centralized access point, automated filtering-based monitoring can be done on both inputs and outputs to ensure the model’s intended use is pre- served [36]. This filtering can sometimes be supplemented by human oversight, depending on desired robustness levels.\n\n[RISK-873] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-873] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-873] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-874] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-874] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-874] Evaluation of Predecessor Models (Testing & Auditing) – Before a frontier model is trained, evaluate predecessor or analogous models to understand how relevant properties (e.g. dangerous capabilities) scale with the overall size of the model. These preliminary evaluations can inform risk assessments.\n\n[RISK-874] Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability (Model Safety Engineering) – Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.\n\n[RISK-874] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-875] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-875] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-876] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-876] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-876] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-876] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-876] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-877] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-877] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-877] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-877] Quantifying privacy risks of AI models (Testing & Auditing) – Measuring privacy risks of an AI model allows the provider and user to calibrate their expectations on where the model can be applied, and it allows them to take the necessary steps to reduce such risks.For example, some metrics include:• Success rate of membership inference attacks [186] - Measures the rate that an attack correctly predicts a given record is part of the training dataset used to train a given AI model.• Discoverable memorization [38] - Theoretical upper-bound of the amount of training data that a given model memorizes. Assuming full knowledge of the training data, it measures the percentage of items that, for a given incomplete data point, a model outputs the remaining (memorized) part.\n\n[RISK-877] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-878] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-886] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-878] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-878] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-878] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-878] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-88] Audits with specific scoped goals (Testing & Auditing) – Audits of AI systems will be easier to perform, and have clearer results if the scope and goals of the evaluation are formulated as precisely as possible [157], or have a connection to concrete existing policy.\n\n[RISK-88] Third-Party Risk (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Policies and proceduresare in place that addressAI risks associated withthird-party entities,including risks ofinfringement of athird-party’s intellectualproperty or otherrights.\n\n[RISK-88] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-88] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-88] Monitor System in Production (Post-deployment Monitoring) – The functionalityand behavior of theAI system and itscomponents – asidentified in the Mapfunction – aremonitored when inproduction.\n\n[RISK-881] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-881] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-882] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-882] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-882] Restricted usage terms for open-source models (Access Management) – Developers of open-weights and open-source AI models can vet and restrict the users of their AI systems by requiring them to sign a Terms of Service agreement before getting access to the model weights. Such agreements can include limitations to the usage, modification, and proliferation of the AI model [88].Such agreements have the advantage that users only need to be vetted once before getting model access, but are often limited in practice in preventing unauthorised use or distribution.\n\n[RISK-882] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-882] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-883] Diverse data labeling and algorithm fairness audits (Data Governance) – To mitigate biases in AI models, model providers may want to prioritize di- versity among data labelers and conduct regular fairness audits on their algo- rithms. Data labeling teams that represent different backgrounds and demo- graphic groups can help create more balanced datasets.\n\n[RISK-883] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-883] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-883] Generated Code Review (Testing & Auditing) – Review GAI system outputs for validity and safety: Review generated code to assess risks that may arise from unreliable downstream decision-making.\n\n[RISK-883] Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the robustness of models to different classes of adversarial attack (such as poisoning, model inversion and model stealing), based on priorities derived from threat modelling. This could involve a combination of benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-884] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-884] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-884] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-884] Stakeholder Testing Involvement (Testing & Auditing) – Involve the end-users, practitioners, and operators in GAI system in prototyping and testing activities. Make sure these tests cover various scenarios, such as crisis situations or ethically sensitive contexts.\n\n[RISK-884] Red-Team for Dangerous Behaviors (Testing & Auditing) – Use red teams and adversarial testing as part of extensive interaction with GPAIS toidentify dangerous capabilities, vulnerabilities, or other emergent properties of suchsystems\n\n[RISK-885] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-885] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-886] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-886] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-887] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-887] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-888] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-888] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-888] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-888] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-888] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-889] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-889] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-889] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-889] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-889] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-89] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-89] Contamination detection (Data Governance) – Contamination detection refers to techniques for assessing whether and to what extent a given model has benchmark data in its training dataset [161, 170]. This can involve a set of technical and regulatory interventions to prevent or identify a model trained on contaminated data.For example, with web-crawled data, contamination detection can involve com- paring the data’s web sources against a dynamic, publicly available blocklist of websites known to generate new benchmarks. Additional measures may in- clude excluding data with improper metadata from the training dataset and conducting overlap analyses between the training data and all known standard benchmark datasets.\n\n[RISK-89] User data usage for training (Data Governance) – When using the default settings of your organization’s most capable AI chatbot, is the data that users submit as input to the system used to train AI models?\n\n[RISK-89] Reporting data decontamination efforts (Data Governance) – Decontamination analysis may involve comparing the training dataset with the benchmark data, and publishing a report with statistics such as data overlap [235]. Especially for already trained models, including contamination statistics can allow experts to reweigh the success of the model on affected benchmarks.\n\n[RISK-89] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-890] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-890] Robust Content Filters (Model Safety Engineering) – Invest in making content filters robust to “jailbreaking” attempts. Work to ensure filters are robust to jailbreaking attempts, for instance by including examples of jailbreaking attempts in the datasets used to develop filters.\n\n[RISK-890] Novel Risk Measurement Methods (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate novel methods and technologies for the measurement of GAI-related risks including in content provenance, offensive cyber, and CBRN, while maintaining the models' ability to produce valid, reliable, and factually accurate outputs.\n\n[RISK-890] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-890] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-891] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-891] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-891] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-891] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-891] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-892] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-892] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-892] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-892] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-892] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-893] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-893] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-893] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-893] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-893] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-894] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-894] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-895] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-895] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-895] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-895] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-895] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-897] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-897] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-897] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-897] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-897] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-898] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-898] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-898] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-898] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-898] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-899] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-899] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-9] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-9] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-9] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-9] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-901] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-9] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-90] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-90] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-90] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-90] Development and testing of Model Evaluation Methods (Testing & Auditing) – Support the development and testing of model evaluation methods. For many relevant properties of models, there do not yet exist accepted evaluation methods. It also remains unclear how reliable or predictive current evaluation methods are. This could involve frontier AI organisations developing model evaluation methods themselves or facilitating the efforts of others, such as by providing access to capable infrastructure for evaluation.\n\n[RISK-90] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-900] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-900] Harmful Data Removal from Training Sets (Data Governance) – When training large models, does your organization remove data that contains information related to dangerous capabilities or harmful outcomes from the training set? If yes, please select the categories of data that are removed.Detailed information about the development, acquisition, or dispersion of CBRN weaponsInstructional content for conducting cyberattacksHateful or discriminatory contentAdvice or encouragement for self-harmGraphic violent contentGraphic sexual contentPersonally Identifiable Information (PII)Detailed information about bomb-making or other terrorism-enabling tactics\n\n[RISK-900] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-900] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-900] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-901] Internal review (Governance Disclosure) – Information regarding internal review mechanisms and audit functions that are relevant to decisions about the development and deployment of highly capable AI models. This includes ethics boards, board risk committees, and audit functions that test risk management practices.\n\n[RISK-901] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-914] Advanced information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implementing advanced cybersecurity measures and insider threat safeguards to protect proprietary and unreleased model weights.\n\n[RISK-901] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-901] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-902] Contract Evaluation Clauses (Testing & Auditing) – Include clauses in contracts which allow an organization to evaluate third-party GAI processes and standards.\n\n[RISK-902] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-902] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-902] Paraphrasing to reduce hidden information (Model Safety Engineering) – Paraphrasing can mitigate steganography and encoded reasoning by reducing the physical size of the hidden information encoded in text [166].\n\n[RISK-902] Tracking model weights (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should have a system that is intended to track all copies of the weights of powerful models.*\n\n[RISK-903] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-903] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-903] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-903] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-903] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-904] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-904] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-904] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-923] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-904] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-904] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-905] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-905] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-905] Development Halt Plan (Safety Decision Frameworks) – Devise a plan to halt development or deployment of a GAI system that poses unacceptable negative risk.\n\n[RISK-906] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-906] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-906] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-906] Evaluations for truthful outputs (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be evaluated for truthfulness when answering questions, includ- ing in contexts where humans tend to give incorrect but widely-accepted answers (i.e., popular misconceptions). Evaluations detect incorrect facts learned about the world, inadequate capabilities of the AI system, and misleading outputs by the AI system [121].\n\n[RISK-906] Paraphrasing to reduce hidden information (Model Safety Engineering) – Paraphrasing can mitigate steganography and encoded reasoning by reducing the physical size of the hidden information encoded in text [166].\n\n[RISK-907] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-907] Audits for Dangerous System Capabilities (Data Governance) – Information that might enhance dangerous system capabilities, such as informationabout weapons manufacturing or terrorism.\n\n[RISK-909] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-909] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-91] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-91] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-910] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-910] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-911] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-911] Cost-Benefit Analysis of Sharing Vulnerability Reports (Risk Disclosure) – Consider the ways in which sharing information about vulnerabilities can both exacerbate and mitigate risks. Sharing can alert would-be attackers, but also alert would-be victims and actors with the power to create defences. One particularly important factor is how easily fixable the model vulnerability is. If a vulnerability will take a very long time to fix, or cannot be fixed, then public reporting may not be justified.\n\n[RISK-912] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-912] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-912] Internal data poisoning diagnosis (Incident Response & Recovery) – Providers can have an internal framework to identify what specific data poison- ing attack their model may be a victim of based on a set of symptoms, such as analysis of target algorithm and architecture, perturbation scope and dimen- sion, victim model, and data type [39]. This framework includes known defenses against the diagnosed attack, which providers can then apply to the model.\n\n[RISK-912] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-912] Restricted usage terms for open-source models (Access Management) – Developers of open-weights and open-source AI models can vet and restrict the users of their AI systems by requiring them to sign a Terms of Service agreement before getting access to the model weights. Such agreements can include limitations to the usage, modification, and proliferation of the AI model [88].Such agreements have the advantage that users only need to be vetted once before getting model access, but are often limited in practice in preventing unauthorised use or distribution.\n\n[RISK-913] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-913] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-913] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-913] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-913] Development and testing of Model Evaluation Methods (Testing & Auditing) – Support the development and testing of model evaluation methods. For many relevant properties of models, there do not yet exist accepted evaluation methods. It also remains unclear how reliable or predictive current evaluation methods are. This could involve frontier AI organisations developing model evaluation methods themselves or facilitating the efforts of others, such as by providing access to capable infrastructure for evaluation.\n\n[RISK-914] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-914] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-923] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-914] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-915] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-915] Dynamic benchmarking (Testing & Auditing) – Dynamic benchmarks are benchmarks that can be continuously updated with new human-generated data. By having one or more target models “in the loop,” examples for benchmarking can be generated with the intent of fooling these target models, or to assess if these models express an appropriate level of uncer- tainty [103]. As the dataset in the benchmark grows, previously benchmarked models can also be reassessed against the updated dataset to reflect its perfor- mance in a more representative manner.Examples of such dynamic benchmarks include DynaSent [153] for sentiment analysis, LFTW [208] for hate speech, and Human-Adversarial VQA [182] for images.\n\n[RISK-915] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-915] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-915] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-916] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-916] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-916] Default Necessary Security Updates (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Ensure necessary security updates are a default part of every product and use secure, modular update procedures to distribute them. This will help your products remain secure in the face of new and developing threats.\n\n[RISK-916] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-916] Criminality Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to prevent criminal behaviour through the use of AI systems (e.g. fraud, online child sexual abuse)\n\n[RISK-917] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-918] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-918] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-918] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-918] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-918] Monitoring and Safeguard Efficacy (Testing & Auditing) – Regularly assess monitoring and safeguard efficacy and continually invest in improvements. Regular assessment of monitoring efficacy can build an understanding of the success rate and speed of issue detection, on which deployment decisions may be based. These assessments may draw, for instance, on the results of internal and external red-teaming efforts, random audits of usage logs, what may be ‘best practice’, as well as available information about real-world misuse. Risks are expected to increase as AI capabilities increase. Hence, active increases in investment in safety, security, and monitoring may be valuable.\n\n[RISK-919] Updates to Government Authorities During Training (Risk Disclosure) – During training, update information provided to relevant government authorities with any significant changes to the model itself or its risk profile. This could include: 1) Updates on model development at each evaluation checkpoint as well as any significant updates to the development plan; 2) Results from model evaluations, including details of emergent dangerous capabilities and whether these were expected; 3) Whether and how the risk context has changed (e.g. if other AI tools have been published that the model could interact with)\n\n[RISK-919] Disclosure of Information with AI-focused Government Authorities (Risk Disclosure) – Share complete forms of this information with central AI-focused government authorities (including central government bodies, security agencies, and regulators) to enable robust government oversight of potentially high-risk areas of AI development and the processes in place to identify and manage those risks. These authorities could then further share information selectively with additional government bodies where relevant. Some particularly security-relevant pieces of information may need to be shared directly with security agencies, such as the cybersecurity measures being used in model development (which would make it easier for those agencies to identify potential security risks), or specific physical or cybersecurity threat incidents. Robust security measures are in place when sharing more sensitive information.\n\n[RISK-919] Adjucation around Publicly Sharing Risks (Risk Disclosure) – Before sharing information, consider the risks of sharing this information and judge whether it is inappropriate to share certain pieces of information. In particular, it is important to consider potential harms from publicly sharing information about dangerous capabilities and methods for eliciting them, as this information could motivate or help other actors to acquire these capabilities. It is also important to consider potential harms from publicly sharing detailed information about how models were produced, as this may lower barriers to producing similar models. If the models have or could be modified to possess dangerous capabilities (e.g. biological capabilities or surveillance capabilities), then facilitating widespread distribution of the model in this way may be harmful. The National Protective Security Authority (NPSA) guidance on a security-minded approach to information management may prove helpful for sharing information appropriately.\n\n[RISK-919] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-919] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-92] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-92] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-92] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-92] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-92] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-920] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-920] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-920] Research into other Societal Harms (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving our ability to prevent other societal harms arising from the use of AI systems, including psychological harm, misinformation, and other societal harms.\n\n[RISK-920] Novel Risk Measurement Methods (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate novel methods and technologies for the measurement of GAI-related risks including in content provenance, offensive cyber, and CBRN, while maintaining the models' ability to produce valid, reliable, and factually accurate outputs.\n\n[RISK-921] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-921] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-921] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-921] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-921] Paraphrasing to reduce hidden information (Model Safety Engineering) – Paraphrasing can mitigate steganography and encoded reasoning by reducing the physical size of the hidden information encoded in text [166].\n\n[RISK-922] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-922] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-922] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-922] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-923] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-923] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-923] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-924] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-924] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-924] AI output watermaking (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced by or with AI assistance can be marked to clearly identify its origin. Verification of the watermark can involve the use of statistical tests or having the mark immediately visible to a human inspector. Ideally, the watermark does not significantly alter the utility of the output, and is robust against digital and physical manipulation that results in data degradation [216, 145].\n\n[RISK-924] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-924] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-925] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-925] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-925] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-925] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-925] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-926] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-926] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-927] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-927] Deploying powerful models in stages (Staged Deployment) – Starting with a small number of applications and fewer users, and gradually scaling up API-access as rigorous monitoring increases confidence in the model's safety. An API-mediated staged release would also be required before open-sourcing a model.\n\n[RISK-927] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-927] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-927] Risk Tolerance Threshold (Safety Decision Frameworks) – In the second step of the framework, AI developers need first to set a risk tolerance, that is, a risk level that they commit to not overshoot.\n\n[RISK-93] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-93] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-930] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-930] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-930] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-930] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-930] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-931] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-931] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-931] User data usage for training (Data Governance) – When using the default settings of your organization’s most capable AI chatbot, is the data that users submit as input to the system used to train AI models?\n\n[RISK-931] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-931] Audits for Misinformation (Data Governance) – Training an AI system on inaccurate information increases the likelihood the outputs of the system will be inaccurate and could lead to harm.\n\n[RISK-932] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-933] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-933] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-933] Restrict web access during AI training (Model Safety Engineering) – Developers can restrict or disable AI systems’ internet access during training. For example, developers can restrict web access to read-only (e.g., by disabling write-access through HTTP POST requests and access to web forms) or limit the access of the AI system to a local network\n\n[RISK-933] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-934] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-935] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-935] Examine & Document Privacy Risk (Risk Management) – Privacy risk of the AIsystem – as identifiedin the Map function– is examined anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-936] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-936] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-936] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-936] Model Evaluation of Controllability (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for controllability issues (i.e. propensities to apply their capabilities in ways that neither the models’ users nor the models’ developers want). This could include, for example, autonomous replication and adaptation (i.e. capabilities that could allow a model to copy and run itself on other computer systems).\n\n[RISK-936] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-937] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-937] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-937] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-938] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-938] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-938] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-938] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-938] Safety Analysis (Model Safety Engineering) – Helps understand the dependencies between components and the system that they are part of, in order to anticipate how component failures could lead to system-level hazards.This approach is used across safety- critical fields, e.g. to anticipate and prevent aircraft crashes or nuclear reactor core meltdowns\n\n[RISK-939] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-950] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-939] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-939] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-939] Security Effectiveness Metrics (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Identify metrics that reflect the effectiveness of security measures, such as data provenance, the number of unauthorized access attempts, inference, bypass, extraction, penetrations, or provenance verification.\n\n[RISK-939] Security Vulnerability Assessment (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply established security measures to: Assess likelihood and magnitude of vulnerabilities and threats such as backdoors, compromised dependencies, data breaches, eavesdropping, man-in-the-middle attacks, reverse engineering, autonomous agents, model theft or exposure of model weights, AI inference, bypass, extraction, and other baseline security concerns.\n\n[RISK-94] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-94] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-94] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-94] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-94] Recurrent Performance analysis of AI models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Measure the performance of AI models and systems on an ongoing basis. A decline in model performance could be an indication of an attack or could indicate that a model is encountering data that is different from that which it was trained on. Either way, further investigation may be required.\n\n[RISK-941] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-941] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-942] Accounting of Applicable Regulatory Frameworks (Data Governance) – Before collecting training data, take account of applicable regulatory frameworks. This may involve, for example, establishing a legal basis to process training data and understanding any copyright considerations that might apply. This could help to mitigate risks further down the line, such as the system revealing personally identifiable information.\n\n[RISK-942] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-943] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-943] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-943] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-943] Interpretability and Explainability Research (Model Alignment) – Improving our ability to understand the inner functioning of AI systems and explain their behaviour\n\n[RISK-943] Robustness Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving the resilience of AI systems e.g. against attacks intended to disrupt their proper functioning\n\n[RISK-944] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-944] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-944] Red Teaming (Testing & Auditing) – An exercise in which a group of people or automated systems pretend to be an adversary and attack an organisation’s systems in order to identify vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-944] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-945] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-945] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-945] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-945] Security Vulnerability Assessment (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply established security measures to: Assess likelihood and magnitude of vulnerabilities and threats such as backdoors, compromised dependencies, data breaches, eavesdropping, man-in-the-middle attacks, reverse engineering, autonomous agents, model theft or exposure of model weights, AI inference, bypass, extraction, and other baseline security concerns.\n\n[RISK-945] Application of Infrastructure Principles (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Apply good infrastructure principles to every part of this process from design to decommissioning. This is important as threats can occur at different stages of an AI project’s life cycle.\n\n[RISK-946] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-946] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-946] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-946] Evaluation Research (Testing & Auditing) – Improving our ability to assess the capabilities, limitations, and safety- relevant features of AI systems\n\n[RISK-946] Safety Analysis (Model Safety Engineering) – Helps understand the dependencies between components and the system that they are part of, in order to anticipate how component failures could lead to system-level hazards.This approach is used across safety- critical fields, e.g. to anticipate and prevent aircraft crashes or nuclear reactor core meltdowns\n\n[RISK-947] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-947] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-947] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-951] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-947] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-947] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-948] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-949] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-949] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-949] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-949] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-949] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-95] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-95] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-95] Determine Deployment Readiness (Safety Decision Frameworks) – A determination ismade as to whetherthe AI system achievesits intended purposesand stated objectivesand whether itsdevelopment ordeployment shouldproceed.\n\n[RISK-95] Least Privilege access (Model Safety Engineering) – Deployers of an AI system can restrict its permissions to a whitelisted set of pre- determined options, such that all options not on the whitelist are not accessible to the AI [200, 146].The entries on the whitelist can be chosen to be as small as possible for the AI system to fulfill its intended purpose, to reduce the attack surface of external attackers, and to decrease the probability that the AI system accidentally takes actions with large unintended side-effects.For example, such whitelisting can apply to network connections, execution of other programs, access to knowledge bases, and the action space of the AI system. It can be implemented through running the AI system on an OS-level virtualization, on networks behind a firewall, and in extreme cases on air-gapped machines.\n\n[RISK-95] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-950] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-950] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-950] Human-AI Configuration; Information Integrity (Testing & Auditing) – Verify that AI Actors responsible for monitoring reported issues can effectivelyevaluate GAI system performance including the application of contentprovenance data tracking techniques, and promptly escalate issues for response.\n\n[RISK-950] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-951] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-952] Safety and Security Model Reports (Risk Disclosure) – Signatories should commit to reporting to the AI Office about their implementation of the Code, and especially the application of their Framework to the development, making available on the market, and/or use of their GPAISRs, by creating a Safety and Security Model Report, which will document: (1) the results of systemic risk assessment and mitigation for the model in question; and (2) justifications of decisions to make the model in question available on the market.\n\n[RISK-952] Document compute usage (System Documentation) – Given that computing power is key to frontier AI development (Sastry et al., 2024), frontier developers can be required to document their compute resources including details such as the usage, providers, and the location of compute clusters.\n\n[RISK-952] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-952] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-952] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-953] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-953] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-954] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-954] Documentation of data collection, annotation, maintenance practices (Data Governance) – Dataset collection, annotation, and maintenance processes can be documented in detail, including potential unintentional misuse scenarios and corresponding recommendations for data usage [80, 175, 99]. This contributes to transparency, ensures that inherent dataset limitations are known in advance, and helps in selecting the right datasets for intended use cases.\n\n[RISK-955] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-956] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-956] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-956] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-956] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-956] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-957] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-958] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-958] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-958] Demonstrate Validity & Generalization Limits (Testing & Auditing) – The AI system tobe deployed isdemonstrated to bevalid and reliable.Limitations of thegeneralizability beyondthe conditions underwhich the technologywas developed aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-958] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-958] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-959] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-959] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-959] Worst-case scenario Response (Incident Response & Recovery) – Implement processes and technical requirements to enable rapid rollback or withdrawal of models in case of egregious, widespread or consistent harms. Rolling back to a previous version of a model that does not suffer from the same misuse threats, or withdrawing access to a model altogether, may be proportionate actions in such situations of extreme threat or consistent misuse. Running periodic dry runs could increase preparedness for such situations.\n\n[RISK-959] Decommission AI Systems Safely (Incident Response & Recovery) – Processes andprocedures are in placefor decommissioningand phasing out AIsystems safely and in amannerthat does not increaserisks or decrease theorganization’strustworthiness.\n\n[RISK-959] Information Security (Incident Response & Recovery) – Establish and maintain procedures for escalating GAI system incidents to theorganizational risk management authority when specific criteria for deactivationor disengagement is met for a particular context of use or for the GAI system as awhole.\n\n[RISK-96] Staged release of model weights (Staged Deployment) – When a model is developed by a provider for use in a certain AI system, it may also be useful to release the model itself more widely. Such developers can follow a staged release approach, in which they first grant access to the model via an API to trusted partners or the public, in order to scope the models’ capabilities and detect harmful or dangerous features [195].After a period of an initial closed release and potentially further safety-training, the developers of the AI model can then release the weights, if they are confident that the AI model poses minimal systemic risk.\n\n[RISK-96] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-96] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-96] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-96] Monitor Pre-trained Models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Pre-trained modelswhich are used fordevelopmentare monitored as partof AI system regularmonitoring andmaintenance.\n\n[RISK-960] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-960] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-961] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-961] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-962] User data usage for training (Data Governance) – When using the default settings of your organization’s most capable AI chatbot, is the data that users submit as input to the system used to train AI models?\n\n[RISK-962] Information Integrity (System Documentation) – Document training data sources to trace the origin and provenance of AI generated content (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-962] Differential privacy (Data Governance) – Differential privacy techniques [8] can be used to protect users’ privacy by ensuring that sensitive information is not leaked from a training dataset, even after thorough statistical analysis. With differential privacy, noise is added to the dataset or the model’s output in such a way that one cannot deduce the presence or absence of a particular data point within the dataset. This provides individuals with plausible deniability and prevents their information from being exposed.\n\n[RISK-962] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-962] Reporting data decontamination efforts (Data Governance) – Decontamination analysis may involve comparing the training dataset with the benchmark data, and publishing a report with statistics such as data overlap [235]. Especially for already trained models, including contamination statistics can allow experts to reweigh the success of the model on affected benchmarks.\n\n[RISK-963] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-963] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-963] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-963] Evaluation of Different Content Filters (Testing & Auditing) – Explore and compare the efficacy of multiple approaches to developing content filters. This may involve comparing available methods, such as those in the following section; selecting the most effective ones; and applying them in combination if doing so substantially increases efficacy. Best practice could be shared with other frontier AI organisations or published broadly.\n\n[RISK-963] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-964] Reporting of Broad Patterns of Misuse (Risk Disclosure) – Report information on broad patterns of misuse. This information can help governments, the public, and other frontier AI organisations to better understand risks. Reports may focus on population-level metrics related to API usage, such as the rate of harmful inputs filtered or the number of users banned for misuse. This information may benefit from being presented in a clear and understandable way and made optimally accessible.\n\n[RISK-965] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-965] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-965] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-965] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-965] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-966] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-966] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-966] Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate the robustness of models to different classes of adversarial attack (such as poisoning, model inversion and model stealing), based on priorities derived from threat modelling. This could involve a combination of benchmarking and red teaming.\n\n[RISK-966] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-966] Robustness Research (Model Safety Engineering) – Improving the resilience of AI systems e.g. against attacks intended to disrupt their proper functioning\n\n[RISK-967] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-967] Pre-training and Fine-tuning of Models (Testing & Auditing) – During pre-training and fine-tuning, evaluate the model to detect signs of undesirable properties and identify inaccuracies in pretraining predictions. These evaluations could be undertaken at various pre-specified checkpoints, and could inform decisions about whether to pause or adjust the training process.\n\n[RISK-967] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-967] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-967] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-968] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-968] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-969] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-969] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-969] Cybersecurity Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to ensure the security of AI systems\n\n[RISK-969] Privacy Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to address the privacy risks associated with AI systems\n\n[RISK-969] Criminality Research (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Improving our ability to prevent criminal behaviour through the use of AI systems (e.g. fraud, online child sexual abuse)\n\n[RISK-97] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-994] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-97] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-97] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-97] Monitor Pre-trained Models (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Pre-trained modelswhich are used fordevelopmentare monitored as partof AI system regularmonitoring andmaintenance.\n\n[RISK-97] Domain Adaptation Risk Assessment (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Re-evaluate risks when adapting GAI models to new domains. Additionally, establish warning systems to determine if a GAI system is being used in a new domain where previous assumptions (relating to context of use or mapped risks such as security, and safety) may no longer hold.\n\n[RISK-970] Staged release of model weights (Staged Deployment) – When a model is developed by a provider for use in a certain AI system, it may also be useful to release the model itself more widely. Such developers can follow a staged release approach, in which they first grant access to the model via an API to trusted partners or the public, in order to scope the models’ capabilities and detect harmful or dangerous features [195].After a period of an initial closed release and potentially further safety-training, the developers of the AI model can then release the weights, if they are confident that the AI model poses minimal systemic risk.\n\n[RISK-970] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-970] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-970] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-970] Built-in Tools for Harm Mitigation (Model Safety Engineering) – When a probable and consequential harm from a frontier AI organisation’s system is identified, investigate whether there are tools that can be built to mitigate this harm. For instance, recognizing the rise in AI-generated child exploitation and abuse content, some social media platforms are developing tools for identifying and removing child sexual abuse content.\n\n[RISK-971] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-971] Release strategy disagreement between developers (Governance Disclosure) – Developers of restricted-access models with similar capabilities may disagree about the strategy or precautions to take for model release, especially in the case of competitive pressure or minimal safety regulation oversight. In such a case, if only a single developer releases an equally capable model unrestricted, malicious actors can use it instead of restricted-access alternatives [88].\n\n[RISK-971] AI output metadata (Content Safety Controls) – Output produced or whose production is aided by AI can contain metadata to record its origin and the transformations it has undergone. Metadata is evidence that subsequent versions or its derivatives come from this original version. The metadata can include the original AI model source, along with ownership, and its subsequent edits [169]. For example, an image produced by an AI model can contain metadata showing the date of creation and the AI model that produced it. Subsequent versions can reference this information and, if they are intermediary versions, can include descriptions of any editing that has taken place.\n\n[RISK-971] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-971] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-972] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-972] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-972] Training Data Diligence (Data Governance) – Conduct appropriate diligence on training data use to assess intellectual property, and privacy, risks, including to examine whether use of proprietary or sensitive training data is consistent with applicable laws.\n\n[RISK-972] Limitations of Input Data Audit (Data Governance) – Acknowledge the limitations of input data audit. Techniques for understanding the impacts of data and filtering out specific data are limited (e.g. excluding information from a dataset does not always prevent the AI system from reasoning about or discovering that information). Other risk mitigation measures will be required and further research on improving data input audit techniques is important.\n\n[RISK-972] Audits on Datasets for Customer Fine-Tuning (Data Governance) – Conduct audits on datasets used by their customers to fine-tune AI systems. Customers are often allowed to fine-tune systems on their own datasets. By carrying out audits to ensure that customers are not encouraging undesirable behaviours, frontier AI organisations can use their expertise and insight into the AI system’s original training data to identify potential harms upstream. It is important that frontier AI organisations are mindful of privacy concerns and make use of privacy preserving techniques, where appropriate.\n\n[RISK-973] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-973] Data curation (Data Governance) – Careful data curation prior to all development stages (including fine-tuning) to filter out high-risk content and ensure the training data is sufficiently high-quality.\n\n[RISK-973] Data Provenance Verification (Data Governance) – Verify GAI system training data and TEVV data provenance, and that fine-tuning or retrieval-augmented generation data is grounded\n\n[RISK-973] Training Data Diligence (Data Governance) – Conduct appropriate diligence on training data use to assess intellectual property, and privacy, risks, including to examine whether use of proprietary or sensitive training data is consistent with applicable laws.\n\n[RISK-973] User data usage for training (Data Governance) – When using the default settings of your organization’s most capable AI chatbot, is the data that users submit as input to the system used to train AI models?\n\n[RISK-974] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-974] Digital Content Transparency (Content Safety Controls) – Use digital content transparency solutions to enable the documentation of each instance where content is generated, modified, or shared to provide a tamper-proof history of the content, promote transparency, and enable traceability. Robust version control systems can also be applied to track changes across the AI lifecycle over time.\n\n[RISK-974] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-974] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-974] Informative and powerful benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – Developers of GPAI systems can select benchmarks that are difficult enough to be informative about the capabilities of their AI systems, and cover a large spec- trum of domains in order to signal areas where the GPAI system is performing poorly [120].Suitable benchmarks contain no label errors, are not vulnerable to being bench- mark contaminants, and are often audited by independent domain experts if they contain domain-specific questions. For multimodal GPAI systems, good benchmarks cover every modality, especially the interaction of different modalities.\n\n[RISK-975] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-976] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-976] Removal of Harmful Input Data (Data Governance) – Remove potentially harmful or undesirable input data, where appropriate. Given the increasingly strong generalisation abilities of AI systems, data curation may prove insufficient to prevent dangerous system behaviour but could provide an additional layer of defence alongside other measures such as fine-tuning and content filters.\n\n[RISK-98] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-976] Monitoring and Safeguard Efficacy (Testing & Auditing) – Regularly assess monitoring and safeguard efficacy and continually invest in improvements. Regular assessment of monitoring efficacy can build an understanding of the success rate and speed of issue detection, on which deployment decisions may be based. These assessments may draw, for instance, on the results of internal and external red-teaming efforts, random audits of usage logs, what may be ‘best practice’, as well as available information about real-world misuse. Risks are expected to increase as AI capabilities increase. Hence, active increases in investment in safety, security, and monitoring may be valuable.\n\n[RISK-976] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-976] Data Audits for AI System Behavior (Testing & Auditing) – Use data audits to improve understanding of how training data affects AI system behaviour. For example, if model evaluations reveal a potentially dangerous capability, data audits can help ascertain the extent to which the training data contributed to it.\n\n[RISK-977] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-977] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-978] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-978] Testing for erroneous or irrelevant features through concept learning (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques, particularly concept learning [77], can be used to test whether a model is learning erroneous features or relying on irrelevant features in its predictions. This can help identify and mitigate potential risks associated with incorrect or non-informative features influencing the model’s outputs.\n\n[RISK-978] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-978] Evaluating AI systems’ performance on self-proliferation-related tasks (Testing & Auditing) – To prevent AI systems from self-proliferating, developers of AI systems can evaluate those systems for their capability to engage in self-proliferation-related tasks. This type of evaluation might assess an AI system’s ability to replicate its com- ponents (including model weights, structural scaffolding, etc.) onto other local or cloud infrastructures prior to deployment. Additionally, it may test the system’s capacity to purchase cloud credits and configure virtual machines on a cloud platform. The evaluation could offer a predefined environment (such as a virtual container) to facilitate self-replication, providing access to the system’s own components, a network connection to a resource-equipped external computer, and other necessary resources [12, 106].Self-proliferation evaluations can be conducted in a secure environment to pre- vent a self-replicating AI system from affecting other computers.\n\n[RISK-978] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-979] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-979] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-979] Security standards (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AGI labs should comply with information security standards (e.g. ISO/IEC 27001 or NIST Cybersecurity Framework). These standards need to be tailored to an AGI context.\n\n[RISK-979] Require security clearances and have private investigators conduct security checks (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Does your organization defend against insider threats by requiring security clearances or by having private investigators conduct background checks? Please select which interventions are applied when hiring or appointing individuals for the groups listed below.- Members of the board of directors- All staff with access to model weights- Certain key employees- All Staff\n\n[RISK-979] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-98] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-98] Monitoring of model capabilities (Post-deployment Monitoring) – AI models are often trained to develop specific capabilities by using appropriate training data and training goals. However, models may develop capabilities that they were not specifically trained for. One subset of this is emergent capabilities, i.e., capabilities that emerged in larger models but not smaller models given a similar training process [215].These capabilities can be monitored, allowing models to be tested not only for their intended capabilities but also for capabilities that are not intended.\n\n[RISK-98] Context-Based Impact Measures (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Determine context-based measures to identify if new impacts are present due to the GAI system, including regular engagements with downstream AI Actors to identify and quantify new contexts of unanticipated impacts of GAI systems.\n\n[RISK-98] Safety of The Intended Function (Model Safety Engineering) – An approach that requires engineers to provide evidence that a system is safe when operating as intended.This approach is used in many engineering fields, such as in the construction and testing of road vehicles.\n\n[RISK-980] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-980] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-980] Modification of model internal representation (Model Safety Engineering) – Model providers can modify the internal representation of the model [135, 237] up to the granular level and in consultation with other tools that aid in under- standing what the internal representation of the model is.For example, if a model that is meant to give factual health data learned an incorrect fact, the model provider can use Linear Artificial Tomography (LAT) [237] to identify the representations responsible for the incorrect fact, and then modify that representation via modifying individual weights, or modify the en- tire representation itself by modifying entire layers.\n\n[RISK-980] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-980] Red teaming to test the resilience of open-weights models to fine-tuning (Testing & Auditing) – Before the release of open-weights models, red teamers can test the resilience of safety training against fine-tuning. Safety training may be partially or fully overridden by fine-tuning intentionally (e.g., by malicious actors) or uninten- tionally (e.g., by fine tuning an AI model for a specific use case)\n\n[RISK-981] Audits for Harmful Content (Data Governance) – Harmful content, such as child sexual abuse materials, hate speech, or online abuse. Having a better understanding of harmful content in datasets can inform safety measures (e.g. by highlighting domains where additional safeguards like content filters should be applied).\n\n[RISK-981] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Assessment (Data Governance) – Assess the proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data and verifytraining data is not overly homogenous or GAI-produced to mitigate concerns ofmodel collapse.\n\n[RISK-981] Human-AI Configuration (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Use human moderation systems where appropriate to review generated contentin accordance with human-AI configuration policies established in the Governfunction, aligned with socio-cultural norms in the context of use, and for settingswhere AI models are demonstrated to perform poorly.\n\n[RISK-981] External Network Analysis (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Observe and analyze how the GAI system interacts with external networks, and identify any potential for negative externalities, particularly where content provenance might be compromised.\n\n[RISK-981] Monitor user interactions and remove access (Post-deployment Monitoring) – Does your organization monitor user interactions with its most capable AI systems to ban accounts that use the system for harmful or illegal purposes?\n\n[RISK-982] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-982] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-982] Evaluating explainability method sensitivity to data inputs and model parameters (Testing & Auditing) – Model parameter and data randomization tests can be employed as sanity checks [2] to determine the relationship between a model, its inputs, and the explainability method. If an explanation is independent from or insensitive to the underlying model or the input data, it would indicate a lack of reliability of the explainability method.\n\n[RISK-982] Information Security; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Model Safety Engineering) – Evaluate GAI content and data for representational biases and employtechniques such as re-sampling, re-ranking, or adversarial training to mitigatebiases in the generated content. (AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring).\n\n[RISK-982] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-983] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-983] Assessment of Training Data (Data Governance) – Assess the overall composition of training data. This could include the data sources, the provenance of the data, indicators of data quality and integrity, and measures of bias and representativeness. The amount and variety of data are simple, reliable predictors of risk, and provide an additional line of defence where more targeted assessments are limited.\n\n[RISK-984] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-984] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-985] Differential privacy (Data Governance) – Differential privacy techniques [8] can be used to protect users’ privacy by ensuring that sensitive information is not leaked from a training dataset, even after thorough statistical analysis. With differential privacy, noise is added to the dataset or the model’s output in such a way that one cannot deduce the presence or absence of a particular data point within the dataset. This provides individuals with plausible deniability and prevents their information from being exposed.\n\n[RISK-985] Use of Synthetic Data (Data Governance) – Synthetic data refers to data that is not collected from the real world. It is used to train AI models as an alternative to, or augmentation of, natural data. Effective use and generation of synthetic data allows for more oversight by the trainer on the training dataset because they have more control over its statistical properties. Synthetic data can help against dataset bias by having more samples from a particular distribution or minority group. It can also help in privacy by having more samples to mask sensitive data\n\n[RISK-986] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-986] Interpretability techniques that target deception (Testing & Auditing) – Interpretability techniques can be used for finding the root causes of outputs of an AI model that reliably lead to false beliefs for its users (e.g., deceptive behavior) [180]. It is often difficult to distinguish a deceptive AI model from an honest AI model, since absence of deception and very sophisticated (hard to detect) deception may appear behaviorally similar. Interpretability techniques and tools can be used to detect whether AI model outputs arise from internal computations representing deception. This can apply in cases of purposely trained deception by the developer or if it emerges unintentionally during training. These interpretability tools can come from mechanistic interpretability, such as identification of features involved in generating the outputs, or attribution of parts of the input most important in generating the output [129].\n\n[RISK-986] Pop-up interventions in LLMs (Model Safety Engineering) – Supplementary information can be shown to the user in specific query topics where factual accuracy is critical. This intervention can effectively divert users from potential inaccuracies generated by AI models in sensitive contexts. For example, during electoral processes, where model hallucinations can be particu- larly costly or have a negative impact on society, LLMs can offer their users the option of being redirected to accurate and up-to-date information sources [11].Since pop-up interventions can be intrusive to workflows, they are best used in situations where the benefits of the information outweigh the cost of distraction.\n\n[RISK-986] Robust Watermarks for AI generated content (Content Safety Controls) – Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations. Explore the use of watermarks for AI generated content that are robust to various perturbations after their creation, including attempts at removal. To make the removal of watermarks more difficult, developers of generative AI models may need to consider how they distribute certain information about their watermarking methods or open-sourcing their classifiers. This may also include monitoring ways in which adversarial users are attempting to scrub off their watermarks and patching, where relevant, such means of circumvention. It also includes a recognition that watermarking however may not be appropriate in all circumstances given the limitations.\n\n[RISK-986] Military-grade information security (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The information security of AGI labs should be proportional to the capabilities of their models, eventually matching or exceeding that of intelligence agencies (e.g. sufficient to defend against nation states).\n\n[RISK-987] Trustworthy AI practices (1.X Governance & Oversight Control not otherwise categorized) – The characteristicsof trustworthy AIare integrated intoorganizational policies,processes, procedures,and practices.\n\n[RISK-987] Pre-Deployment Evaluations (Testing & Auditing) – After training, subject the model to extensive pre-deployment evaluations. These evaluations can inform decisions about whether and how to deploy the system, as well as allowing governments and potential users to make informed decisions about regulating or using the model. Their intensity will be proportional to the risk of the deployment, taking into account the model’s capabilities, novelty, expected domains of use, and number of individuals expected to be affected by it.\n\n[RISK-987] Benchmarks (Testing & Auditing) – A standardised, often quantitative test or metric used to evaluate and compare the performance of AI systems on a fixed set of tasks designed to represent real- world usage\n\n[RISK-987] Benchmarks for Situational Awareness (Testing & Auditing) – A model is situationally aware if it internally represents that it is a machine learning model and if it can accurately infer or act on model-relevant facts - e.g., if it is currently in training, testing, evaluation or deployment, or the desired outcome of an evaluation. Some benchmarks exist for situational awareness of AI models, which test whether the AI models can classify stereotypical inputs from training, test- ing, evaluation and deployment as such, and whether the AI model can use this information correctly to take actions in the world [25, 111].\n\n[RISK-987] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-988] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-988] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-988] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-988] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-988] Protect proprietary or unreleased AI model ar- chitecture and parameters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – The developers of AI models can invest in cybersecurity to prevent compute re- sources, training source code, model weights, and other critical resources from being accessed and copied by unauthorized third parties (e.g., through insider threats or supply chain attacks).Access to model source code and weights can be restricted through an access control scheme, such as role-based access control. If access to model outputs by third parties is required, it can be provided through an API. Air gaps can block unauthorized remote access. In the case of necessary interaction with an external network, network bandwidth limitations can also be enforced to increase the detection window of potential breaches [108].\n\n[RISK-989] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-989] Implement a privacy incident detection and response (Data Governance) – Deploy monitoring and response mechanisms to detect and address potential privacy exposures, including PII leak detection, sensitive information monitoring, and privacy incident handling procedures.\n\n[RISK-989] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-989] Using an AI model to evaluate AI model outputs (Testing & Auditing) – In cases where the outputs of AI models cannot be easily evaluated, AI mod- els can be used to evaluate their outputs or the outputs of other AI models [82, 16, 17, 91]. The evaluations can then provide a training signal to improve the original model’s performance or offer explanations of the output for human users.\n\n[RISK-989] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-99] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-99] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-99] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-99] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-99] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-990] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-990] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-990] Independent Evaluation Policy (Testing & Auditing) – Policies are in place to bolster oversight of GAI systems with independent evaluations or assessments of GAI models or systems where the type and robustness of evaluations are proportional to the identified risks.\n\n[RISK-990] Involve Independent & External Assessors (Testing & Auditing) – Internal experts whodid not serve asfront-line developersfor the system and/or independentassessors are involvedin regular assessmentsand updates. Domainexperts, users, AIactors external to theteam that developedor deployed the AIsystem, and affectedcommunities areconsulted in supportof assessmentsas necessary perorganizational risktolerance.\n\n[RISK-990] Validate Trustworthiness with Expert Input (Testing & Auditing) – Measurement resultsregarding AI systemtrustworthiness indeployment context(s)and across the AIlifecycle are informedby input from domainexperts and relevantAI actors to validatewhether the system isperforming consistentlyas intended. Results aredocumented.\n\n[RISK-991] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-991] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-992] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-992] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-992] Security mitigations (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Signatories commit to implementing state-of-the-art security mitigations designed to thwart such unauthorised access by well-resourced and motivated non-state-level adversaries, including insider threats from humans or AI systems, so as to meet at least the RAND SL3 security goal or equivalent, and achieve higher security goals (e.g., RAND SL4 or SL5)\n\n[RISK-992] Evaluate Security & Resilience (Model & Infrastructure Security) – AI system security andresilience – as identifiedin the Map function– are evaluated anddocumented.\n\n[RISK-993] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-993] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-993] Adversarial training (Model Safety Engineering) – Adversarial training [83] is a technique for training AI models in which adver- sarial inputs are generated for a model, and the model is then trained to give the correct outputs for those adversarial inputs.Adversarial training can involve adversarial examples generated by human experts, human users, or other AI systems.\n\n[RISK-993] Limit deployment scope (Access Management) – AI models can be restricted in terms of its use cases, where providers can require the deployers to limit its deployment to a predefined scope [81], such that models built for specific purposes and tested under specific environments are not used in environments or for purposes that are potentially unsafe.\n\n[RISK-993] Model Evaluation of Societal Harm (Testing & Auditing) – Evaluate models for societal harms. These could include, for example, bias and discrimination (e.g. the risk that they produce content that reinforces harmful stereotypes or their potential discriminatory influence if used to inform decisions), recognising that ‘bias’ can be difficult to define and can be subject to different interpretations in different contexts.\n\n[RISK-994] Use of copyrighted data in training (Data Governance) – Many artists, writers, programmers, journalists, photographers, musicians, and filmmakers complain that AI models are trained on their copyrighted works without consent, compensation, or attribution, offering rival services that harm their ability to make a living. Does your organization engage in such practices?\n\n[RISK-994] Hardware limitations on data center network connections (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Hardware-enforced bandwidth limitations on data center network connections can protect AI model weights from unauthorized access or exfiltration, by lim- iting the speed of model weight access on the connections between data centers and the outside world.Such limitations can be put in place in multiple ways, for example by only constructing connections with a specific bandwidth. The output rate on all data channels can be set low enough that copying the weights is possible in principle (e.g., to enable regular backups), but would take so long that an unauthorized exfiltration of the weights could be detected and prevented.Such rate-limiting is only effective if it applies to all output connections for all storage locations on which the weights of the model are stored [139].\n\n[RISK-994] Discretion for Development, Storage, and Sharing of Content Filters (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Exercise appropriate caution when developing, storing, or sharing content filters – and any specialised datasets used to produce them. In some cases, components of filters or datasets used to create them can be used to train higher-risk models. For instance, a classifier that evaluates the aggressiveness of a model’s outputs may be used to train the model to be more aggressive.\n\n[RISK-994] Cybersecurity protocols to prevent the model from being unintentionally stolen (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Implement reasonable administrative, technical, and physicalcybersecurity protections to prevent unauthorized access to, misuseof, or unsafe post-training modifications of, the covered modeland all covered model derivatives controlled by the developer thatare appropriate in light of the risks associated with the coveredmodel, including from advanced persistent threats or othersophisticated actors.\n\n[RISK-995] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-995] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-995] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-995] AI model-assisted oversight of AI systems (Model Alignment) – AI model-assisted oversight can help monitor and supervise the training of increasingly capable GPAI systems, which may become difficult to oversee at scale by human supervisors during training or testing. Monitoring and supervision may become especially difficult in cases where increasingly advanced GPAIs perform near or above human level in some specialized domains, where supervision quality might fail to keep pace with capabilities improvement. The training signal may include labeled data, reward function, and user feedback on produced outputs. Currently, there are two broad approaches to provide scalable training signals to such systems:1. Scalable oversight: Improving of the supervisor’s capabilities to supervise, such that they can provide accurate training signals quickly and at scale [31].For example, a debate format can be used between two GPAI systems (two instances of the same GPAI, or similarly capable systems). A Human supervisor judges the debate, making it easier to assess correct responses in domains which might otherwise require significant time investment of domain specific expertise [102].2. Weak-to-strong generalization: Enhance the training signals while ensur- ing that the enhanced signal remains faithful to the intentions of the orig- inal human-provided signal [37].For example, a hierarchical (“bootstrapping”) oversight approach can be implemented: A series of GPAI models with increasing capabilities are used, where each model in the hierarchy provides oversight for the next more capable model. The least capable model at the base of the hierarchy is the only one directly overseen by human supervisors, as it is easier to oversee than the more capable models.\n\n[RISK-995] Fine-Tuning Models (Model Alignment) – Fine-tune models to reduce the tendency to produce harmful outputs. This may involve using reinforcement learning from human or AI-generated feedback regarding the appropriateness of different model outputs e.g. a constitutional based approach where human input to the fine-tuning process is provided by a list of principles. It may also involve fine-tuning models on curated datasets of appropriate responses to prompts. Where human feedback is used, the mental wellbeing of moderators may need to be considered.\n\n[RISK-996] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-996] Data Privacy; Intellectual Property; Information Integrity; Confabulation; Harmful Bias and Homogenization (Data Governance) – Consider opportunities to responsibly use synthetic data and other privacyenhancing techniques in GAI development, where appropriate and applicable,match the statistical properties of real-world data without disclosing personallyidentifiable information or contributing to homogenization.\n\n[RISK-997] Sourcing or Generation of Additional Data for Training Dataset (Data Governance) – Consider sourcing or generating additional data and adding it to the training dataset, where it is determined that data is missing or inadequate. Improving the representativeness of training data can improve performance and reduce potential negative societal impacts and discriminatory effects. However, additional data should only be sought through appropriate means that respect and empower those individuals who are missing from the data.\n\n[RISK-997] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.\n\n[RISK-997] Encouraging downstream provider to evaluate models for deployment-specific failure modes (Testing & Auditing) – In some cases, AI system deployers are better positioned to perform certain risk management measures on the AI model in a provided AI system, relative to upstream model providers. For example, they understand their use case better and are more easily able to predict foreseeable misuse or failure modes. These evaluations can inform upstream model providers, or inform supplementary mitigations by the deployer.\n\n[RISK-997] Tracking Tools for Security and Version Control Assets (Model & Infrastructure Security) – Have processes and tools to track, authenticate, secure and version control assets and be able to roll back to a known safe state in the event of a compromise. This is important as data and models do not remain static during the whole lifespan of an AI project.\n\n[RISK-997] GPAI models explaining model outputs in a zero-sum debate game (Model Alignment) – Debate is a technique that aims to produce reliable explanations of AI model outputs that are too complicated for humans to understand, by letting two GPAI models role-playing in a debate produce an explanation in a dialogue [102].For example, an AI model may produce an output which is time-consuming for humans to verify as doing so may require going through extensive sources.Given such an output, the developer can use two natural language AI systems in an adversarial two-player setup to explain the output. These two natural language AI systems can be copies of the AI model that produced the output.In this setup, one AI system gives a short explanation of the output. The second AI system responds to this explanation with a counter-explanation or an argument why the first explanation was not correct. This continues for a fixed number of turns, with the two AI systems pointing out inconsistencies in each others’ explanations.After this sequence of statements, a human or an AI judge evaluates the ex- planations of both AI systems to determine which one is more convincing. The results of this debate can then be used for further training of the models via reinforcement learning, where outputs that are more truthful and convincing arguments and explanations are positively reinforced, while misleading or false outputs are negatively reinforced.\n\n[RISK-998] Preventing or mitigating data contamination and leakage (Data Governance) – Developers of GPAIS and creators of benchmarks can take actions to prevent AI models from being trained on contaminated or leaked data, or mitigate such data contamination and leakage.For example, developers of AI models can try to find and remove contaminated or leaked data from the training corpus, and creators of benchmarks can help them by adding globally unique “canary strings” to the documents containing their benchmarks, which makes them easier to find [197]. More involved inter- ventions by benchmark-creators include restricting access to benchmarks over an API, or continually updating benchmarks to focus on recent data.\n\n[RISK-998] Harmful Bias and Homogenization Data Review (Data Governance) – Review, document, and measure sources of bias in GAI training and TEVV data:Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, includingintersecting groups; Completeness, representativeness, and balance of datasources; demographic group and subgroup coverage in GAI system trainingdata; Forms of latent systemic bias in images, text, audio, embeddings, or othercomplex or unstructured data; Input data features that may serve as proxies fordemographic group membership (i.e., image metadata, language dialect) orotherwise give rise to emergent bias within GAI systems; The extent to whichthe digital divide may negatively impact representativeness in GAI systemtraining and TEVV data; Filtering of hate speech or content in GAI systemtraining data; Prevalence of GAI-generated data in GAI system training data\n\n[RISK-999] Audits for Private or Sensitive Information (Data Governance) – AI systems may be subject to data extraction attacks, where determined users can prompt systems to reveal pieces of training data, or may even reveal this information accidentally. This makes it important to know whether datasets include private or sensitive information e.g. names, addresses, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n[RISK-999] Sandboxing of AI Systems (Testing & Auditing) – AI systems can be developed and tested within a sandbox, (a secure and iso- lated environment used for separating running programs), such that outside access to information within the sandbox is restricted. Within this environ- ment, resources such as storage and memory space, and network access, would be disallowed or heavily restricted [15]. With sandboxing, dangerous or harmful outputs generated during testing will be contained.\n\n[RISK-999] Audits for Biases in the Data (Data Governance) – Training data that is imbalanced or inaccurate can result in an AI system being less accurate for people with certain personal characteristics or providing a skewed picture of particular groups. Ensuring a better balance in the training data could help to address this.	2ccbe112-29af-4fd4-88fb-416749a54102	[6457, 13754, 13755, 13636, 13637, 5565, 5564, 5563, 13516, 13517, 3905, 3915, 3916, 3926, 13173, 13174, 13175, 3, 1, 2, 12788, 12784, 12785, 9589, 9588, 14199, 14200, 9587, 13811, 13812, 6780, 6779, 6778, 13813, 13814, 6795, 6793, 29, 3821, 6794, 13518, 13519, 12786, 12787, 17, 16, 15, 3724, 3725, 13372, 13373, 3723, 10397, 12789, 26, 27, 40, 12790, 12791, 38, 39, 12792, 12793, 52, 51, 50, 12794, 64, 12795, 63, 62, 12796, 77, 76, 75, 74, 13407, 13408, 3946, 13409, 13410, 12797, 12798, 90, 89, 88, 14526, 14527, 12799, 12800, 101, 100, 99, 13464, 13639, 12805, 13465, 4381, 4382, 4380, 13176, 13177, 13178, 13179, 5578, 13638, 5577, 5576, 5573, 14201, 14202, 14203, 5587, 5586, 5585, 5583, 13815, 13816, 6801, 6802, 6798, 12801, 12802, 112, 113, 6814, 6815, 114, 3962, 3961, 13411, 13412, 3959, 14204, 14205, 13817, 6817, 6816, 13180, 13181, 2551, 2550, 2549, 13520, 13521, 4387, 4388, 4389, 4390, 4391, 13818, 13819, 6825, 4976, 6826, 6824, 13374, 13375, 13413, 13414, 12803, 12804, 123, 124, 125, 14206, 14207, 4977, 13522, 4973, 4975, 132, 133, 134, 135, 12024, 12025, 12023, 2561, 13182, 13183, 2560, 2559, 12148, 12806, 12807, 148, 147, 146, 14208, 14209, 4991, 4990, 13523, 13524, 4987, 4395, 4396, 14383, 10722, 10723, 10724, 10725, 13345, 13346, 12808, 12809, 13640, 13641, 10730, 10732, 10733, 10729, 10731, 12810, 12811, 168, 170, 165, 14619, 12110, 12111, 12107, 12108, 4413, 13466, 4412, 4411, 4410, 10746, 14386, 14387, 10742, 10743, 14056, 14057, 8478, 8479, 6