y incident databases documenting cases of AI-enabled manipulation or deception, (10) Regulatory guidance and standards related to AI transparency and trustworthiness. Evidence should prioritize empirical observations from deployed systems while incorporating theoretical frameworks from academic research.	Intentional	Other	technical	Technical Risks	Security Risk; Data Risk
25	RISK-102	AI System Exploitation for Harmful Information	Human-Computer Interaction	This risk addresses the potential for AI systems to provide information or guidance on harmful, immoral, or illegal activities when solicited by users. As AI systems become more capable and are deployed across diverse domains, they may inadvertently facilitate harmful actions by providing detailed instructions, normalizing unethical behavior, or failing to recognize and refuse inappropriate requests. This represents a content safety and alignment challenge where the AI's helpfulness objective may conflict with safety constraints, particularly when users employ social engineering techniques or exploit edge cases in content filtering systems.	This risk manifests when AI systems fail to maintain appropriate content safety boundaries when responding to user queries about harmful activities. The technical challenge involves several components: (1) Prompt injection and jailbreaking techniques where users craft inputs to bypass safety filters, (2) Ambiguous boundary cases where legitimate educational content overlaps with potentially harmful information, (3) Context-dependent harm where information may be harmful in certain contexts but legitimate in others, (4) Multi-turn conversation exploitation where users gradually escalate requests across multiple interactions to circumvent single-query filters. The underlying issue stems from the difficulty of encoding nuanced ethical reasoning into AI systems and the inherent tension between maximizing user satisfaction (reward hacking potential) and maintaining safety constraints. Detection requires analyzing both explicit content violations and subtle patterns indicating systematic boundary testing or filter evasion attempts.	AI systems face increasing risk of being exploited to provide information on harmful, immoral, or illegal activities through social engineering and content filter evasion techniques. As these systems become more capable and