s. Mitigation requires robust content policies, continuous monitoring, data governance to prevent contamination, and equitable access to AI safety tools across all communities.	Attack vectors include: (1) Direct prompting: Users explicitly request criminal guidance through natural language queries, (2) Prompt injection: Malicious instructions embedded in user inputs that override safety guidelines, (3) Jailbreaking: Multi-turn c	Observable indicators include: (1) Content policy violations: Automated detection of outputs matching criminal activity patterns (fraud schemes, identity theft methods, IP circumvention), (2) Unusual query patterns: Spike in requests for sensitive topics from specific user segments or geographic regions, (3) Jailbreak attempt signatures: Detection of known prompt injection patterns, role-playing preambles, or multi-turn manipulation sequences, (4) Error rate anomalies: Increased safety filter rejections or content moderation flags, (5) Latency spikes: Performance degradation during adversarial input processing, (6) User feedback signals: Reports of harmful content generation or accessibility barriers, (7) Demographic disparity metrics: Disproportionate harmful output exposure across different user communities, (8) Hardware anomalies: RAS (Reliability, Availability, Serviceability) log irregularities, temperature/voltage sensor deviations during high-risk inference operations.	Required data sources include: (1) Content monitoring data: Full-text logs of user prompts and model outputs with policy violation annotations, (2) Safety filter telemetry: Content moderation decisions, rejection rates, and filter bypass attempts, (3) User interaction metadata: Session patterns, query sequences, geographic/demographic attributes (privacy-preserving), (4) Hardware telemetry: RAS logs, temperature/voltage sensor data, inference latency metrics, GPU/TPU utilization patterns, (5) Redundant inference outputs: Parallel model predictions for consistency validation and adversarial input detection, (6) Continuous validation metrics: Real-time safety benchmark performance, alignment scores, content policy compliance rates, (7) Accessibility metrics: Service availability by region/community, error rates across user segments, support ticket analysis, (8) Incident reports: User-submitted harmful content examples, community feedback on accessibility barriers.	Evidence sources include: (1) AI safety research literature on content policy enforcement, adversarial robustness, and alignment techniques, (2) Industry incident reports and post-mortems from AI deployments documenting harmful content generation, (3) Academic studies on AI accessibility disparities and digital divide impacts, (4) Content moderation research examining detection methods for criminal content enablement, (5) Hardware reliability engineering literature on RAS monitoring and inference integrity validation, (6) AI governance frameworks addressing equitable access and harm prevention, (7) Security research on prompt injection, jailbreaking, and adversarial attacks against language models, (8) Empirical studies on demographic disparities in AI system access and harm exposure.	Other	Post-deployment	unknown	Operational Risks	Compliance/Regulatory Risk
