ries; (9) Cross-session correlation of similar attack patterns from different accounts suggesting coordinated activity; (10) Monitoring alerts from downstream content moderation systems detecting harmful outputs that bypassed initial filters. Baseline establishment and anomaly detection thresholds should be calibrated per deployment context.	Required data collection includes: (1) Comprehensive request logs capturing full prompt text, user identifiers, timestamps, session context, and metadata (IP addresses, geographic locations, device fingerprints); (2) Content generation logs including model outputs, safety classifier scores, filter activation records, and rejection reasons; (3) Multi-turn conversation histories with semantic analysis and topic tracking; (4) Real-time monitoring metrics including request rates, latency distributions, error rates, and safety processing overhead per user/API key; (5) Content moderation data from both automated systems and human review, including flagged content, violation categories, and severity ratings; (6) User behavior analytics tracking request patterns, temporal dynamics, and cross-session correlations; (7) Security event logs capturing authentication attempts, API abuse patterns, and rate limiting triggers; (8) Incident reports from internal teams, users, and external stakeholders documenting suspected misuse; (9) Model performance metrics including safety benchmark scores, adversarial robustness testing results, and red team findings; (10) External threat intelligence on emerging jailbreaking techniques, adversarial prompt databases, and attack tool developments. Data retention policies should balance security monitoring needs with privacy requirements and regulatory compliance.	Evidence sources include: (1) AI safety research literature on adversarial attacks, jailbreaking techniques, and safety alignment failures in large language models; (2) Industry security reports and threat intelligence on AI system exploitation and content generation abuse; (3) Academic studies on content moderation, harmful content detection, and automated safety systems; (4) Regulatory guidance and legal frameworks addressing AI-generated harmful content and CSAM; (5) Red team assessments and penetration testing reports evaluating model robustness against adversarial prompting; (6) Incident databases and case studies documenting real-world AI misuse for harmful content generation; (7) Technical documentation on content filtering architectures, safety classifiers, and multi-layer defense systems; (8) Standards and best practices from organizations focused on AI safety, trust, and responsible AI development; (9) User community reports and bug bounty submissions identifying safety vulnerabilities; (10) Monitoring and analytics platforms providing behavioral baselines and anomaly detection capabilities. Evidence should be continuously updated to reflect evolving attack techniques and mitigation strategies.	Other	Post-deployment	unknown	Technical Risks	Security Risk; Data Risk
