 AI systems generating content that enables, encourages, or provides actionable guidance for violent crimes, cyber attacks, or other harmful activities through various exploitation techniques including prompt manipulation, safety bypass methods, and adversarial optimization. While present threat levels appear manageable, the risk trajectory is concerning as AI capabilities advance, access broadens, and attack techniques become more sophisticated. Organizations deploying general-purpose AI systems require robust content filtering, behavioral monitoring, incident response capabilities, and continuous safety evaluation to mitigate dual-use risks. The challenge lies in balancing legitimate use cases with preventing malicious exploitation, requiring ongoing investment in safety research, detection systems, and governance frameworks.	Attack vectors include: (1) Direct prompt manipulation - crafting inputs that bypass content filters through obfuscation, role-playing scenarios, or multi-step reasoning chains; (2) API exploitation - automated querying to extract harmful outputs while ev	Observable indicators include: (1) Anomalous query patterns - unusual request volumes, repetitive similar prompts, systematic probing of content boundaries; (2) Safety filter violations - increased rejection rates, policy violation flags, content moderation alerts; (3) Behavioral anomalies - rapid iteration on similar harmful themes, multi-turn conversations attempting to build toward harmful outputs, use of known jailbreaking patterns; (4) Output characteristics - generation of code with malicious functionality, detailed instructions for harmful activities, content matching known attack templates; (5) System performance indicators - latency spikes during complex filtering operations, elevated error rates from safety systems, resource consumption anomalies; (6) User behavior signals - account creation patterns suggesting automation, access from anonymization services, correlation with known 