157	RISK-1139	Steganographic Attacks on Generative AI Systems	Socioeconomic and Environmental	This risk concerns the potential use of steganographic techniques to embed hidden messages or data within generative AI model outputs, enabling covert communication channels that bypass content moderation and security controls. The broader context includes systemic issues of Western-centric AI development and unequal global participation in AI governance, which may create blind spots in detecting culturally-specific steganographic methods. These concerns represent approximately 2% of documented AI risks in socioeconomic assessments.	Steganographic embedding in generative AI outputs involves encoding hidden information within model-generated text, images, audio, or video content using techniques such as: (1) subtle linguistic pattern manipulation in text generation, (2) imperceptible pixel modifications in image outputs, (3) frequency domain alterations in audio, or (4) temporal encoding in video sequences. Detection challenges are amplified by Western-centric training data and evaluation frameworks that may not recognize steganographic patterns rooted in non-Western linguistic structures, cultural references, or communication norms. The unequal participation in AI development means detection systems may lack diverse cultural expertise needed to identify region-specific encoding methods. Technical detection approaches include statistical analysis of output distributions, anomaly detection in generation patterns, and multi-modal consistency checking, though these methods show reduced effectiveness across culturally diverse contexts.	Generative AI systems face dual security and equity challenges: (1) Technical vulnerability to steganographic attacks where malicious actors embed hidden communications in model outputs to bypass security controls, and (2) Systemic bias in AI development characterized by Western-centric design and unequal global participation, which creates blind 