d scale. Malicious actors can exploit these systems to produce persuasive, contextually appropriate disinformation content that appears legitimate and evades traditional detection methods. The risk is characterized by low technical barriers to entry, high scalability, multi-platform applicability, and the ability to target specific demographics with tailored messaging. This capability enables coordinated influence campaigns that can rapidly spread false narratives, manipulate public opinion, and undermine trust in information ecosystems. The threat is amplified in post-deployment scenarios where models are widely accessible through APIs or open-source releases. Mitigation requires multi-layered approaches including robust content moderation, usage monitoring, output watermarking, and coordination with platform providers to detect and respond to coordinated inauthentic behavior.	Multi-stage social engineering attack exploiting human trust in AI-generated content. Primary vector involves malicious users accessing LLM systems (via API, web interface, or local deployment) to generate propaganda content, then distributing this conten	Behavioral indicators: (1) Anomalously high user acceptance rates (>80%) for generated content compared to baseline (<60%), (2) Shortened review times (<30 seconds) suggesting reduced critical evaluation, (3) Elevated trust sentiment scores in dialog logs and user feedback, (4) Unusual content generation patterns (high volume, repetitive themes, coordinated timing). Technical indicators: (5) API usage spikes from specific accounts or IP ranges, (6) Batch generation requests with similar prompt structures, (7) Rapid iteration on prompt variations suggesting optimization attempts, (8) Generation of content in multiple languages from single accounts. Distribution indicators: (9) Coordinated posting patterns across platforms, (10) Unusual sharing velocity for generated content, (11) Bot-like distribution networks amplifying specific narratives, 