ion pathways; approval/override patterns; escalation triggers and responses; integration with external systems or databases. (4) Content analysis data: User-generated content associated with AI tool usage; query formulation patterns and language choices; output utilization tracking. (5) Behavioral analytics: Sentiment analysis on user communications; trust indicator extraction from dialog; bias detection in query patterns; anomaly detection baselines for normal usage. (6) Contextual data: Domain-specific metadata (industry sector, geographic region, regulatory environment); user account characteristics and history; network relationship mapping for coordination detection. (7) System performance data: Model confidence scores; recommendation acceptance rates; filter activation logs; override frequency. Analysis methods: Apply natural language processing for intent detection, statistical anomaly detection for usage patterns, network analysis for coordination identification, and supervised learning on labeled harmful/benign examples where available. Implement privacy-preserving techniques (differential privacy, federated learning) where collecting sensitive data. Establish baseline metrics for legitimate use cases to enable comparative analysis.	Evidence collection framework: (1) System monitoring data: Production telemetry from deployed AI systems, including interaction logs, usage analytics, and performance metrics; automated anomaly detection alerts; content moderation system logs. (2) User research: Behavioral studies on human-AI interaction patterns in high-stakes domains; trust calibration research in AI systems; studies on automation bias and over-reliance. (3) Domain expertise: Animal welfare research literature on industrial practices and illegal activities; wildlife conservation monitoring data; veterinary and agricultural technology assessments. (4) Incident reports: Documented cases of AI misuse in animal-related contexts; regulatory enforcement actions; investigative journalism on wildlife trafficking and illegal animal operations. (5) Technical audits: Red team exercises simulating harmful use cases; penetration testing of content filters and safety mechanisms; bias audits of recommendation systems. (6) Academic literature: AI ethics research on dual-use technologies; alignment research on value specification problems; sociotechnical studies on AI deployment in contested domains. (7) Industry standards: Best practices from responsible AI frameworks; sector-specific guidelines for animal welfare technology; content moderation benchmarks. (8) Regulatory guidance: Compliance frameworks for AI in agriculture and wildlife management; data protection requirements for monitoring systems; ethical review board assessments. Evidence synthesis requires triangulation across multiple sources to distinguish legitimate optimization from harmful facilitation, accounting for legal and cultural variations in animal welfare standards.	Intentional	Post-deployment	safety	Operational Risks	Compliance/Regulatory Risk
956	RISK-735	AI System Controls Insufficient for Human-Computer Interaction Safety	Human-Computer Interaction	This risk category addresses AI systems that generate content enabling, encouraging, or providing detailed guidance for violent crimes. Large language models may produce harmful outputs through several mechanisms: (1) insufficient safety training allowing direct harmful responses, (2) jailbreak techniques that bypass content filters, (3) indirect harm through seemingly benign responses that can be combined into harmful instructions, and (4) manipulation tactics that gradually escalate user requests toward violent content. The risk is amplified by anthropomorphization effects where users may perceive AI-generated violent content as more authoritative or acceptable than similar human-generated content. Research in AI safety indicates that conversational interfaces can create psychological distance from harmful actions, potentially lowering inhibition thresholds. The epistemic challenge of distinguishing AI-generated violent content from human-created content complicates content moderation and attribution of responsibility. This risk intersects with broader concerns about AI systems influencing human behavior through persuasive language generation, emotional manipulation, and the gradual normalization of harmful ideation through repeated interactions.	This risk manifests through multiple technical pathways in generative AI systems: (1) Training data contamination where violent content in pre-training corpora creates latent capabilities for generating harmful instructions, (2) Alignment failures where reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) or constitutional AI methods inadequately cover violent crime scenarios, (3) Prompt injection attacks where adversarial inputs bypass safety filters through encoding, obfuscation, or role-playing scenarios, (4) Context window exploitation where harmful requests are embedded within longer benign conversations to evade detection, and (5) Multi-turn manipulation where the model is gradually steered toward harmful outputs through seemingly innocent intermediate steps. Technical challenges include: the difficulty of defining comprehensive safety boundaries without over-restricting legitimate use cases (e.g., creative writing, security research, educational content), the computational cost of real-time content filtering at inference time, and the adversarial nature of safety measures where each mitigation spawns new bypass techniques. Model architectures with stronger reasoning capabilities may exhibit increased susceptibility to sophisticated jailbreaks that exploit logical reasoning to circumvent safety training. The risk is compounded by fine-tuning vulnerabilities where small amounts of harmful data can compromise safety alignments, and by the emergence of open-source models that lack centralized safety controls. Detection mechanisms face challenges with semantic understandingâ€”distinguishing between descriptions of violence for educational purposes versus instructional content for commission of crimes requires nuanced contextual analysis beyond simple keyword filtering.	Generative AI systems, particularly large language models, pose risks of enabling violent crimes by generating detailed instructions, encouragement, or normalization of violent acts. This occurs through alignment failures, safety bypass techniques (jailbreaks), and psychological effects including anthropomorphization and reduced inhibition in human-AI interactions. The risk is technically challenging to mitigate due to the difficulty of comprehensive safety coverage, the adversarial nature of bypass attempts, and the need to distinguish harmful content from legitimate educational or creative uses. Detection and prevention require multi-layered approaches including robust safety training, real-time content filtering, behavioral monitoring, and careful consideration of how conversational AI interfaces may influence human decision-making toward harmful actions.	Multi-stage social engineering attack combining adversarial prompt techniques with exploitation of human-AI interaction dynamics. Attack progression typically follows: (1) Initial probing to identify model safety boundaries and response patterns, (2) Grad	Behavioral indicators: (1) Anomalously high user acceptance rates of AI-generated content without verification (baseline comparison needed), (2) Shortened review times for AI outputs compared to human-generated content, (3) Dialog logs showing elevated trust language ('the AI said', 'according to the model') without critical evaluation, (4) Repeated refinement requests for increasingly specific harmful instructions, (5) Multi-turn conversations with gradual topic escalation toward violent content. Technical indicators: (1) Unusual prompt patterns including encoding schemes, role-playing frameworks, or jailbreak templates, (2) API usage patterns showing parameter manipulation or repeated requests with slight variations, (3) Fine-tuning requests with small datasets that may compromise safety, (4) High rejection rates followed by reformulated requests suggesting bypass attempts, (5) Clustering of similar harmful requests from distributed sources indicating coordinated probing. Content indicators: (1) Generated outputs containing detailed procedural knowledge about violent acts, (2) Responses that normalize or minimize the severity of violent crimes, (3) Content that provides specific targeting information or victim selection criteria, (4) Outputs that combine technical knowledge with motivational framing. Monitoring should establish baseline metrics for normal interaction patterns to enable anomaly detection. User sentiment analysis may reveal increasing emotional investment or identification with AI-generated harmful content.	Multi-source data collection strategy: (1) System logs: Complete conversation histories including user prompts and model responses, timestamp data for interaction patterns, session metadata (duration, turn count, topic progression), API call logs with parameters and rejection events, fine-tuning request logs with dataset characteristics. (2) Monitoring metrics: Content filter trigger rates and types, user acceptance/rejection rates of AI outputs, prompt similarity clustering to identify jailbreak patterns, response time distributions, user engagement metrics (session length, return frequency), A/B testing results for safety interventions. (3) Incident reports: User reports of harmful content generation, internal safety team escalations, external researcher disclosures of vulnerabilities, content moderation flags and human review decisions. (4) Security analytics: Anomaly detection on prompt patterns, behavioral clustering to identify coordinated attacks, sentiment analysis of user interactions, topic modeling to track conversation drift toward harmful content, network analysis of distributed attack patterns. (5) Safety evaluation data: Red team testing results, adversarial prompt databases, jailbreak technique catalogs, safety benchmark performance metrics. (6) Contextual data: User account characteristics (age, history, previous violations), deployment environment (API vs. consumer interface), geographic and temporal patterns. Data should be collected with appropriate privacy protections and retention policies. Establish baseline distributions for normal usage to enable statistical anomaly detection.	Evidence should be drawn from multiple research domains: (1) AI Safety Research: Academic literature on language model alignment, safety training methods (RLHF, constitutional AI), jailbreak techniques and defenses, adversarial robustness of content filters. (2) Human-Computer Interaction Studies: Research on anthropomorphization of AI systems, trust calibration in human-AI interaction, psychological effects of conversational interfaces, decision-making influenced by AI-generated content. (3) Security Research: