966	RISK-744	LLM Security Risks from Adversarial Attacks	Privacy and Security	Adversarial attacks against Large Language Models (LLMs) exploit architectural vulnerabilities, training data weaknesses, and inference-time behaviors to achieve multiple malicious objectives: extracting proprietary training data or model parameters (model extraction/stealing), manipulating outputs to produce harmful or incorrect responses (adversarial examples, prompt injection), bypassing safety guardrails (jailbreaking), or causing denial of service through resource exhaustion. These attacks range from simple prompt manipulation requiring minimal technical expertise to sophisticated gradient-based attacks requiring white-box or gray-box access to model internals.	LLM attack vectors exploit multiple vulnerability classes: (1) Training-time attacks: data poisoning to embed backdoors or biases into model weights; (2) Inference-time attacks: adversarial prompts crafted to bypass content filters, extract training data through membership inference, or trigger unintended behaviors through carefully constructed input sequences; (3) Model extraction attacks: using API queries to reconstruct model architecture, parameters, or training data through techniques like model inversion or knowledge distillation; (4) Resource exhaustion attacks: crafting inputs that trigger excessive computation or memory usage. Attack complexity varies significantly - prompt injection may require only natural language manipulation skills, while gradient-based adversarial attacks require understanding of model architecture, loss functions, and optimization techniques. White-box attacks assume full model access, gray-box attacks leverage partial knowledge (architecture but not weights), and black-box attacks operate through API interactions only.	Large Language Models face a diverse threat landscape including adversarial manipulation of outputs, extraction of proprietary model information or training data, circumvention of safety controls, and service disruption. Attack sophistication ranges from simple prompt engineering accessible to non-technical users to complex gradient-based methods requiring machine learning expertise and computational resources. Primary business impacts include intellectual property theft (model parameters, training data), reputational damage from harmful outputs, regulatory compliance violations, and service availability issues. Risk severity depends on model deployment context, access controls, and sensitivity of training data. Mitigation requires layered defenses including input validation, output filtering, rate limiting, adversarial training, and continuous monitoring.	Attack vectors include: (1) API-based attacks through public or authenticated endpoints using crafted prompts, queries designed to extract information, or inputs triggering resource exhaustion; (2) Direct model access attacks when adversaries obtain model	Technical indicators: (1) Anomalous query patterns - unusual token sequences, repetitive probing queries, systematic parameter variation suggesting automated attacks; (2) Performance anomalies - latency spikes exceeding baseline by >2-3 standard deviations, memory usage increases, GPU utilization patterns inconsistent with normal workloads; (3) Output anomalies - increased safety filter triggers, content policy violations, outputs exhibiting adversarial characteristics (unnatural phrasing, embedded instructions); (4) Access pattern anomalies - high-frequency API calls from single sources, queries targeting model introspection or system prompts, attempts to extract training data through completion attacks; (5) Error rate changes - increased authentication failures, input validation rejections, or timeout errors. Behavioral indicators include user accounts exhibiting systematic exploration of model boundaries, queries designed to elicit information about model architecture or training, and coordinated attack patterns across multiple accounts or IP addresses.	Essential data collection: (1) Complete request/response logs including prompts, completions, timestamps, user identifiers, API endpoints, and response metadata (latency, token counts, safety filter activations); (2) System telemetry: CPU/GPU utilization, memory consumption, request queue depths, error rates by type, authentication events; (3) Security-specific logs: input validation failures, content policy violations, rate limit triggers, anomalous access patterns; (4) User behavior analytics: query frequency distributions, topic clustering, prompt engineering patterns, account activity timelines; (5) Model performance metrics: output quality scores, coherence measures, safety alignment indicators. Data should be retained with sufficient granularity for forensic analysis while respecting privacy requirements. Implement real-time streaming analytics for immediate threat detection and batch processing for pattern identification. Apply anomaly detection algorithms, statistical process control, and machine learning-based classification to identify attack signatures. Correlation across multiple data sources enhances detection accuracy.	Evidence for LLM attack risks derives from multiple research domains: (1) Academic AI security research documenting adversarial attack methodologies, defense mechanisms, and empirical attack success rates; (2) Industry security reports from AI providers, cloud platforms, and cybersecurity vendors describing real-world attack incidents and threat intelligence; (3) Vulnerability disclosure databases and security advisories detailing specific LLM vulnerabilities and exploitation techniques; (4) AI safety research examining alignment failures, reward hacking, and unintended model behaviors that may be exploited; (5) Penetration testing and red team exercises conducted by security researchers and organizations; (6) Incident response case studies from organizations experiencing LLM-related security events. Evidence quality varies - controlled research environments may not reflect production attack complexity, while real-world incident data often lacks public disclosure due to confidentiality concerns.	Intentional	Other	technical	Technical Risks	Security Risk; Data Risk
