l data. The risk manifests post-deployment and typically results from unintentional system behavior rather than malicious design. Mitigation requires implementing output filtering, differential privacy during training, regular privacy audits, and robust data governance practices. Regulatory frameworks including GDPR, CCPA, and emerging AI-specific regulations impose significant liability for privacy breaches.	Privacy leakage can occur through multiple vectors: (1) Passive leakage via normal model operation where memorized training data surfaces in generated outputs without adversarial prompting; (2) Active extraction through adversarial prompting techniques de	Technical indicators: (1) Data Loss Prevention (DLP) systems flagging outbound API responses containing PII patterns (SSNs, credit cards, email addresses, phone numbers); (2) Automated content scanning detecting high similarity scores (>80%) between generated outputs and known sensitive documents or copyrighted material; (3) Anomalous egress traffic patterns showing unusual volume, timing, or destination of model outputs; (4) Repeated queries with extraction-focused prompt patterns (e.g., 'repeat after me', 'what was in your training data'); (5) User reports of seeing others' personal information in generated content; (6) Privacy audit tools detecting PII in model outputs during testing; (7) Membership inference attack success rates exceeding baseline thresholds during security assessments; (8) Logs showing identical or near-identical outputs across different user sessions suggesting memorization.	Essential data sources for identifying privacy leakage risks: (1) API request/response logs capturing full prompt-completion pairs with timestamps and user identifiers; (2) DLP system alerts and classifications of flagged content; (3) Model output samples from production traffic for automated PII scanning; (4) Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) logs showing access patterns and anomalies; (5) User feedback and incident reports regarding privacy concerns; (6) Privacy audit results from red-teaming exercises and membership inference tests; (7) Training data manifests and data lineage documentation to trace potential leakage sources; (8) Model versioning and deployment logs to correlate incidents with specific model versions; (9) Network traffic analysis showing data egress volumes and destinations; (10) Compliance monitoring data from regulatory scanning tools. Data retention should follow privacy regulations while maintaining sufficient history for pattern detection (typically 90-365 days depending on jurisdiction).	Evidence for privacy leakage risks derives from multiple research and operational domains: (1) Academic AI safety research on training data memorization and extraction attacks in large language models; (2) Privacy-preserving machine learning literature covering differential privacy, federated learning, and secure multi-party computation; (3) Industry security research on prompt injection, jailbreaking, and adversarial attacks against AI systems; (4) Regulatory guidance and compliance frameworks (GDPR Article 25 on privacy by design, NIST AI Risk Management Framework, ISO/IEC 27701 for privacy information management); (5) Incident reports and case studies from organizations experiencing AI privacy breaches; (6) Red team assessments and penetration testing reports from AI security evaluations; (7) Privacy audit methodologies and tools for AI systems; (8) Data protection impact assessments (DPIAs) for AI deployments in regulated industries. Evidence strength is strong given documented incidents, reproducible attack techniques, and established regulatory requirements.	Unintentional	Post-deployment	data	Technical Risks	Security Risk; Privacy Risk
