identifying high-confidence matches between model outputs and protected training data; (5) Access pattern anomalies including unusual geographic origins, off-hours access spikes, or single users making excessive API calls; (6) Content filter bypass attempts detected through prompt injection signatures or adversarial input patterns; (7) Model output monitoring detecting verbatim reproduction of training examples or PII disclosure in responses; (8) Embedding space analysis revealing clustering patterns consistent with membership inference attacks. Threshold examples: DLP alerts on >5 PII elements per response, query rates exceeding 100 requests/minute from single source, similarity scores >0.95 to training data, egress traffic >10x baseline.	Comprehensive risk identification requires multi-layered data collection: (1) Model inference logs capturing all input prompts, output responses, timestamps, user identifiers, and session metadata to enable pattern analysis and anomaly detection; (2) API access logs including authentication events, rate limiting triggers, geographic origins, and user agent strings; (3) DLP system alerts and content filtering logs showing PII detection events, similarity matches, and policy violations; (4) Network traffic data monitoring egress patterns, payload sizes, destination addresses, and protocol usage from model serving infrastructure; (5) Model behavior metrics including output entropy, confidence scores, response lengths, and generation patterns that may indicate extraction attempts; (6) Training data provenance records documenting data sources, PII presence, anonymization procedures, and data retention policies; (7) Hardware telemetry (RAS logs, temperature, voltage sensors) detecting infrastructure compromise or unusual computational patterns; (8) Continuous validation metrics comparing model outputs against known training examples to detect memorization; (9) User behavior analytics tracking query patterns, session durations, and inter