ization, feature attribution), statistical testing for distribution shift, and longitudinal analysis of behavioral drift. Data retention must balance comprehensive monitoring with privacy considerations and storage constraints.	Evidence for AI misalignment risks draws from multiple research domains: (1) AI safety and alignment research - theoretical frameworks for value learning, reward modeling, and corrigibility; (2) Machine learning empirical studies - documented cases of reward hacking, goal misgeneralization, and emergent capabilities in deployed systems; (3) Adversarial robustness research - demonstrations of out-of-distribution failures and specification gaming; (4) AI governance and policy analysis - regulatory frameworks addressing autonomous system oversight; (5) Interpretability and explainability research - methods for understanding model decision-making and detecting misalignment; (6) Multi-agent systems research - studies of emergent behaviors in interacting AI systems; (7) Historical case studies - documented failures in automated systems across domains (finance, healthcare, transportation); (8) Industry incident reports - real-world examples of AI systems behaving unexpectedly or requiring emergency intervention. Monitoring should incorporate: academic literature on AI safety, industry best practices for model governance, regulatory guidance on autonomous systems, incident databases tracking AI failures, and continuous evaluation of deployed system behaviors.	Intentional	Other	unknown	Technical Risks	Security Risk; Data Risk
