2169	RISK-861	A1008_Gipiškis2024	Frequent benchmarking to identify when red teaming is needed	Testing & Auditing	Benchmarks, once created, are inexpensive to apply but may be less informative than red teaming. One reason is that sensitive data (e.g., relating to CBRN- related capabilities) cannot be included in the public questions and answers of benchmarks. On the other hand, red teaming can be more accurate given par- ticipants with diverse attack strategies, but it requires more resources to execute than benchmarking. If there is a correlation between benchmarking and red- teaming scores, then employing frequent benchmarking during the development of the model can identify arising vulnerabilities and inform the developers when more thorough red teaming is required [21].Benchmarks can act as early warning signs of a larger issue, and red teaming can then be employed to investigate the severity and extent of such an issue.
